// Generated by tools/build-help.mjs from the website's docs-content.js. Do not edit: edit the site's articles and run the builder again.
window.MOHelpData = {"site":"https://lukagrcar.com/","docs":"https://lukagrcar.com/apps/meshoptimiser/docs.html","groups":[{"id":"start","title":"Start here","blurb":"What the app is, what it needs, and how to get from a download to your first model on screen."},{"id":"install","title":"Install and set up","blurb":"Every step from the download to a computer that is ready to work: Windows and macOS, the first session, the optional extras, shortcuts, moving or removing the app, and a fix for each message the launcher can print."},{"id":"cad","title":"Getting your model out of your CAD program","blurb":"MeshOptimiser opens STEP files. How to save one from Inventor, Onshape, Creo and Rhino, with the options that matter."},{"id":"around","title":"Find your way around","blurb":"The window, the mouse, selecting, hiding, the parts tree, search, undo, tabs and settings."},{"id":"lighter","title":"Make it lighter","blurb":"The tools that remove triangles and parts, one at a time, and a sensible order to use them in."},{"id":"look","title":"Look and arrange","blurb":"View modes, materials, moving and measuring, editing the points, edges and polygons of a part, standing a model on the floor, and adding parts."},{"id":"claude","title":"Work with Claude","blurb":"Connect the app to Claude, what Claude can do in it, and what stays in your hands."},{"id":"save","title":"Save and export","blurb":"Keep your work as a scene, or write a file for another program."},{"id":"jobs","title":"Common jobs, start to finish","blurb":"Three jobs people come here to do: convert a STEP file to GLB, take the bolts out of an assembly, and make a model safe to send to a customer."},{"id":"reference","title":"Reference","blurb":"Every shortcut, every command-line option, the file formats and what is in the folder."},{"id":"help","title":"Help","blurb":"What to do when something goes wrong, and answers to common questions."}],"articles":[{"slug":"what-it-is","group":"start","title":"What MeshOptimiser is","summary":"A free app that runs on your own computer. It turns a heavy CAD file into a light 3D file and lets you inspect, clean up and export it.","blocks":[["p","MeshOptimiser is a free, open-source app that runs on your own computer. You give it a heavy 3D file from an engineering program. It gives you back a much lighter file that a web page or another 3D program can load quickly, and a viewer to look at the model, clean it up and export it."],["p","You do not need to know anything about 3D to start. These pages explain each word the first time it is used, and [Words you will meet](#glossary) lists them all in one place."],["h","The problem it solves"],["p","Engineers design machines in CAD programs (CAD stands for computer-aided design). They usually hand the design over as a STEP file, a common exchange format for CAD. A STEP file describes every part exactly, down to the last screw, so it is often very large and slow to show."],["p","A real assembly (a model made of many parts) can hold 400 identical bolts and 80 identical brackets. MeshOptimiser notices the parts that are the same and stores each shape once, with a list of the places where it sits. It also turns the exact CAD surfaces into triangles, which is what screens draw, and can leave out parts that are too small to see."],["terms",[["400 bolts × 50 KB","Become 1 shape of 50 KB and 400 positions."],["80 brackets × 12 KB","Become 1 shape of 12 KB and 80 positions."],["A 320 MB STEP file","Becomes an 11 MB compressed GLB. This is the example from the app’s own README, not a promise for every file."]]],["h","The two halves"],["list",["The converter. A small Python program, `step2glb.py`, reads a STEP file and writes a GLB file. GLB is a compact 3D format that browsers and most 3D programs can open.","The viewer. A page that opens in a window of its own and shows the model. Here you look around, select parts, remove what you do not need, reduce triangles, recolour, and export."]],["p","You do not have to run the two halves yourself. One launcher starts everything, and you drop a file on the window."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-viewer.webp","A gearbox assembly in 95 parts and 646,064 triangles: the parts tree on the left, the scene’s totals on the right."],["h","What you can do with it"],["list",["Open STEP, GLB, glTF, FBX, OBJ, 3MF and STL files.","See how heavy a model is, and which parts make it heavy.","Delete parts nobody will see, close holes, simplify parts and reduce triangles.","Rename, group and tidy the list of parts.","Recolour materials, move parts, measure distances and add standard parts from a library.","Save your work, and export to GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ, STL, PLY, or a parts list."]],["h","What it is not"],["list",["It is not a CAD program. It reads STEP files but does not write them, and it does not edit exact CAD surfaces.","It is not an online service. Nothing is uploaded to the internet. See [Privacy and offline use](#privacy-offline).","It is not finished. The version is below 1.0, and things may change between versions."]],["note","MeshOptimiser runs on Windows and macOS, is free, and is published under the MIT licence."],["see",["glossary","requirements","first-start","cad-files-step-first"]]]},{"slug":"glossary","group":"start","title":"Words you will meet","summary":"A plain-language glossary: STEP, mesh, triangle, GLB, instancing, decimation, WebGPU, virtual environment and the other terms these pages use.","blocks":[["p","You do not need to learn these before you start. Come back to this page when a word is new."],["h","Files and formats"],["terms",[["CAD","Computer-aided design. The programs engineers use to design real objects, such as SolidWorks or NX."],["STEP (.step, .stp)","A file format for exchanging CAD designs between programs. It stores exact surfaces, part names, colours and how the parts fit together."],["GLB","A compact 3D file made of triangles, with colours and part names inside one file. Browsers and most 3D programs can open it. This is the format MeshOptimiser writes by default."],["glTF","The same format as GLB, written as readable text. The app’s own format sheet describes it as the same capabilities, larger."],["FBX, OBJ, STL, PLY, USDZ, 3MF","Other 3D file formats. [File formats](#file-formats) says what each one is for."],["XCAF","The part of a STEP file that holds names, colours and the assembly tree. You see the word in the import settings."]]],["h","What a model is made of"],["terms",[["Part","One piece of the model: a bolt, a cover, a bracket. Each part has a row in the parts tree."],["Assembly","A model made of many parts, often arranged in groups inside groups."],["Group","A folder in the parts tree. It holds parts or other groups."],["Mesh","The surface of a part, built from many small flat triangles."],["Triangle (tri)","The smallest piece of a mesh. The app counts everything in triangles: more triangles mean a heavier model."],["Polygon (n-gon)","A flat face with any number of corners. Several triangles that lie in one plane can be joined into one polygon. [Remesh](#remesh) does that, and turns polygons back into triangles. In [Edit mesh](#edit-mesh), two triangles that make a four-sided shape count as one polygon."],["Vertex","A corner point of a triangle. The plural is vertices. In [Edit mesh](#edit-mesh) it is called a point."],["Edge","The line between two points where two polygons meet, or where a surface ends."],["Loop","A row of polygons or edges that runs round a part, like a ring round a tube. [Edit mesh](#edit-mesh) picks one with a double-click."],["Normal","A hidden arrow on the surface that tells the light which way the surface faces. Wrong normals make a part look oddly shaded."],["Bounding box (bbox)","The smallest box that a part fits inside. The app uses it to measure how big a part is."],["Origin and pivot","The origin is the point 0,0,0 of the scene. A pivot is the point a part turns around."],["Material","What a surface looks like: its colour and how shiny or metallic it is. Many parts can share one material."]]],["h","What the app does to a model"],["terms",[["Tessellation","Turning the exact curved surfaces of a CAD file into triangles. Finer tessellation means smoother curves and more triangles."],["Instancing","Storing one mesh for a shape that repeats, plus the list of places where it sits. 400 identical bolts become one bolt and 400 positions."],["Decimation","Removing triangles from a mesh while keeping its overall shape. It is lossy: detail that is removed does not come back, except through undo."],["Lossy","An action that throws detail away to save size."],["Compression","Packing the same data into a smaller file. Meshopt and Draco are two methods for 3D files."],["Meshopt","A compression method for GLB files. The converter applies it through a separate tool called gltfpack, when that tool is installed."],["Draco","Another compression method for GLB files. It is a switch in the Export window."],["Scene","Everything in one tab of the app: the parts, their materials, the camera and the undo history."],["BOM","Bill of materials: a parts list. The CSV export writes one."]]],["h","The screen"],["terms",[["Viewport","The large 3D view in the middle of the window."],["Parts tree","The list of parts and groups in the left sidebar."],["Gizmo","The coloured arrows, rings or handles that appear on a selected part, for moving, rotating or scaling it. In Edit mesh it moves the picked points, edges or polygons instead."],["Orbit, pan, zoom","Turning the view around the model, sliding it sideways, and moving closer or further away."],["Isolate","Showing only the selected parts and hiding everything else for a moment."],["Edit mesh","A mode in which you pick and change the points, edges and polygons of a part instead of whole parts. See [Edit mesh](#edit-mesh)."],["Bake geo","A button in {{Properties}} that turns a library shape into an ordinary mesh. The shape’s numbers go, and the mesh tools work on it. See [The parts library](#library)."],["Align and spread","Lining parts up on one side or the middle along an axis, or spacing them evenly. See [Align parts](#align-parts)."],["Dissolve, weld","Two Edit mesh tools. Dissolve takes pieces out and closes the surface over them. Weld makes several points one."],["Shortcut profile","A named set of keys that you changed. The app’s own keys are the Default profile, which cannot be changed. See [Keyboard shortcuts](#shortcuts)."],["Draw call","One instruction to the graphics card to draw something. Fewer draw calls usually mean a smoother view. The optimisation report counts them."]]],["h","The technical side"],["terms",[["Python","A programming language. The converter and the small local server are written in it, so Python has to be installed."],["Virtual environment (.venv)","A private folder of Python add-ons that belongs to this app only. The launcher creates it inside the app folder the first time, so nothing else on your computer is changed."],["Terminal","A window where you type commands. On Windows it is called Command Prompt or PowerShell, on macOS it is called Terminal."],["Local server","A small program on your own computer that hands the app’s pages to your browser. Its address is normally `http://localhost:4242`. Localhost means this computer."],["PATH","The list of folders where your computer looks for programs. Python has to be on it for the launcher to find it."],["WebGPU","The modern way for a browser to use the graphics card. The viewer uses it when the browser has it."],["WebGL2","An older way to do the same. The viewer uses it when WebGPU is not available."],["Installing the app (PWA)","Chrome and Edge can add a web page to your desktop and Start menu as if it were a program. A page that supports this is called a progressive web app, or PWA."]]],["see",["what-it-is","file-formats","read-the-numbers"]]]},{"slug":"requirements","group":"start","title":"What you need","summary":"Windows or macOS, Python 3.10 to 3.12, a recent browser and about 2 GB of free disk space. How to check and install Python.","blocks":[["terms",[["Computer","Windows or macOS."],["Python","Version 3.10, 3.11 or 3.12. Version 3.13 is not supported yet, because one of the add-ons the converter needs (cadquery-ocp) is not available for it."],["Browser","One with WebGPU: a recent Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari. For the app to open in a window of its own, the launcher looks for Chrome, Edge or Brave."],["Disk space","About 2 GB free for the Python environment that is created on the first run."],["Internet","Needed once, on the first run, to download the Python add-ons. After that the app runs without a connection."]]],["h","Check whether you have Python"],["steps",["Open a terminal. On Windows, press the Windows key, type `cmd` and press [[Enter]]. On macOS, open the app called Terminal.","Type the command below and press [[Enter]]. On macOS, type `python3` instead of `python`.","Read the answer. `Python 3.12.7` means version 3.12 is installed, which is fine. A message that Python was not found means you need to install it."]],["code","python --version"],["note","You can skip this check. The launcher looks for Python itself and prints the version it found."],["h","Install Python on Windows"],["p","There are two ways. The first is the simplest."],["steps",["Start the app with `start.bat` (see [Start it the first time](#first-start)).","If no Python is found, the window says so and asks whether to install Python 3.12 automatically. Press [[Y]].","Wait while it installs. The launcher then carries on by itself."]],["p","Or install it yourself:"],["steps",["Go to `https://www.python.org/downloads/` and download Python 3.12.","Run the installer. On its first screen, tick “Add Python to PATH”. This is the step people miss.","Finish the installer, then close any terminal windows that were open, so they pick up the change."]],["h","Install Python on macOS"],["p","The macOS launcher uses the command `python3`. If it is missing and you have Homebrew (a tool for installing software on a Mac), the launcher installs Python through Homebrew. Without Homebrew, download Python from `https://www.python.org/downloads/` and run the launcher again. Make sure the version is 3.10, 3.11 or 3.12."],["h","Optional: gltfpack"],["p","The converter can shrink its GLB files further with Meshopt compression. It does this through a separate free tool called gltfpack, and only when gltfpack is installed and on your PATH. The app works without it. The files are simply larger."],["see",["download","first-start","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"download","group":"start","title":"Download and unzip","summary":"Get the zip, unpack it into a normal folder, and learn why there is no installer.","blocks":[["p","MeshOptimiser comes as one zip file. There is no installer. Everything, including its Python environment, lives in the folder you unpack."],["steps",["Open the Download page of this site and press the download button. Your browser saves a zip file, usually in your Downloads folder.","Unpack the zip. On Windows, right-click it and choose the option to extract everything. On macOS, double-click it.","You now have a folder, named something like `MeshOptimiser-main`. Move it wherever you like, for example into your Documents folder.","Open the folder. You should see files called `start.bat`, `start.command`, `serve.py` and `index.html`, among others."]],["note","Unpack the zip before you start anything. Windows lets you look inside a zip without unpacking it, and the launcher does not work from there."],["h","Where to put the folder"],["list",["Somewhere you are allowed to write files. The app creates two folders inside its own: `.venv` for its Python environment and `inbox` for converted files.","On a drive with about 2 GB free."]],["p","To remove the app later, delete the folder. Nothing is installed anywhere else, apart from Python itself if you installed it for this."],["see",["first-start","files-in-folder","stop-restart-update"]]]},{"slug":"first-start","group":"start","title":"Start it the first time","summary":"Double-click the launcher for Windows or macOS. What the first run sets up, how long it takes, and what you see when it is ready.","blocks":[["p","The first start takes about three minutes, because the launcher downloads and sets up what the converter needs. Every start after that takes a second or two."],["h","On Windows"],["steps",["Open the app folder and double-click `start.bat`.","A black window with white text opens. This is the launcher. Leave it open.","It works through four numbered steps. `[1/4] Checking Python...` looks for Python and prints the version it found.","`[2/4] Checking local Python environment...` creates the folder `.venv` (about 30 seconds) and installs the add-ons (one to three minutes). You need an internet connection for this step.","`[3/4] Starting the local server...` starts the small server that the app runs on, and prints its address, normally `http://localhost:4242`.","The app opens in a window of its own and shows the start screen."]],["note","If the launcher finds no Python, it offers to install Python 3.12 for you. Press [[Y]] to accept. See [What you need](#requirements)."],["h","On macOS"],["steps",["Open the app folder and double-click `start.command`.","If macOS refuses with “Operation not permitted”, right-click `start.command` and choose “Open”. macOS blocks a double-click on a freshly unzipped script the first time.","A Terminal window opens. It creates the folder `.venv`, installs the add-ons, and prints `Starting server...`.","The app opens and shows the start screen."]],["h","What you see"],["p","The start screen has links to Help, the keyboard shortcuts and Settings at the top, and a row of tiles: {{Open file…}} (or drop a file anywhere on the screen), {{New scene}} to start with nothing and {{Import…}} to add a file to the scene that is already open. Under them are four shapes to start from (Cube, Sphere, Cylinder and Torus) and the list of recent files, with a search box above it and a {{Clear all}} button. The list is empty the first time."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-start.webp","The start screen: open or drop a file, reopen a recent one, or start from a shape."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["The launcher window is the server. If you close it, the app stops working. Keep it open while you work and see [Stop, restart and update](#stop-restart-update) for the tidy way to quit.","The app opens in a window with no address bar and no browser tabs when Chrome, Edge or Brave is installed. Without one of those, it opens as a tab in your usual browser. Both work the same.","The address is normally `http://localhost:4242`. If something else on your computer already uses that number, the launcher tries another one and prints the address it chose.","It uses your usual browser profile, so your settings and recent files are remembered between starts."]],["h","Start with a file"],["p","On Windows you can drag a `.step` or `.glb` file onto `start.bat` instead of double-clicking it. The app starts and opens that file. For a STEP file the launcher window first asks a few questions, such as the tessellation quality. Press [[Enter]] at each question to accept the suggested answer."],["see",["first-file","install-as-app","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"install-as-app","group":"start","title":"Install it as an app window","summary":"Since v0.13.0 the app opens in its own window. You can also install it for a desktop shortcut, and fold its title bar away.","blocks":[["p","From v0.13.0 the launcher already opens MeshOptimiser in a window of its own: a title bar and the app, with no address bar, no browser tabs and no extension buttons. It does this when Chrome, Edge or Brave is on your computer, and you do not have to install anything for it."],["p","Installing is an extra. It gives the app its own icon, a shortcut on the desktop and in the Start menu, and a window to itself."],["h","Install it"],["steps",["Start the app as usual, so the local server is running.","Open the app in an ordinary tab of Chrome or Edge. Type the address the launcher printed, normally `http://localhost:4242`, into the browser.","The browser offers to install the page as an app (“Install”). Accept it.","The app now has a shortcut and opens in its own window."]],["note","Installing does not replace the launcher. The installed app is a window onto the same local server, so start the server first with `start.bat` or `start.command`. Without it the window has nothing to show."],["h","Fold the title bar away"],["p","In the installed app, the title bar has a small `^` button. Press it and the title bar disappears. The window’s own buttons (minimise, maximise, close) float over the top right corner, and the app’s top bar becomes the title bar."],["list",["Drag an empty stretch of the top bar to move the window.","Buttons, tabs and fields in the top bar still work as before.","Press `^` again to bring the title bar back."]],["h","An ordinary browser tab instead"],["p","If you prefer the app in a normal tab, start the server from a terminal in the app folder with the option `--tab`. [The command-line converter](#command-line) explains how to open a terminal in the folder."],["code","python serve.py --tab"],["h","Start without the black window (Windows)"],["p","The file `start_hidden.vbs` starts the app the same way as `start.bat`, without showing the launcher window. Use `start.bat` when you want to see what the launcher is doing, for example when something does not start."],["see",["first-start","stop-restart-update","the-window"]]]},{"slug":"first-file","group":"start","title":"Open your first file","summary":"Drop a file on the window. What the import settings mean, what conversion does, how to follow its progress, and where the result is kept.","blocks":[["p","The app opens STEP (`.step`, `.stp`), GLB, glTF, FBX, OBJ, 3MF and STL files. A STEP file is converted first. The other formats open directly."],["h","Open a file"],["steps",["Drag the file from your file manager onto the app window. You can drop it on the start screen or on the 3D view. While you hold it over the view, the words {{Drop to open}} appear.","Or press [[Ctrl + O]] and pick the file. On the start screen the same button is called {{Open file…}}.","For a STEP file, a window called {{Import settings}} appears. The first time, leave everything as it is and press {{Import}}.","A progress card shows what is happening, with a running clock and a log of messages. A large assembly can take several minutes.","When it is done, the model appears in the 3D view. The parts tree on the left fills with its parts, and {{Properties}} on the right shows its totals."]],["note","Your original file is never changed. The app works on its own copy, and nothing is written to a file until you save or export."],["h","What conversion does"],["p","A STEP file holds exact surfaces. The converter, running in Python on your computer, turns it into a GLB file made of triangles. Along the way it:"],["list",["reads the names and colours of the parts and the tree they are arranged in;","tessellates the surfaces, which means it covers them with triangles;","finds parts that have the same shape and stores that shape once (instancing);","leaves out parts below a size you choose, if you ask for it;","compresses the result with Meshopt, if the tool gltfpack is installed."]],["h","The import settings for a STEP file"],["terms",[["Surface quality","How closely the triangles follow curved surfaces: {{Coarse}}, {{Standard}}, {{Fine}} or {{Ultra}}. Coarse is the fastest and shows facets on curves. Ultra is slow and very smooth. Standard is the recommended one. {{Custom}} means your own numbers, under Tessellation."],["Tessellation","The numbers behind that choice. Click the row to open it. It is explained below."],["Colours and structure","{{Colours & hierarchy}} reads colours, names and the assembly tree. {{Geometry only}} is the fastest and gives you neither colours nor a tree. It suits huge files where you only need the shape."],["What to read","Five switches for extra information in the file: {{Instance overrides}}, {{Layers}}, {{Materials}}, {{Product names}} and {{Validation properties}}. Each one adds a pass over the file. Instance overrides is the slowest on assemblies with many repeated parts."],["Drop small parts","Leaves out parts smaller than this share of the model during conversion. 0 keeps everything."],["Share identical shapes","Stores a repeated shape once. Leave it on."],["Swap Y and Z","Stands a model up that comes in lying on its side."],["Convert again","Ignores a stored conversion of the same file, if there is one, and converts from scratch."],["Add to the open scene","Adds the file to the scene that is open, instead of opening it as a scene of its own."],["Fit the view","Moves the camera so the whole model is in view when it has loaded."],["Don't ask again for this format","Skips this window next time and uses the same answers. Leave it unticked until you know which settings you want."]]],["p","IGES and BREP files, two other CAD formats, get the same window. FBX, OBJ, 3MF and STL files get a shorter version of it, with only the last three choices. GLB and glTF files open without it."],["h","Tessellation: how the surfaces become triangles"],["p","Tessellation is the step that covers the exact CAD surfaces with triangles. You do not have to open this block: the four choices of {{Surface quality}} fill it in for you. Open it when curves come in too faceted, or the model comes in too heavy."],["terms",[["Sag","How far a triangle may stand off the true surface, in millimetres. Smaller is smoother, and makes more triangles."],["Angle","The most a curve may turn from one triangle to the next, in degrees. Smaller means rounder holes and rounder fillets (a fillet is a rounded edge)."],["Min edge","The smallest triangle edge allowed, in millimetres. It keeps tiny fillets and threads from filling up with thin slivers. 0 is no limit."],["The four choices and Custom","Each choice sets the sag and the angle: Coarse is 1 mm and 30°, Standard 0.5 mm and 30°, Fine 0.2 mm and 20°, Ultra 0.05 mm and 12°. Type a number of your own and {{Custom}} is selected."],["By part size","Fine on the large parts, coarse on the small ones. Small, medium and large parts each get a sag and an angle of their own, so the housing comes in fine and the screws coarse. Two fields, {{Small under}} and {{large from}}, say where the limits are, as a share of the model’s size: 5% and 30% to begin with. While it is on, the four choices are greyed out. {{Min edge}} still applies."],["Smooth shading","Takes the normals, the directions the light is worked out from, from the real CAD surfaces. A fillet then runs into the faces beside it without a visible seam, and sharp edges stay sharp. On by default."]]],["note","A coarser setting here makes fewer triangles at the source. That usually looks better than reducing a fine mesh afterwards."],["h","Follow the progress, or stop it"],["list",["The progress card shows the latest message from the converter. {{Copy log}} copies all messages, which is useful when you report a problem.","{{Cancel}} stops the conversion.","If the conversion fails, the card says so. Press {{Copy log}} to keep the messages. See [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting)."]],["h","Where the converted file goes"],["p","The converter writes the GLB into the folder `inbox`, inside the app folder. The copy of your STEP file that was made for the conversion is removed when it finishes. To keep a GLB somewhere you choose, with a name you choose, use [Save a scene](#save-scene) or [Export](#export)."],["h","More than one file"],["list",["Opening a second file does not replace the first. It opens in a new tab. See [Scene tabs](#scene-tabs).","To put two files into one scene, use {{Menu}}, then {{Import into scene…}}, or press [[Ctrl + Shift + O]].","To throw away every edit and load the file again as it was, use {{Menu}}, then {{Revert to source…}}."]],["see",["the-window","move-the-view","read-the-numbers","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"stop-restart-update","group":"start","title":"Stop, restart and update","summary":"Quit the app cleanly, start it again in a second or two, and move to a newer version without losing your settings.","blocks":[["h","Stop the app"],["steps",["Click {{Menu}} in the top bar and choose {{Quit app}}.","If the scene has parts in it, the app asks whether to save first. Choose to save, to quit without saving, or to cancel.","The page says that MeshOptimiser is closed and the server has stopped. On Windows the launcher window closes by itself.","Close the app window if it is still open."]],["p","Closing only the app window does not stop the server. The launcher window stays open and the server keeps running. You can also stop it there: click the launcher window and press [[Ctrl + C]], or close that window."],["h","Start it again"],["p","Double-click `start.bat` or `start.command` again. The launcher finds the `.venv` folder from the first run and reuses it, so the app is ready in a second or two."],["p","With the start screen’s resume option on, the file you had open last is offered at the top of the start screen. See [Settings](#settings)."],["h","Update to a new version"],["p","From version 0.22.0 the app can update itself, and only itself: a few megabytes of the app’s own files, not the Python environment."],["steps",["Open {{Settings}} and choose {{Updates}}. The app also checks by itself when it opens, if enough time has passed since the last check (you choose how often), and a small card in the corner says when a newer version is out.","Read what is new, then press {{Update now}}. Press {{Later}} or {{Skip this version}} on the card if you would rather not.","The app downloads the new files, checks them against a fingerprint, replaces only the files that changed and restarts. Save your scene first: the restart closes the window."]],["p","Your scenes, your settings, the inbox and exports folders and the Python environment are never touched. One earlier version is kept, so {{Earlier version}} in the same section puts it back with one press. If the new version never gets a working window up in three starts, the app puts the old one back by itself."],["p","A check downloads one small file from lukagrcar.com that lists the newest version. Nothing about you, your computer or your models is sent. The update itself comes from the releases on GitHub. In Settings, Updates you can switch the automatic check off, choose Stable or Beta, and turn off the notice in the corner. If a release changes the launcher files (start.bat, start.command), the app cannot replace them while they run, and it tells you to download the full zip instead."],["h","Updating a version before 0.22.0"],["p","Those versions have no updater, so the first move is by hand. A new version is a new zip. This is a suggested way to move over, based on how the app stores things:"],["steps",["Quit the app.","Download the new zip and unpack it into a new folder, next to the old one.","If you want to keep converted files, copy them out of the old folder’s `inbox` folder.","Start the new version with its launcher. The first start sets up its Python environment again and takes about three minutes.","When you are happy with the new version, delete the old folder."]],["note","Your settings and the recent files list are kept by your browser, not in the app folder, so they are still there after an update."],["see",["first-start","files-in-folder","save-scene","settings"]]]},{"slug":"install-windows","group":"install","title":"Install on Windows, step by step","summary":"From the zip to a working app on Windows 10 or 11: what to check first, what each line in the launcher window means, and how to know it worked.","blocks":[["p","This page walks through the whole install on Windows, one step at a time, and says what you should see at each step. It takes about ten minutes, most of it the one-time setup on the first start. There is nothing to run as administrator and nothing to uninstall later: the app lives in one folder."],["h","Before you start"],["list",["Windows 10 or 11. The launcher can install Python for you through `winget`, which comes with current versions of Windows, and falls back to a direct download if `winget` is missing.","About 2 GB of free disk space on the drive where you put the folder.","An internet connection for the first start. After that the app runs offline.","Chrome, Edge or Brave, if you want the app in a window of its own. Without one of them it opens in a tab of your usual browser, and works the same."]],["p","Python 3.10, 3.11 or 3.12 is needed as well. You do not have to check for it first: the launcher looks, and offers to install it if it is missing."],["h","Step 1: download and unpack"],["steps",["Open the Download page of this site and press the download button. Your browser saves a zip file, usually into your Downloads folder.","Right-click the zip and choose {{Extract All…}}, then {{Extract}}. Do not double-click your way into the zip and run the launcher from there: Windows lets you look inside a zip, but the launcher needs real files next to it.","Move the unpacked folder (it is named something like `MeshOptimiser-main`) to a place you are allowed to write to, for example your Documents folder. The app creates two folders inside it, `.venv` and `inbox`.","Open the folder. You should see `start.bat`, `start_hidden.vbs`, `serve.py` and `index.html`, among others."]],["note","Windows may ask whether you are sure you want to open a file that was downloaded from the internet when you start `start.bat`. If you trust the download, confirm. `start.bat` is a plain text file: right-click it and choose {{Edit}} if you want to read what it does before you run it."],["h","Step 2: Python (usually nothing to do)"],["p","There are three cases, and the launcher handles all of them."],["terms",[["You already have Python 3.10, 3.11 or 3.12","Nothing to do. The launcher finds it, even when you have several versions installed, and picks the newest supported one. A newer Python, such as 3.13, can stay installed next to it: it is simply not used."],["You have no Python","The launcher says “Python was not found on this PC” and asks “Install Python 3.12 automatically now”. Press [[Y]]. It tries `winget` first, and if that fails it downloads the official installer from python.org and runs it quietly, for your user only, with Python added to the PATH. When it is done, the launcher carries on by itself."],["You only have an unsupported Python","The launcher names the version it found, for example “Found Python 3.13”, and offers to install 3.12 next to it. Your own Python is left as it is."]]],["p","If you prefer to install Python yourself, go to `https://www.python.org/downloads/`, download Python 3.12 and run the installer. On its first screen, tick “Add Python to PATH”. Then close every open terminal window before you start the launcher."],["h","Step 3: the first start"],["p","Double-click `start.bat`. A black window with white text opens. This is the launcher, and it is also the server that the app runs on, so leave it open while you work. This is what it prints on a first run:"],["terms",[["`No file dropped - starting viewer empty.`","You started it by double-clicking, so it opens the app without a file. You can also drag a file onto `start.bat`; see [Start it the first time](#first-start)."],["`[1/4] Checking Python...`","It looks for a supported Python and prints its version, for example `Python 3.12.7`."],["`[2/4] Checking local Python environment...`","Then `First-time setup - creating .venv (takes ~30 seconds)...`. This builds the private Python environment inside the app folder."],["`Installing dependencies (takes 1-3 minutes the first time)...`","It downloads and installs the three add-ons the converter needs: cadquery-ocp (the CAD reader), trimesh and numpy. This is the step that needs the internet. The text scrolls quickly. It is working, even if it looks quiet for a while."],["`Setup complete.`","The environment is ready. On every later start this part is replaced by `Found .venv - reusing it.`"],["`[3/4] Starting the local server...`","The server starts and prints the address it is using, normally `http://localhost:4242`. The app opens in a window of its own and shows the start screen."],["`[4/4] Server stopped.`","You will only see this when the app has been quit. A clean quit closes the launcher window by itself."]]],["h","Step 4: check that it works"],["steps",["On the start screen, click one of the four shapes (Cube, Sphere, Cylinder or Torus). It opens in the 3D view. This needs no file, so it is a quick way to know that the app itself is fine.","Press [[F1]] to open the help window, and [[Esc]] to close it again.","Optional: drag a small STEP file onto the window, to test the converter as well. See [Open your first file](#first-file)."]],["p","If something stopped on the way, find the message in [Install problems, message by message](#install-problems)."],["h","What the install left on your computer"],["list",["The app folder, with a `.venv` folder (up to about 2 GB) and an `inbox` folder (converted files) inside it.","Python 3.12, only if the launcher installed it for you. It shows up in {{Settings}}, {{Apps}} as a Python 3.12 entry, and you can remove it there like any other program.","Nothing else from the app itself: no service, no startup entry and no registry entries. (Python’s own installer adds its usual entries, if it was installed.) Your settings are kept by your browser."]],["see",["install-problems","setup-first-session","desktop-shortcut","first-start"]]]},{"slug":"install-macos","group":"install","title":"Install on macOS, step by step","summary":"From the zip to a working app on a Mac: Python, the first run, the one-time macOS permission prompt, and what you should see at every step.","blocks":[["p","This page walks through the whole install on a Mac. It takes about ten minutes, most of it the one-time setup on the first start. Nothing is installed system-wide apart from Python itself, if you do not have it yet. The app lives in one folder."],["h","Before you start"],["list",["About 2 GB of free disk space where you put the folder.","An internet connection for the first start. After that the app runs offline.","Chrome, Edge or Brave if you want the app in a window of its own. With Safari or Firefox as your only browser, it opens in a tab, and works the same."]],["h","Step 1: download and unpack"],["steps",["Open the Download page of this site and press the download button. The zip is saved in your Downloads folder.","Double-click the zip. macOS unpacks it next to the zip, into a folder named something like `MeshOptimiser-main`.","Move that folder where you want to keep it, for example into Documents.","Open the folder. You should see `start.command`, `serve.py` and `index.html`, among others. The folder `.venv` that appears later is hidden in Finder, because its name starts with a dot. Press [[Cmd + Shift + .]] to show hidden files if you want to see it."]],["h","Step 2: Python"],["p","The launcher needs Python 3.10, 3.11 or 3.12, and looks for `python3.12`, `python3.11`, `python3.10` and then `python3`, in that order. The Python that comes with some Macs, or a newer one such as 3.13, is skipped if it is not a supported version."],["terms",[["You have a supported Python","Nothing to do."],["You have none, and you have Homebrew","Homebrew is a tool for installing software from the Terminal. The launcher says “Homebrew is available - installing Python 3.12” and runs it for you. It takes about a minute. Python 3.12 is installed next to anything you already have."],["You have neither","The launcher tells you to install Python 3.12 from `https://www.python.org/downloads/`. Download the macOS installer, run it, then double-click `start.command` again. Or install Homebrew first (`https://brew.sh`), and let the launcher do the rest."]]],["h","Step 3: the first start"],["steps",["Double-click `start.command`. A Terminal window opens. This is the launcher and the server, so leave it open while you work.","If macOS says “Operation not permitted” or that it cannot open the file, right-click `start.command`, choose {{Open}}, and then {{Open}} again in the dialog. macOS blocks a double-click on a freshly unzipped script the first time. The launcher also removes the download flag from the folder, so this is only needed once.","macOS may ask whether Terminal can access files in the folder where you put the app, for example Documents. Allow it. The app cannot read its own files without it.","The launcher prints that it is creating a virtual environment in `.venv`, with the version of Python it found, and builds the app’s private Python environment (about 30 seconds).","Then it prints `Installing/updating Python dependencies...` and installs the three add-ons the converter needs: cadquery-ocp (the CAD reader), trimesh and numpy. This is the step that needs the internet, and takes one to three minutes. The text scrolls quickly. It is working, even if it looks quiet for a while.","Finally it prints `Starting server...`. The app opens in a window of its own (or in your browser) and shows the start screen."]],["p","Every later start skips the setup and takes a second or two."],["note","If you copy the app folder from a Windows computer, the launcher notices that `.venv` was built on Windows (“Detected a non-macOS .venv”) and rebuilds it for the Mac by itself."],["h","Step 4: check that it works"],["steps",["On the start screen, click one of the four shapes (Cube, Sphere, Cylinder or Torus). It opens in the 3D view. This needs no file, so it is a quick way to know that the app itself is fine.","Press [[F1]] to open the help window, and [[Esc]] to close it again.","Optional: drag a small STEP file onto the window, to test the converter as well. See [Open your first file](#first-file)."]],["p","If something stopped on the way, find the message in [Install problems, message by message](#install-problems)."],["h","Start it from the Terminal instead"],["p","You can also run the launcher from a terminal opened in the app folder. This is useful when you want to see every message, pass an option or open a file straight away:"],["code","./start.command"],["code","./start.command part.step"],["code","./start.command --tab"],["p","A file name opens that file. Anything that starts with two dashes is passed on to the server; `--tab` opens the app in an ordinary browser tab instead of a window of its own."],["h","What the install left on your computer"],["list",["The app folder, with a `.venv` folder (up to about 2 GB) and an `inbox` folder (converted files) inside it.","Python 3.12, only if you or the launcher installed it for this.","Nothing else. Your settings are kept by your browser."]],["see",["install-problems","setup-first-session","desktop-shortcut","first-start"]]]},{"slug":"setup-first-session","group":"install","title":"Set it up after installing","summary":"A checklist for the first session: check the renderer, choose the units, set the look and the performance options, and know where your settings are kept.","blocks":[["p","The app works the moment it opens, so none of this is required. It is a short checklist of the choices that are worth a minute on the first day, in the order that is most useful. Everything here is in {{Settings}}: press [[Ctrl + ,]] (on a Mac, [[Cmd + ,]]) or use {{Menu}}, then {{Settings…}}. Changes apply as you make them, with nothing to confirm."],["h","1. Check which renderer you have"],["p","The viewer draws with WebGPU when your browser and graphics card support it, and with WebGL2 when they do not. Both give the same picture. WebGPU is faster on heavy models."],["steps",["Open {{Settings}}, then {{About}}. The details list the renderer, your browser and the size of your screen.","Open {{Performance}}. {{Renderer}} shows what is in use. Change it to WebGL2 only if the 3D view is empty or glitchy. The page reloads when you change it."]],["p","If you are on WebGL2 and want WebGPU, update your browser and your graphics driver first."],["h","2. Choose the units you think in"],["p","Under {{Scene}}, {{Display units}} sets the unit for sizes and distances on screen: millimetres, centimetres, metres, inches, feet, or none. A STEP file normally comes in millimetres. This setting belongs to the open scene and is saved with it, so check it again for a scene from a different source."],["p","Under {{Scene}}, {{Up axis (viewport)}} is Z for most CAD files and Y for glTF files and some 3D programs. It changes how the model is shown. What the exported file uses is chosen in the Export window."],["h","3. Make it comfortable to look at"],["p","Under {{Appearance}} you can change the accent colour, the surfaces, the typeface, the text size, the roundness of corners and the height of buttons and fields. None of it touches your models. {{Reset}} at the bottom of the section puts everything back."],["p","Under {{Viewport}}, check {{Background}} and {{Ground grid}}. Switch {{Outlines}} off if you prefer shading without edge lines."],["h","4. Set the performance options for your computer"],["terms",[["A fast computer, models up to a few hundred thousand triangles","Leave everything as it is."],["A heavy assembly, or a modest graphics card","Keep {{Skip tiny parts}} and {{Lower the resolution if it stutters}} on. Set {{Quality}} to Low if the view still stutters."],["Only the exported result matters, not the view","Make the model lighter first. See [A sensible order to work in](#workflow)."]]],["h","5. Decide how the app starts"],["p","Under {{General}}:"],["list",["{{Start screen}} and {{Offer the last file}} control whether the start screen opens with a Resume button for the file you had last.","{{Splash screen}} is the short splash with the version number. Switch it off if you would rather have the app fade straight in.","{{Warn before leaving with unsaved changes}} is on by default. It asks before a window with unsaved edits is closed. Leave it on.","{{Undo history}} is 50, 200 or 500 steps. A longer history uses more memory."]],["h","6. Learn the three keys that find everything"],["keys",[["F1","The help window: every article of this knowledge base, with a search, and it works offline."],["Ctrl + K","Search: every menu item and every control in the sidebars, with suggestions for what is selected."],["?","The list of every keyboard shortcut."]]],["h","Where your settings are kept"],["p","In your browser’s storage, on this computer, not in the app folder. That has three consequences:"],["list",["They survive an update of the app.","They are separate for each browser. The app window the launcher opens uses your usual browser profile, so an installed app in Chrome and a tab in Edge each have their own settings.","The folder can be deleted and unpacked again without losing them. See [Move, copy or remove the app](#move-or-remove).","They belong to the address the app runs at. If the launcher ever has to choose another port than 4242, the browser treats it as a different site, and the settings seem to be gone. They come back when the app runs at its usual address again."]],["p","The buttons under {{Storage}} clear each kind of remembered thing, one at a time: the list of recent files, file permissions, the “don’t ask again” import answers, panel sizes, or all settings at once."],["see",["settings","install-gltfpack","privacy-offline","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"install-gltfpack","group":"install","title":"Add gltfpack for smaller files","summary":"An optional free tool. With it on your PATH, the converter compresses its GLB files with Meshopt, which can make them many times smaller. How to install it and how to check.","blocks":[["p","The converter can compress the models it writes with Meshopt compression, and can simplify them while it does. It does that through a separate free program called gltfpack. The app works without it: the files are simply larger. If gltfpack is installed and findable, compression turns on by itself, and the README of the app puts the saving at about ten times for a typical model."],["h","What “findable” means"],["p","The converter asks the system for a program named `gltfpack`, the same way a terminal does. It is found when the folder that holds it is on your PATH, which is the list of folders the system searches for programs. That is why an installed gltfpack that is not on the PATH does nothing."],["h","Install it with npm"],["p","This is the way the converter itself suggests. It needs Node.js, a free program for running JavaScript, from `https://nodejs.org`. Install Node.js with its normal installer first, then open a new terminal and type:"],["code","npm install -g gltfpack"],["p","The `-g` means “for the whole computer”, which puts gltfpack on the PATH for you."],["h","Or download the program"],["p","The author of the tool publishes ready-made downloads for each system at `https://github.com/zeux/meshoptimizer/releases`, and describes the tool at `https://meshoptimizer.org/gltf/`."],["steps",["Download the gltfpack file for your system from the releases page.","Put it in a folder that you will keep, for example `C:\\Tools` on Windows or `/usr/local/bin` on a Mac. On a Mac, in a terminal, make it runnable with `chmod +x gltfpack`.","Windows: press the Windows key, type “environment variables” and open {{Edit environment variables for your account}}. Select {{Path}} under User variables, press {{Edit}}, then {{New}}, paste the folder, and press {{OK}} on every window. A Mac folder such as `/usr/local/bin` is on the PATH already.","On a Mac, the first run of a downloaded program can be blocked. Right-click it in Finder, choose {{Open}} once, and confirm."]],["h","Check that it is found"],["steps",["Open a new terminal. A terminal that was open during the install does not see the change.","Type `gltfpack -h` and press [[Enter]]. A list of options means it works. “Command not found” or “is not recognized” means the folder is not on the PATH yet.","Quit MeshOptimiser from {{Menu}}, {{Quit app}}, and start it again with its launcher. The server reads the PATH when it starts, so it has to be restarted to see the new program."]],["p","Convert a STEP file. The launcher window then shows a line that starts with `post:` and says gltfpack. Without gltfpack, a conversion that asks for compression prints “gltfpack not on PATH” and carries on without it."],["h","Using it from the command line"],["p","The options that use it, such as `--meshopt` and `--simplify`, are explained in [The command-line converter](#command-line)."],["note","Draco compression, an alternative that GLB export offers, needs nothing to be installed: its encoder is bundled with the app."],["see",["command-line","install-problems","choose-format","setup-first-session"]]]},{"slug":"desktop-shortcut","group":"install","title":"Add a shortcut or an icon","summary":"Start the app from the desktop, the Start menu or the Dock instead of opening the folder every time. Windows and macOS, with and without the launcher window.","blocks":[["p","You start the app by running its launcher, which lives in the app folder. A shortcut saves you opening the folder. Make the shortcut to the launcher, not a copy of the launcher: the launcher finds everything relative to its own folder, so it has to stay where it is."],["h","Windows"],["p","There are two launchers. `start.bat` shows its black window, which is useful when you want to see what is happening. `start_hidden.vbs` does the same without the black window, so only the app opens. The first time, when the Python environment does not exist yet, even `start_hidden.vbs` shows a normal window, because that run asks questions and takes minutes."],["steps",["Open the app folder in File Explorer.","Right-click `start_hidden.vbs` (or `start.bat`) and choose {{Show more options}} if you are on Windows 11, then {{Send to}}, then {{Desktop (create shortcut)}}.","Optional: give it the app’s icon. Right-click the new shortcut, choose {{Properties}}, then {{Change Icon…}}, {{Browse…}}, and pick `start.ico` from the app folder.","Optional: rename the shortcut to “MeshOptimiser”.","Optional: right-click the shortcut and choose {{Pin to Start}}."]],["note","With `start_hidden.vbs`, if something goes wrong the app shows a message that MeshOptimiser stopped with an error and asks you to double-click `start.bat` to see what happened. That is the cue to use the visible launcher."],["h","macOS"],["steps",["Open the app folder in Finder.","Click `start.command` once, then choose {{File}}, {{Make Alias}}. An alias is a shortcut that points to the original.","Drag the alias to the Desktop, and rename it if you like.","To keep it in the Dock, drag the alias onto the right-hand end of the Dock, next to the Trash. It sits there as a file."]],["p","On a Mac the launcher opens a Terminal window and keeps it open. To avoid seeing it, minimise it. Closing it stops the app."],["h","The installed-app icon, as well"],["p","Chrome and Edge can install the page as an app of its own, with its own icon in the Start menu or Dock. That icon opens a window onto the local server, so the server still has to be started first. The shortcut above is what starts the server. See [Install it as an app window](#install-as-app)."],["h","Start with a file"],["p","On Windows, drag a `.step`, `.stp`, `.glb` or `.gltf` file onto `start.bat` or onto a shortcut to it, and the app starts with that file open. On a Mac, give `start.command` the file name in the Terminal. See [Start it the first time](#first-start)."],["see",["install-as-app","first-start","stop-restart-update","files-in-folder"]]]},{"slug":"move-or-remove","group":"install","title":"Move, copy or remove the app","summary":"Move the folder, put it on another computer, keep what matters, or remove everything it ever stored, including the settings in your browser.","blocks":[["p","The app is one folder, and what it stores outside that folder is small and easy to find. This page covers moving it, copying it to another computer, backing up what matters, and removing it completely. Quit the app first in every case, with {{Menu}}, then {{Quit app}}."],["h","Move the folder"],["steps",["Quit the app.","Move the whole folder to its new place, with File Explorer or Finder.","Start it again from the new place. If it starts, you are done.","If the launcher says that the Python environment in `.venv` no longer works, delete the `.venv` folder and start again. It rebuilds in a few minutes. Nothing else is lost."]],["p","Shortcuts you made point to the old place. Make them again from the new one. See [Add a shortcut or an icon](#desktop-shortcut)."],["h","Put it on another computer"],["p","The clean way is to install it again from the zip, as described in [Install on Windows](#install-windows) or [Install on macOS](#install-macos). The Python environment in `.venv` belongs to the computer it was made on, and it is not worth carrying along. If you do copy a whole folder, the launcher repairs what it can: a `.venv` from Windows is rebuilt on a Mac automatically, and any other broken environment is reported with the instruction to delete it."],["p","Your settings and recent files do not move with the folder. See below."],["h","What is worth backing up"],["terms",[["Scenes you saved","Wherever you chose to put them. They are your own files, outside the app folder unless you saved into it."],["Exported files","The same: they are wherever you exported them to."],["`inbox`","The converted GLB files. A STEP file that was converted once is converted again quickly when you open it, because the result is kept here. Copy out the ones you want to keep, and delete the rest."],["Your original STEP files","The app never changes them, and never moves them."],["Settings, the recent files list, panel sizes","Kept by your browser, not in the folder. They stay on the computer and in the browser profile, whatever you do to the app folder. To start over on the same computer, use the buttons under {{Settings}}, {{Storage}}."]]],["h","Remove the app completely"],["steps",["Optional, and best done first: open {{Settings}}, {{Storage}}, and clear what you do not want kept: recent files, file access, import options, panel sizes. {{All settings}} puts every preference back to its default.","Quit the app from {{Menu}}, {{Quit app}}, and check that the launcher window has closed.","If you installed the page as an app, remove that too. In Chrome, open `chrome://apps`, right-click MeshOptimiser and choose {{Uninstall}}. In Edge, open `edge://apps` and do the same. You can also open the app and choose {{Uninstall}} from the menu of its title bar.","Delete the app folder, and any shortcuts you made.","Optional: remove what the browser still holds for the address `localhost`. In the browser’s settings, search for “site data” and delete the entry for `localhost:4242`. If you did the first step, there is little left.","Optional: remove Python, if it was installed only for this. On Windows, open {{Settings}}, {{Apps}} and uninstall “Python 3.12”. On a Mac, remove it the way you installed it: with `brew uninstall python@3.12` if Homebrew installed it."]],["p","The app adds nothing else: no service, no scheduled task, no startup entry and no registry entries of its own to clean up."],["see",["stop-restart-update","files-in-folder","privacy-offline","settings"]]]},{"slug":"install-problems","group":"install","title":"Install problems, message by message","summary":"Every message the launcher can print when the setup stops, what it means, and exactly what to do about it. Windows and macOS.","blocks":[["p","When the setup cannot go on, the launcher says why in plain words and stops. Find your message below. If none matches, run the launcher from a terminal so that the window stays open, and read the last lines. On Windows: open the app folder, click the address bar, type `cmd`, press [[Enter]], then type `start.bat`. On a Mac: open Terminal, type `cd ` and a space, drag the folder onto the window, press [[Enter]], then type `./start.command`."],["h","Python"],["terms",[["“Python was not found on this PC”","Windows. Press [[Y]] to let the launcher install Python 3.12. If you press [[N]], install Python 3.12 from `https://www.python.org/downloads/`, tick “Add Python to PATH”, close the window, and start again."],["“Found Python 3.13, but MeshOptimiser needs Python 3.10, 3.11 or 3.12”","Your Python is too new for the CAD reader. Let the launcher install 3.12 next to it, or install 3.12 yourself. Both versions can stay."],["“Auto-install failed”","Windows. Both ways of installing Python failed, usually for lack of an internet connection or because of a restriction on the computer. Install Python 3.12 by hand and start again."],["“Python was installed but is not yet visible on PATH”","Windows. The install worked, but this window does not know yet. Close the launcher window, open the folder again, and double-click `start.bat` once more."],["“python3 is not installed” or “Homebrew is available - installing Python 3.12”","macOS. The first means no Python was found and Homebrew is not there either: install Python 3.12 from python.org or install Homebrew, and start again. The second is the launcher doing it for you; wait for it."],["“That did not give a usable Python”","macOS. Homebrew ran but there is still no supported Python. Install Python 3.12 from `https://www.python.org/downloads/` and double-click `start.command` again."]]],["h","The Python environment"],["terms",[["“Failed to create virtual environment”","The `venv` part of Python is missing, or the folder is not writable. Move the app folder to somewhere you may write to, such as Documents, and make sure a normal Python from python.org is installed."],["“The Python environment in the .venv folder no longer works”","The Python it was made from has been removed or replaced. Delete the `.venv` folder and start again."],["“The environment in the .venv folder was made with the Python above”","An older environment, made with a version outside 3.10 to 3.12. Delete the `.venv` folder and start again."],["“Detected a non-macOS .venv (probably built on Windows). Rebuilding...”","Not a problem: the folder came from a Windows computer, and the launcher is making a new environment for the Mac."],["“ModuleNotFoundError: No module named ‘OCP’”","The environment is incomplete. Delete the `.venv` folder and start again. The same goes for a missing `numpy` or `trimesh`."]]],["h","Downloading the add-ons"],["terms",[["“ERROR: pip install failed”","The add-ons could not be downloaded. Check your internet connection, check that Python is 3.10 to 3.12, and check that the drive has about 2 GB free. Then start again: the launcher picks up where it stopped."],["Slow or stuck at “Installing dependencies”","The CAD reader is a large download. On a slow connection it takes longer than the three minutes mentioned. Let it finish. If nothing has moved for more than ten minutes, close the window, delete the `.venv` folder and start again."],["A network that needs a proxy","The installer that downloads the add-ons (pip) reads the standard `HTTPS_PROXY` setting. In a terminal opened in the app folder, set it first, then run the launcher. On Windows: `set HTTPS_PROXY=http://your-proxy:port`. On a Mac: `export HTTPS_PROXY=http://your-proxy:port`. Your network administrator knows the address."]]],["h","The app does not open"],["terms",[["The window does not appear, but the launcher window says it is running","Look at the address in the launcher window and open it in a browser by hand. Normally `http://localhost:4242`."],["The address is not localhost:4242","Something else on your computer was using that number, so the server picked another one. This is fine. The launcher prints the address it chose. You can also ask for a number yourself, from a terminal opened in the app folder: `./start.command --port 5000` on a Mac, `.venv\\Scripts\\python serve.py --port 5000` on Windows."],["“Operation not permitted” on a Mac","Right-click `start.command`, choose {{Open}}, and confirm. Only needed once."],["An empty page, or “This page needs a local server”","The page was opened straight from the file, or the server is not running. Start the app with its launcher. Do not open `index.html` by double-clicking it."],["“MeshOptimiser stopped with an error”","Windows, when started with `start_hidden.vbs`. Double-click `start.bat` instead, which shows what went wrong."]]],["h","Starting with a file"],["terms",[["“ERROR: file not found”","The path you gave does not exist. Drag the file onto the launcher instead of typing the path."],["“Unsupported file type”","Starting the app with a file works for `.step`, `.stp`, `.glb` and `.gltf`. Open the other formats from inside the app."],["“keeps part of the model in separate files”","A `.gltf` that has its geometry or textures in files beside it cannot be handed over as one file. Save it as a single `.glb`, or open it from inside the app."]]],["h","Still stuck?"],["p","[Report a problem](https://lukagrcar.com/apps/meshoptimiser/report.html) on the website. Say which system you use, which version of Python the launcher printed, and paste the last lines of the launcher window into the form; it packs them into one file you can send. See [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting) for problems that come after the install, such as an empty 3D view or a slow conversion."],["see",["install-windows","install-macos","requirements","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"cad-files-step-first","group":"cad","title":"Native CAD files: export a STEP file first","summary":"MeshOptimiser opens STEP, not SolidWorks, Inventor, Creo or CATIA files. Why, which STEP version to pick, and where to find the steps for your program.","blocks":[["p","MeshOptimiser reads STEP files. It does not read the files your CAD program saves for itself, such as SolidWorks (`.sldprt`, `.sldasm`), Inventor (`.ipt`, `.iam`), Creo (`.prt`, `.asm`), Fusion (`.f3d`), Rhino (`.3dm`), CATIA or NX files. Those formats belong to the company that made the program, and reading them needs that company’s own software, which a free app cannot include."],["p","Almost every CAD program can write STEP, and STEP is the one format they all share. So the job is always the same: open your model in your CAD program, save a copy as STEP, and open that copy in MeshOptimiser."],["h","Pick your program"],["list",["[Autodesk Inventor](#step-from-inventor)","[Onshape](#step-from-onshape)","[PTC Creo](#step-from-creo)","[Rhino](#step-from-rhino)"]],["p","Using something else, such as NX, CATIA or Solid Edge? Look for Export or Save As in its File menu and choose STEP. The ideas below apply to any program."],["h","What a STEP file keeps, and what it leaves behind"],["terms",[["Kept","The exact shape of every part, the assembly tree with the names of the parts, and the colours, if the STEP version you choose carries them."],["Left behind","The feature tree, sketches, dimensions and the history of how the part was made."]]],["h","Which STEP version to choose"],["p","Many programs ask for an “application protocol”, a short code for how much information goes into the file."],["terms",[["AP203","The plain one: geometry, the structure and configuration data. No colours and no layers."],["AP214","Everything AP203 has, plus colours and layers. This is the usual choice for MeshOptimiser, because the colours arrive."],["AP242","The newest. It merges AP203 and AP214 and adds more manufacturing information. Not every program offers it, and some do not offer it for assemblies."]]],["p","If your program offers AP214, choose it. If it does not, use whatever it calls its normal STEP export and check the colours after opening the file."],["h","Export the whole assembly into one file"],["p","MeshOptimiser opens one file. Several programs can also write an assembly as a top-level file plus one file per part. Leave that switched off, so that everything is in one STEP file."],["h","If you cannot get a STEP file"],["p","Export a mesh instead, as OBJ, FBX, STL or GLB. MeshOptimiser opens those directly. You lose something: the copies of a repeated part are found when a STEP file is converted, so a mesh file arrives with every copy as a mesh of its own, and the model is heavier to start with."],["note","Menu names change a little from one version of a CAD program to the next. If your screen differs from these steps, search your program’s own help for “export STEP”."],["see",["first-file","file-formats","convert-step-to-glb"]]]},{"slug":"step-from-inventor","group":"cad","title":"How to export a STEP assembly from Autodesk Inventor","summary":"Save an Inventor assembly as one STEP file with Save Copy As, pick AP214, and learn what Shrinkwrap can do for a very large assembly.","blocks":[["p","Inventor writes STEP with Save Copy As. When you save an assembly this way, the assembly and all the parts it refers to go into a single file, which is what MeshOptimiser wants."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the assembly (the `.iam` file).","Choose {{File}}, {{Save As}}, then {{Save Copy As}}.","In {{Save as type}}, choose the STEP files entry (`*.stp`, `*.ste`, `*.step`).","Click {{Options}}. The STEP options window opens.","Under the application protocol, choose {{214}}.","Leave the spline fit accuracy as it is, press {{OK}}, name the file and press {{Save}}."]],["h","What the choices mean"],["terms",[["203","Configuration Controlled Design. The plain protocol, without colours."],["214","Automotive Design. The one to choose here, because it also carries colours and layers."],["242","Managed Model Based 3D Engineering. It includes product manufacturing information. Inventor does not offer it for assemblies."],["Spline fit accuracy","How closely curves are approximated, between 0.00001 cm and 0.001 cm. A smaller value is more exact and makes a bigger file. The default is fine."]]],["h","If the assembly is very large"],["p","Inventor has a command called Shrinkwrap that builds a single simplified part from an assembly. One of its options, {{Remove internal parts}}, is on by default. It looks at the model from 14 directions and drops the parts that cannot be seen, which is close to what {{Select hidden parts}} does in MeshOptimiser. You can export the shrinkwrapped part as STEP instead of the full assembly. Autodesk’s help describes the command under “Create a Shrinkwrap part”."],["p","You do not have to do this. MeshOptimiser finds the hidden parts itself, so the full assembly works too. Shrinkwrap is useful when the full assembly is too big for your computer to export or convert comfortably, or when you want a simplified part for other reasons."],["note","If your version of Inventor looks different, search Autodesk’s help for “Save As Export Options”."],["h","Next"],["p","Open the file in MeshOptimiser: see [Open your first file](#first-file). To take out the hidden parts there, see [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts)."],["see",["cad-files-step-first","select-hidden-parts","convert-step-to-glb"]]]},{"slug":"step-from-onshape","group":"cad","title":"How to export a STEP assembly from Onshape","summary":"Export an Onshape assembly or Part Studio as STEP by right-clicking its tab, and why STEP is a better start than Onshape’s own glTF export.","blocks":[["p","Onshape works in the browser, and so does its export. You export from the tab at the bottom of the window."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open your model. For a whole assembly, open the Assembly tab. For parts, open the Part Studio.","Right-click the tab at the bottom of the window and choose {{Export}}.","Choose {{STEP}} as the format.","Check the options (below). The defaults are fine for a first try.","Choose how you want the file: {{Download}} saves it to your computer.","Press {{Export}}."]],["p","In an assembly, the tab’s Export writes the whole assembly at once."],["h","The options that matter"],["terms",[["Use latest version","On by default. The latest supported version of STEP is written."],["Use custom units for export","STEP only. Choose Millimeter, Centimeter, Meter, Inch or Foot. Millimetres are the usual choice for CAD."],["Preprocessing","STEP only. Options to remove small entities or to convert all surfaces to b-surfaces before export. Leave it as it is."]]],["h","Why STEP and not glTF"],["p","Onshape can also export glTF and GLB directly. People on Onshape’s own forum report that this export writes a separate mesh for every face of a part, which is slow to draw in real-time viewers. Exporting STEP and letting MeshOptimiser build the mesh avoids that, and it lets MeshOptimiser find the copies of repeated parts."],["note","If Onshape’s dialog looks different in your version, search its help for “Exporting files”."],["h","Next"],["p","Open the file in MeshOptimiser: see [Open your first file](#first-file)."],["see",["cad-files-step-first","first-file","convert-step-to-glb"]]]},{"slug":"step-from-creo","group":"cad","title":"How to export a STEP assembly from PTC Creo","summary":"Save a Creo assembly as one STEP file with Save a Copy, set the application protocol and keep Export assembly as on Single File.","blocks":[["p","Creo exports STEP with Save a Copy. The settings live in a window called STEP Export Profile Settings."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the assembly.","Choose {{File}}, {{Save As}}, then {{Save a Copy}}.","In the {{Type}} list, choose {{STEP (*.stp)}}.","Enter a file name, or accept the default.","Click {{Options}}. The editor {{STEP Export Profile Settings}} opens.","Set {{Application protocol}} to {{ap214_is}}.","Check that {{Export assembly as}} is on {{Single File}}. That is the default.","Press {{OK}} in the editor, and {{OK}} in the Save a Copy window."]],["h","What the choices mean"],["terms",[["Application protocol","Creo lists ap202_is, ap203_is (the default), ap203_is_ext, ap203_e2, ap209_dis, ap214_is, ap242 and ap242_e3. ap214_is is the AP214 protocol, the one that adds colours and layers in other CAD programs. Open the result in MeshOptimiser and check that the colours came through."],["Export assembly as: Single File","Writes all the geometry of the assembly into one file. This is what MeshOptimiser needs."],["Export assembly as: Separate Parts","Writes the structure to one file and every part as a file of its own. Do not use it for this."],["Export assembly as: Separate All","Writes the assembly, every sub-assembly and every part as files of their own. Do not use it for this."]]],["p","The {{Export assembly as}} choice appears when the application protocol is ap214_is, ap203_e2, ap242 or ap242_e3. PTC’s help says Separate Parts and Separate All are offered only for those protocols."],["h","Other things in the export"],["list",["Tick {{Customize Export}} in the Save a Copy window if you want to choose which layers go in, or the coordinate system. The defaults are fine to begin with.","The profile can be saved and loaded again with {{Load Profile}}, so you set it up once."]],["h","If the assembly is very large"],["p","Work on a copy, remove what you will not show, such as hardware inside housings, and export that. A smaller file converts faster. See also [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting) for ways to speed up the conversion."],["note","Menus differ between Creo releases. If yours does, search PTC’s help for “Exporting Part or Assembly to STEP”."],["h","Next"],["p","Open the file in MeshOptimiser: see [Open your first file](#first-file)."],["see",["cad-files-step-first","first-file","convert-step-to-glb"]]]},{"slug":"step-from-rhino","group":"cad","title":"How to export a STEP file from Rhino","summary":"Export selected Rhino objects as STEP with File, Export Selected: which protocol keeps colours, what happens to black objects, and what to do with meshes.","blocks":[["p","Rhino models are made of surfaces and solids, and Rhino writes them to STEP with its Export command."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the objects you want to export.","Choose {{File}}, then {{Export Selected}}. {{Save As}} writes the whole model instead.","In {{Save as type}}, choose {{STEP (.stp; .step)}}.","Name the file and press {{Save}}.","The window {{STEP Export Options}} opens. Choose {{AP214AutomotiveDesign}} as the schema, so colours and layers come along.","Leave the other options as they are, and press {{OK}}."]],["h","What the choices mean"],["terms",[["AP203ConfigControlDesign","The default. Geometry, topology and configuration data, without colours and layers."],["AP214AutomotiveDesign","Everything AP203 has, plus colours, layers and GD&T. Choose this one."],["AP214AutomotiveDesignCC2","A minor syntax update of the earlier AP214."],["AP242","The newest standard, new in Rhino 8. It merges AP203 and AP214."]]],["p","Rhino’s help says your company’s standards decide which schema to use, and that without a standard the default is fine. For MeshOptimiser, AP214AutomotiveDesign is the one that keeps colours."],["h","Things to know"],["list",["A Rhino object that is black gets no colour in the STEP file, so the next program gives it its default colour. This is on purpose, because black objects look like ink blots in some programs. If you want a colour on them, recolour the parts in MeshOptimiser.","SubD objects are turned into polysurfaces when they are exported.","If your model is already a mesh, for example from a scan, STEP is not the best route. Export it as OBJ, FBX or STL instead. MeshOptimiser opens those directly."]],["note","If Rhino’s dialog looks different in your version, search the Rhino help for “STEP import and export”."],["h","Next"],["p","Open the file in MeshOptimiser: see [Open your first file](#first-file)."],["see",["cad-files-step-first","first-file","convert-step-to-glb"]]]},{"slug":"the-window","group":"around","title":"The window","summary":"A tour of the screen: the top bar and tabs, the parts tree, the 3D view and its toolbars, the cards on the right, the drawers, the status bar and how to resize it all.","blocks":[["p","The window has four areas: a top bar, a left sidebar, the 3D view in the middle (called the viewport) and a right sidebar. A thin status bar runs under the viewport."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-viewer.webp","The whole window with an assembly open."],["p","When the app starts, a short splash screen shows the version and what the app is doing, then the window fades in. You can switch the splash off under {{Settings}}, {{General}}."],["h","Top bar"],["terms",[["The app’s mark","At the far left. Click it for the version number, what is new, links to the source code and to report a problem, {{Help & docs}} and the list of keyboard shortcuts."],["Menu","The file commands: {{New scene}}, {{Open file…}}, {{Import into scene…}}, {{Save scene…}}, {{Export…}}, {{Revert to source…}}, {{Scene settings…}}, {{Settings…}}, {{Help & docs}}, {{Welcome screen}} and {{Quit app}}."],["Sidebar button","Right after Menu. Shows or hides the left sidebar. When the sidebar is hidden, the button stays next to Menu."],["Scene tabs","One tab for each open scene, and a `+` to start a new one. See [Scene tabs](#scene-tabs)."],["Export","At the right. Writes the model to a file for another program. See [Export](#export)."],["Camera button","Next to Export. Saves a picture of the view. See [Screenshots](#screenshots)."]]],["h","Left sidebar"],["p","It shows the parts tree: every part of the model, arranged in groups, with a search field and three buttons above it ({{Collapse}}, {{Rename}}, {{Flatten}}). A {{Transform}} card with position, rotation and size can be shown at its foot. It works on several selected parts at once (see [Move, rotate, scale](#move-rotate-scale)). It floats over the bottom of the sidebar and takes its own room under the tree. The library of parts you can add is not in this sidebar any more. It is a drawer under the viewport (see below)."],["h","Viewport"],["terms",[["Tool strip, left edge","At the top, the mode button: Model, or the points, edges and polygons of [Edit mesh](#edit-mesh). Then the move, rotate and scale tools, {{Dynamic place}} ([[D]]), a button for the transform card, four view modes (solid, wireframe, X-ray, heatmap) and a button that highlights small parts. The Clay and CAD looks are in the {{Shading}} card on the right."],["View name, top centre","Says which camera view is active, {{Cam}} to begin with. Click it to switch to Top, Front or Side. Small pills to its right show a mode that is on: {{Isolated}}, {{Exploded}}, {{Measure}} and {{Snap}}. Click a pill to leave that mode."],["View cube, top right","A small cube that turns with the model. Click a face or an axis dot and the view turns there."],["Toolbar, bottom centre","Search, add a shape, {{Split}}, {{Remove holes}}, {{Draw lines}}, {{Smart fit}}, {{Remesh}}, {{Select hidden parts}}, {{Fasteners}}, {{Measure}}, {{Library}}, {{Materials}}, and `…` for the list of all commands."],["Edit mesh bar, right edge","A vertical bar that appears under the view cube while Edit mesh is on: how a click picks (Click, Box, Brush), the gizmo’s axes, Loops, and the buttons that change the picked pieces. The tool strip shows Points, Edges and Polygons as three buttons then. See [Edit mesh](#edit-mesh)."],["Shortcut tips, bottom right","A faint list of what the mouse and keys do right now. It changes when you select something."],["Command cards, bottom left","A tool with settings, such as Remove holes, Align to floor, Align parts or Dynamic place, opens as a card over the viewport. [[Enter]] runs it and [[Esc]] puts it away."]]],["h","Right sidebar"],["p","At the top is {{Properties}}. With nothing selected it describes the whole scene. With a selection it describes that: the name, the material, the triangle count with a bar that shows its share of the scene, and the number of vertices, the size and the mesh data. Under it are cards you open and close by clicking their title. A card slides open and shut. Every card starts folded except {{Properties}} and {{Shading}}, and the ones you open or close are remembered."],["terms",[["Shading","Every look for the Clay and CAD views as a small picture: Clay, Porcelain, Steel and Red wax, and the CAD looks Ceramic, Light and Mono. Click one to choose it and turn the view on. Click the lit one again to go back to Solid. The {{Outlines}} switch draws a fine dark line on the sharp edges of every part. See [Colours and materials](#colours-materials) and [View modes](#view-modes)."],["Selection & actions","Select all, invert, select similar, by colour, by name, and Merge, Group and Delete."],["Select similar","Sets how close a part has to look to the selected ones to count as similar."],["Inspect","{{Heavy parts}} (with a small count of how many there are), {{Filters & flags}}, {{Measurements}} (only while there is a measurement) and {{Exploded view}}."],["Clean up","{{Delete small parts}}, {{Auto cleanup}} and {{Tidy geometry}}."],["Reduce","{{Smart optimise}} and {{Reduce triangles}}, which holds Fit to budget, Decimate, Smart fit all parts and the optimisation report."]]],["p","Tools with their own settings, such as {{Align to floor}} and {{Find stacked copies}}, are not cards in the sidebar. They open over the viewport, from the toolbar, the list of all commands or the search."],["h","Status bar"],["p","Under the viewport: the number of vertices, the size of the mesh data, how many parts are selected (click it to frame them), how many are flagged (click it to isolate them), the frame rate, and a {{Console}} button. The numbers of parts and triangles are in {{Properties}}."],["h","Drawers"],["p","Three panels slide up from the bottom of the viewport and take turns: only one is open at a time. Drag the top edge of a drawer to change its height. The Library and the Materials drawer can also pop out as floating windows over the view. Then they do not take turns, and both can be open side by side."],["terms",[["Library","The parts you can add. Opened with the {{Library}} button on the bottom toolbar or [[Shift + L]]. See [The library](#library)."],["Materials","Every material in the scene. Opened with [[Shift + M]]. See [Colours and materials](#colours-materials)."],["Console","A log of everything the app has done, with filters for information, warnings and errors and a button to copy it. Opened with the backtick key or the {{Console}} button in the status bar."]]],["h","Make room"],["p","Each sidebar has a drag handle on its inner edge. A thin line shows when you rest the pointer on it for a moment."],["list",["Drag a handle to make a sidebar wider or narrower. Double-click it to put the width back.","The left sidebar cannot be narrower than 220 pixels and the right one not narrower than 240. Narrower than that, buttons and labels would wrap or be cut off.","The window itself cannot be smaller than 960 by 560 pixels. In a smaller window the app scrolls instead of squeezing the panels.","Use the sidebar button in the top bar to hide the left sidebar.","On a narrow window, buttons of the bottom toolbar fold away into the `…` list."]],["h","Help"],["p","Press [[F1]] to open the help inside the app. It holds the same articles as this website and works without an internet connection. See [The help window](#help-window)."],["see",["help-window","move-the-view","parts-tree","search-and-commands","read-the-numbers","edit-mesh"]]]},{"slug":"move-the-view","group":"around","title":"Move the view","summary":"Orbit, pan and zoom with the mouse, frame a part with F, and switch between the camera, Top, Front and Side views.","blocks":[["p","Moving the view never changes the model. You are only moving the camera."],["h","With the mouse"],["keys",[["Drag","Orbit: turn the view around the model (left mouse button)"],["Middle drag","Pan: slide the view sideways (press the wheel and drag)"],["Right drag","Pan as well"],["Scroll","Zoom in and out"],["Alt + Right drag","Zoom smoothly: drag up to come closer"]]],["h","Bring things into view"],["keys",[["F","Frame: fit the selected parts in the view, or the whole model when nothing is selected"]]],["p","Frame stops where everything it frames is in the picture, with a small margin. A long thin part seen from the side fills the width, and a small part in a big assembly fills the view."],["p","If you get lost, click an empty spot to clear the selection and press [[F]]. The whole model comes back into view."],["h","The arrow to a selection out of sight"],["p","When something is selected and none of it is in the picture, a faint arrow sits at the edge of the view, on the side the selection is on. Click the arrow and the view frames the selection. To switch the arrow off, open {{Settings}}, {{Viewport}} and turn off {{Arrow to a selection out of view}}."],["h","Standard views"],["p","The pill at the top centre of the viewport names the current view. Click it to choose another."],["keys",[["Ctrl + 1","Cam: the free camera with perspective"],["Ctrl + 2","Top"],["Ctrl + 3","Front"],["Ctrl + 4","Side"]]],["p","Top, Front and Side are flat views without perspective, like a technical drawing. As soon as you orbit out of one, the app returns to the free camera from where you are."],["h","The view cube"],["p","The small cube in the top right corner turns with the model. Click one of its faces, or the dot at the end of an axis, to look straight along that axis: Top, Bottom, Front, Back, Side or Left. The view pill shows the name."],["h","The floor grid"],["p","The grid marks the floor of the scene. Press [[G]] to hide or show it."],["h","Settings that change how the view moves"],["p","Under {{Settings}}, {{Camera}}:"],["terms",[["Projection","Perspective, where far things look smaller, or Orthographic, where they do not."],["Field of view","How wide the lens is."],["Clip planes","How near and how far the camera can see. Leave it on Auto unless parts of a very small or very large model are cut off."],["Orbit around","What the view turns around. {{Selection}}: the selected parts, or the centre of the view when nothing is selected. {{Scene}}: always the centre of the view. {{Cursor}}: the point under the pointer when the drag starts."],["Zoom toward the cursor","The wheel zooms toward the pointer instead of the centre."],["Pan speed","How far a pan drag moves the view. Orbit speed and Zoom speed are beside it."],["Invert orbit direction","The view turns the other way for a drag: drag right and the model turns left."],["Stop at the horizon","The camera cannot go below the grid, so you do not end up looking at the model from underneath by accident."]]],["note","On a heavy model, very small parts are left out while the view is moving and drawn again the moment it stops. This keeps movement smooth. You can turn it off under {{Settings}}, {{Performance}}, {{Skip tiny parts}}."],["see",["select-parts","view-modes","settings","shortcuts"]]]},{"slug":"select-parts","group":"around","title":"Select parts","summary":"Click, add with Shift, toggle with Ctrl, drag a box, or let the app select by shape, colour or name.","blocks":[["p","Most tools work on the parts you have selected. A selected part gets an outline in the viewport and its row is highlighted in the parts tree."],["h","In the viewport"],["keys",[["Click","Select one part. The tree scrolls to its row."],["Shift + Click","Add a part to the selection"],["Ctrl + Click","Add a part, or take it out if it was selected"],["Ctrl + Drag","Box select: draw a rectangle around parts"],["Ctrl + Shift + Drag","Box select and add to what is already selected"],["Esc","Select nothing. A click on empty space does the same."]]],["h","Click, Box or Brush"],["p","The selection-tool button in the left toolbar chooses what a drag does. Press it and let go over a row of its list to pick that tool, or let go on the button and click a row. With {{Edit mesh}} on, the same button picks pieces of a mesh instead of whole parts."],["terms",[["Click","The usual way: click a part."],["Box","A plain left drag draws a rectangle. A part is picked only when one of its own triangles is under the rectangle, not when its bounding box is."],["Brush","A left drag paints: every part under the circle is picked. Hold [[Shift]] to add to what is picked; without it, every stroke starts from nothing. Drag with the middle button to change the size of the circle: to the right is bigger, to the left smaller."]]],["p","In every tool, [[Alt]] + drag turns the camera."],["h","In the parts tree"],["keys",[["Click","Select one part. Click a group to select every part inside it."],["Ctrl + Click","Add or remove one row"],["Shift + Click","Select every row between the last one you clicked and this one"]]],["h","Let the app select"],["p","The card {{Selection & actions}} in the right sidebar has buttons for the common cases:"],["terms",[["All","Selects every visible part. [[Ctrl + A]]."],["Invert","Selects what was not selected, and the other way round. [[Ctrl + I]]."],["Clear","Selects nothing. [[Esc]]."],["Select similar","Adds every part that has the same shape as the selection. Select one bolt, press it, and all the bolts like it are selected."],["By colour","Selects every part that shares a colour with the selection."],["By name","Asks for a pattern and selects every part whose name matches it. Typing `screw` selects every part with screw in its name, in capitals or not. The pattern is a regular expression, a compact way to describe text, so some characters such as `.` and `*` have a special meaning."]]],["p","Two tools select for you in a cleverer way: [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts) and the size threshold of [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts)."],["h","Find what you selected"],["list",["Press [[Shift + S]] to scroll the tree to the selected part and open the groups it is in.","Press [[F]] to frame the selection in the viewport.","The status bar shows how many parts are selected. Click that number to frame them.","When none of the selection is in the picture, a faint arrow at the edge of the view points to the side it is on. Click the arrow to frame the selection. See [Move the view](#move-the-view).","{{Properties}} shows the triangle count of the selection and its share of the scene."]],["h","The right-click menu"],["p","Right-click a part, in the viewport or in the tree, for a menu of things to do with the selection: frame it, isolate it, select similar parts, copy, duplicate or delete."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-context-menu.webp","Right-click a part to frame, isolate, select similar, copy or delete."],["note","A common mistake: dragging without [[Ctrl]] turns the view instead of drawing a selection box. Hold [[Ctrl]] first, then drag."],["see",["hide-isolate","parts-tree","search-and-commands"]]]},{"slug":"hide-isolate","group":"around","title":"Hide, isolate, show all","summary":"Hide parts that are in the way, look at a few parts on their own, and bring everything back with one key.","blocks":[["p","Hiding is how you look inside a model. A hidden part is still in the scene. It is only not drawn."],["keys",[["H","Hide the selected parts"],["Shift + H","Hide the rest: everything that is not selected"],["Alt + H","Show all parts again"],["S","Isolate the selection. Press it again to show everything."]]],["h","Isolate"],["steps",["Select the parts you want to look at.","Press [[S]]. Everything else disappears.","A pill at the top of the viewport says so, for example “Isolated · 1 of 95 parts”.","Press [[S]] again, or click the pill, to show everything."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-isolate.webp","A set of parts on their own, isolated."],["h","In the parts tree"],["p","Every row has an eye at its right end. Click it to hide or show that part. The eye on a group hides or shows everything inside it."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["Hiding and showing are undo steps. [[Ctrl + Z]] takes one back.","{{Properties}} shows how many parts are hidden.","A saved scene remembers which parts were hidden.","When you export, the switch {{Visible parts only}} decides whether hidden parts are written to the file. See [Export](#export)."]],["note","Hiding a part does not make the file lighter. To remove a part for good, delete it with [[Del]]."],["see",["select-parts","view-modes","select-hidden-parts"]]]},{"slug":"parts-tree","group":"around","title":"The parts tree","summary":"Search, rename, group and reorder the parts of a model, rename many at once, and flatten a hierarchy that is deeper than you need.","blocks":[["p","The parts tree in the left sidebar lists everything in the scene. A part is one row. A group is a folder that holds parts or other groups. A STEP file usually arrives with the groups its designer made."],["h","Read a row"],["list",["A part row shows its name and its triangle count.","A group row shows how many parts it holds. A group with nothing in it says “empty”.","The eye at the right hides or shows the row.","Click the small box in front of a group to open or close it."]],["h","The three buttons"],["terms",[["Collapse","Closes every group. When every group is closed, the button says {{Expand}} and opens them all. The small arrow beside it, or a right-click on the button, opens a menu with more choices. See below."],["Rename","Renames many parts at once. [[F2]]."],["Flatten","Removes levels of grouping."]]],["h","Collapse and expand part of the tree"],["p","A big assembly has many groups. Sometimes you want to close only some of them. Click the arrow beside {{Collapse}} to choose. Select a part or a group in the tree first, for the choices that need a selection."],["terms",[["Collapse the whole tree","Closes every group. The same as pressing {{Collapse}}."],["Collapse the selection","Closes the groups you selected and every group inside them. For a selected part, it closes the group the part is in."],["Collapse from here on","Closes the first selected row and every group after it in the tree. The groups above stay as they are, so the row you are on stays in view."],["Expand the whole tree","Opens every group."],["Expand the selection","Opens the groups you selected and every group inside them. For a selected part, it opens every group on the way down to it."],["Expand from here on","Opens the first selected row and every group after it."]]],["p","The choices that need a selection are dimmed while nothing is selected."],["h","Find the selected part"],["p","Picked a part in the 3D view and want to see where it sits in the tree? Press [[Shift + S]]. The tree scrolls to the part, opens the groups it is in and flashes its row. A click on a part in the 3D view also scrolls the tree to it."],["h","A file you add to the scene"],["p","When you add a file to a scene that is already open ({{Import into scene…}}, or a dropped file with {{Add to this scene}}), the new parts are selected, shown in the tree and framed in the 3D view. That tells you where they landed."],["h","Search"],["p","Type in {{Search parts…}} above the tree. Only the rows whose names match stay, including matches inside closed groups. Press the small cross in the field to clear the search."],["p","The search reads more than a plain word. Case, accents and the marks `_`, `-` and `.` are ignored, and so are the joins inside a name such as GearBox, so `gearbox` finds Gear_Box and `gear box` finds GearBox. You can combine these:"],["terms",[["`gear box`","Several words: every word has to be in the name, in any order."],["`\"m6 bolt\"`","Words in quotes are matched together, as you wrote them."],["`bolt, nut`","A comma means or: names with either."],["`-washer`","A minus sign leaves out the names that have the word."],["`>5000`, `<200`","Parts with more or fewer triangles than the number. `5k` and `1.2m` work as numbers."],["`is:hidden`, `is:visible`, `is:selected`","Parts by state."]]],["p","When nothing at all is found, the search tries again more forgivingly. In a word of four letters or more, one letter may be wrong, missing, extra or swapped with its neighbour, and in a word of eight letters or more, two. So `braket` finds Bracket. Groups are matched by their names only, so the triangle and state marks do not apply to them."],["h","Rename one row"],["steps",["Double-click the name of a part or a group.","Type the new name.","Press [[Enter]]."]],["h","Rename many rows"],["p","CAD files often come with names like `Part_0031_geom`. Batch rename changes many names in one go."],["steps",["Select the parts to rename.","Press [[F2]] or the {{Rename}} button. The {{Batch rename}} window opens.","Choose a tab. {{Find & Replace}} swaps one piece of text for another. {{Pattern}} builds each name from pieces such as `{name}`, `{parent}` and `{counter:003}`. {{Presets}} has ready-made rules, such as lower-casing every name or removing a number at the end.","Look at the {{Preview}}. It shows every name before and after.","Press {{Apply}}, or [[Ctrl + Enter]]."]],["h","Group and ungroup"],["keys",[["Ctrl + G","Put the selected parts in a new group"],["Ctrl + Shift + G","Ungroup: remove the selected group and keep what was inside"],["Del","Delete the selected parts. With a group selected, the group and everything in it."]]],["p","A new group is called “Group” with a number. Double-click it to give it a name. Parts in a group move together when you move the group."],["h","Drag and drop"],["p","Drag a row onto a group to move it into that group, or between two rows to change the order. A group cannot be dropped into itself."],["h","Flatten"],["p","A STEP file can have groups inside groups ten levels deep. Flatten removes levels you do not need. Press {{Flatten}} to open the {{Advanced flatten}} window."],["terms",[["Scope","The whole tree, or only the groups you selected."],["Total flatten","Removes every group inside the scope. All parts end up side by side."],["Keep top N levels","Keeps the first levels of grouping and removes everything deeper."],["Flatten last level only","Removes only the deepest groups, the ones that hold nothing but parts."],["Collapse single-child chains","Removes groups that hold nothing but one other group. This is a common tidy-up for STEP files."],["Ungroup (dissolve scope itself)","Removes the selected groups and moves what they held one level up."],["Preserve user groups","Leaves alone the groups you made yourself."],["Remove empty groups left behind","Deletes groups that end up with nothing in them."]]],["h","Walk the tree with the keyboard"],["p","Click a row in the tree, and the arrow keys work in it until you click somewhere else. [[↑]] and [[↓]] go to the row above and below. Hold [[Shift]] to add rows to the selection one at a time. [[→]] opens a closed group, and on an open group steps to its first row. [[←]] closes an open group, and on a part or a closed group goes to the group that holds it. [[Home]] and [[End]] go to the first and the last row."],["h","The right-click menu"],["p","The menu opens where you clicked. Right-click a part row for frame, isolate, hide, select similar, group, merge, Split, Remove holes, lock, Smart fit, copy, duplicate and delete. Right-click a group row to select its parts, frame it, rename it, ungroup it or delete it with its contents."],["p","A locked part cannot be moved, and delete skips it. Use the same menu entry to unlock it."],["h","Undo"],["p","Every change to the tree is an undo step: renames, groups, drags, flatten and deletes. Press [[Ctrl + Z]]."],["note","By default an emptied group stays in the tree, marked “empty”, so you can fill it again. To have such groups removed automatically, switch on {{Delete groups when they become empty}} under {{Settings}}, {{General}}."],["see",["select-parts","undo-redo","cleanup"]]]},{"slug":"organise-the-tree","group":"around","title":"Organise the tree: groups, sorting and picking","summary":"Pick groups and parts together, take a group out of the tree without losing what is in it, flatten, sort and move groups.","blocks":[["p","The parts tree can be tidied from the right-click menu of a group, and groups and parts can be picked together. Everything here is one undo step."],["h","Pick groups and parts together"],["keys",[["Click","Picks this row alone. A group brings all its parts."],["Ctrl + Click","Adds the row to the selection, or takes it out again. A group goes in or out with all its parts."],["Shift + Click","Picks every row from the last one you clicked to this one, groups and parts alike. Only the rows you can see count: a closed group comes with its group, not row by row."],["Ctrl + Shift + Click","Adds that range to what is already picked."]]],["p","Control and Shift never start a drag, so a slip of the mouse cannot move rows when you only meant to pick them."],["h","The right-click menu of a group"],["terms",[["Ungroup","Dissolves this group; its children move up one level."],["Remove group, keep contents","Takes the group, or every selected group when it is one of them, out of the tree. What was inside stays as it was, nesting included, and moves up to where the group stood."],["Flatten this group","Keeps the group but dissolves every group inside it, so all its parts sit directly under it."],["Sort inside A to Z and Z to A","Orders what is directly inside the group by name. Numbers go by value (part 2 before part 10) and groups come first."],["Move group up and Move group down","Moves the group one place among the rows beside it, with its contents."],["Delete empty groups","Removes every group that holds no part."],["Expand this branch and Collapse this branch","Opens or closes this group and every group inside it."],["Delete group + contents","Deletes the group and everything in it. Ctrl + Z brings it back."]]],["note","Lines you draw are rows of the tree too, so they can be grouped, nested, sorted and moved in the same way."],["see",["parts-tree","select-parts","undo-redo","draw-lines"]]]},{"slug":"search-and-commands","group":"around","title":"Search and commands","summary":"Press Ctrl + K or Shift + C to find any command, part, library part or setting by name, turn a switch without leaving the list, and work out a sum or convert a length.","blocks":[["p","You do not have to remember where a button is. The search finds it. It is the fastest way to use the app once you know roughly what you want."],["h","Open the search"],["steps",["Press [[Ctrl + K]] or [[Shift + C]], or click the magnifying glass at the left of the bottom toolbar.","Start typing. The list below narrows as you type.","Use [[↑]] and [[↓]] to choose a line.","Press [[Enter]] to run it. Press [[Esc]] to close the search without doing anything."]],["h","What it finds"],["terms",[["Commands","Everything in the menus and on the toolbars, each with its shortcut (the one you have set, if you changed it). Before you type, the commands you ran lately come first, with suggestions that fit what is selected."],["Sidebar controls","Every button, slider, switch and field in the right sidebar, listed under the name of its card. Choosing a button runs it. Choosing anything else opens its card and scrolls to it."],["Parts","Parts of the scene by name. [[Enter]] selects the part and frames it, even when it is hidden. When several parts carry what you typed in their name, one row selects them all. Hidden parts are left out of that row."],["Library parts","Parts you can add. Type `add flange` or just `flange`, and [[Enter]] adds one."],["Settings","The rows of the Settings window, by name. Choosing one opens the window at the right section and takes your eye to the row."]]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-search.webp","Search for “bolt”: the parts in the scene by name, and the bolts in the library ready to add."],["p","Each kind of result has a colour of its own on its icon: commands by their group, parts, library parts, settings and answers."],["h","Turn a switch without leaving the search"],["p","When the search finds a switch, it draws the switch in the result. That goes for a setting, and for a switch in the sidebars or on a command card. Press [[Enter]], click the row or click the switch itself, and it turns on or off. The search stays open, so you can turn another one."],["p","Press [[Shift + Enter]] to go to the switch instead. The options of a tool stay ordinary results: choosing one takes you to it."],["h","You do not have to type it exactly"],["list",["The words can be in any order.","Another word for the same thing works: `remove` finds Delete, `bolts` finds Fasteners and `picture` finds Screenshot.","One slip of the finger is forgiven: a letter wrong, a letter missing, one too many, or two the wrong way round.","Two to five letters are read as initials too: `rh` finds Remove holes."]],["p","The better a command fits, the higher it stands in the list. Among commands that fit about as well, the ones you ran lately stand higher."],["h","Narrow it with a mark"],["p","Type one of these marks in front of the words, and the list holds one kind of result only."],["terms",[["`>`","Commands."],["`@`","Parts of the scene. `@bracket` lists parts and nothing else."],["`+`","Library parts."],["`#`","Settings."]]],["h","Move the search"],["p","The search does not have to stay in the middle of the window. Press and hold the magnifying glass at its left and drag, and the whole card follows. Drag its right edge to make it wider or narrower. It cannot be narrower than 320 pixels."],["p","The place and the width are remembered, also after you close the search and the next time you open the app. Double-click the magnifying glass to put it back in the middle at its own width."],["h","Commands that cannot run right now"],["p","A command that cannot run right now is greyed out, and says why: “Select a part first”, “Open a model first” or “Nothing to undo”. A command that works on the selection, or on every part when nothing is selected, needs only an open model."],["p","What the search looks through, and how loosely it reads what you type, can be changed under {{Settings}}, {{Search}}. See [Settings](#settings)."],["h","Commands worth knowing"],["p","Some tools have no button in the sidebar, or no key of their own. The search is the easy way to reach them. Type a few letters of the name:"],["terms",[["Smart optimise","Runs every clean-up in one go. See [Smart optimise](#smart-optimise)."],["Select fasteners…","Finds the bolts, screws, nuts and washers. See [Fasteners](#fasteners)."],["Find stacked copies…","Finds parts that sit on an identical copy of themselves. See [Stacked copies](#stacked-copies)."],["Select hidden parts…","Finds the parts that cannot be seen from outside. See [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts)."],["Align model to the floor…","Opens the card that stands the model on the floor. {{Align model to the floor (with the last choices)}} does it at once, without opening the card. See [Align to floor](#align-to-floor)."],["Align and spread parts…","Opens the Align parts card, which lines the selected parts up or spaces them evenly. See [Align parts](#align-parts)."],["Dynamic place","Turns on dragging the selected part over other surfaces ([[D]]). See [Move, rotate and scale](#move-rotate-scale)."],["Remove floating pieces…","Deletes the small loose pieces of a part. See [Remove floating pieces](#remove-floating-pieces)."],["Sweep…","Opens the Sweep card, which has no button on the toolbar. See [Sweep](#sweep)."],["Fit to triangle budget","Takes you to the budget field so you can type a number."],["Optimisation report","Shows the model as it was opened against what it is now."],["Duplicate scene, Close other scenes, Close all scenes","Work on the scene tabs. See [Scene tabs](#scene-tabs)."],["Revert to source file…","Throws away every edit and loads the file again."],["Help & docs","Opens the help window ([[F1]]). See [The help window](#help-window)."],["Open welcome screen","Shows the start screen again."]]],["h","A calculator"],["p","Type a sum or a length and the search works it out. [[Enter]] copies the answer, ready to paste into a field."],["terms",[["12*25.4","A sum."],["2 in","A length in inches, given in millimetres."],["50 mm in in","A length converted to the unit you name."],["3 thou","Thousandths of an inch, given in millimetres."]]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-search-sum.webp","The search works out a sum or a length. Enter copies the answer."],["h","The list of all commands"],["p","The `…` button at the right end of the bottom toolbar opens every command that acts on the model in one list, with its shortcut. A command that cannot run right now is dimmed. Hover over it to see why, for example because nothing is selected."],["h","Command cards"],["p","Some commands have settings: Split, Remove holes, Remove floating pieces, Remesh, Smart fit, Select hidden parts, Fasteners, Stacked copies, Align to floor, Align parts and Dynamic place. They open as a card over the bottom left of the viewport."],["keys",[["Enter","Run the open card"],["Esc","Put the card away. A second press clears the selection."]]],["p","A tool that has a key of its own, such as [[X]] for Split, closes its card when you press the key again."],["h","Numbers you can drag"],["p","Any number field in the app can be dragged sideways to change it, or clicked to type a value. Hold [[Shift]] while dragging to go ten times faster and [[Alt]] to go ten times finer."],["p","Right-click a number field for a menu of its own. It has Cut, Copy, Paste, Select all and Reset, which puts the field back to its own default. It also has a few quick edits of the number: a step up or down, doubled or halved, a round value, or the other sign. The value of a slider has the same menu. A name you are typing has a menu like it, with quick edits for names: the case, a suffix such as `_L` or `_01`, and a clean-up."],["see",["shortcuts","library","align-parts","the-window"]]]},{"slug":"quick-wand","group":"around","title":"Quick wand","summary":"Hold W and a ring of commands opens under the pointer. Flick toward one and let go.","blocks":[["p","The Quick wand puts the commands you use most under your hand, so you do not have to go to a button. Hold [[W]] with the pointer over the viewport and a ring opens around the pointer. Move toward a slice and let go of [[W]]. That runs it."],["p","Only the direction counts, not the distance, so a short flick is enough. The slices are always in the same places, so after a while your hand knows where Hide is without looking."],["h","Hold, flick, let go"],["steps",["Put the pointer over the viewport and hold [[W]]. The ring opens around the pointer.","Move the pointer toward a slice. It lights up.","Let go of [[W]]. The command runs."]],["p","To change your mind, let go with the pointer in the middle of the ring, or press [[Esc]]. Nothing happens."],["h","With something selected"],["p","The ring works on the selection, and has four slices."],["terms",[["Hide","Hides the selected parts."],["Reduce","Opens a fan of commands: Decimate −50 %, −25 %, −75 % and −90 % (each shows how many triangles there are before and after), Smart fit, Split and Remove holes."],["Delete","Deletes the selection. It is red, so it is hard to pick by mistake."],["More","Opens a fan of commands: Isolate, Select similar, Select same colour, Group, Duplicate, Merge, Frame, Hide others and Show all."]]],["h","With nothing selected"],["p","The ring works on the whole scene, and has six slices."],["terms",[["Fit view","Zooms to the whole model."],["Select all","Selects every part."],["View","Opens a fan of commands: Camera, Top, Front and Side views, Solid, Wireframe and X-ray, and the ground grid."],["Show all","Shows every hidden part."],["File","Opens a fan of commands: Save screenshot, Save scene and Revert to source file."],["Clean","Opens a fan of commands: Recentre on origin, Align to the floor, Align parts, Smart fit all parts, Remove empty parts, Deduplicate geometry and Fix degenerate parts."]]],["h","The fans"],["p","A slice that holds a group opens its fan as soon as the pointer is on it. Keep going outward onto a command and let go. You can head straight for a command: the ring remembers which group you were going for, so it does not turn into another slice on the way."],["p","The ring shows icons only. In a fan, the command under the pointer shows its name and its shortcut in a small label beside it. A command that cannot run right now stays on the ring, dimmed, and says why: “Nothing is hidden”, “Select two or more parts” or “Too few triangles to take away”."],["h","Tap instead of hold"],["p","A quick tap of [[W]] leaves the ring open. Click a slice or a command to run it. Press [[W]] or [[Esc]] to close it. The same thing is in the command palette: press [[Ctrl + K]] and type “wand”."],["h","It will not act by accident"],["list",["Esc, any other key, the scroll wheel, a click outside the ring, a window that loses focus and a resize all close it without running anything.","It does not open while a mouse button is down (when you are orbiting or dragging a gizmo), in a text field, behind a dialog, or when the pointer is not over the viewport.","The right-click menu does not open on top of it.","The ring stays inside the window, however close to the edge you are."]],["note","What a command does is what it does anywhere else, and the same Undo takes it back."],["see",["hide-isolate","select-parts","search-and-commands","shortcuts"]]]},{"slug":"undo-redo","group":"around","title":"Undo and redo","summary":"Every editing action can be taken back with Ctrl + Z. What counts as one step, and how to go back to the file as it was opened.","blocks":[["keys",[["Ctrl + Z","Undo the last action"],["Ctrl + Y","Redo: do it again"],["Ctrl + Shift + Z","Redo as well"]]],["p","There are no undo buttons on screen. Undo and redo are keyboard only."],["h","What can be undone"],["p","Every editing action: deleting, hiding and showing, moving, renaming, grouping, flattening, recolouring, and every mesh tool such as Remove holes, Decimate, Smart fit and Split."],["h","What counts as one step"],["list",["A tool that changes many parts at once is one step. One [[Ctrl + Z]] takes back a whole Remove holes run or a whole Fit to budget.","In Align to floor, a run of adjustments is one step.","Changing the threshold of Delete small parts while its parts are isolated adds no step of its own.","In [Edit mesh](#edit-mesh), a move, turn or scale of the picked pieces is one step, however long you drag, and so is each of Delete, Dissolve, Planarize, Straighten, Weld, Fill, Extract and Reverse normals. After an undo the pieces are picked again.","Quick remesh ([[B]]) is one step, taken when you let go on a level.","Each press of a button in [Align parts](#align-parts) is one step, and so is [Bake geo](#library) on a library shape ([[Ctrl + Z]] gives its parameters back).","A number typed into the Transform panel with several parts selected is one step for all of them."]],["p","In Edit mesh, [[Ctrl + Z]] also takes back the last change of what is picked (a click, a box or a stroke of the brush)."],["h","Each tab has its own history"],["p","Undo works on the scene in the tab you are looking at. Other tabs are not touched. See [Scene tabs](#scene-tabs)."],["h","Start over"],["p","To throw away every edit and reload the file as it was, choose {{Menu}}, then {{Revert to source…}}. The app asks before it does this."],["note","Your original file on disk is never changed by anything you do in the app. Edits reach a file only when you save a scene or export."],["note","After a change that is too large to keep an undo copy of, the app clears the history and tells you so. This is rare. Save a scene before a very heavy operation if you want a way back."],["see",["save-scene","scene-tabs","shortcuts","edit-mesh","align-parts"]]]},{"slug":"scene-tabs","group":"around","title":"Scene tabs","summary":"Each tab is a scene of its own. Open several files side by side, drop a file to add it or open it in a tab, drag tabs into order, and save from the tab’s dot.","blocks":[["p","A scene is everything you are working on in one tab: its parts, materials, camera and undo history. The tabs sit in the top bar, like tabs in a browser. Each one is a separate scene."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-tabs.webp","Scene tabs: drag them into order. The dot on the icon is yellow while there are unsaved changes."],["h","Open and close"],["list",["Click the `+` after the last tab, or press [[Alt + N]], for a new empty scene. A browser keeps [[Ctrl + N]] for itself: there it opens a new browser window. In the installed app, [[Ctrl + N]] works as well.","Opening a file never replaces a scene that has something in it. The file opens in a new tab.","Click a tab to switch to it. Nothing is reloaded.","Click the cross at the right end of a tab to close it. A click with the middle mouse button closes it too.","If the scene has changes that are not saved, the app asks before it closes the tab."]],["h","Drop a file on the window"],["p","You can drop a file anywhere on the window. What happens depends on the scene you have open."],["terms",[["The scene is empty","The file opens there."],["The scene has parts in it","A small question comes up: {{Add to this scene}} or {{Open in new tab}}. Add puts the parts of the file in the scene you are looking at, next to what is there. Open in new tab makes a scene of its own. Press [[Esc]] or click outside the question to do nothing."]]],["p","The {{Import into scene…}} command in the menu ([[Ctrl + Shift + O]]) always adds to the open scene. After an add, the new parts are selected, so you can see where they landed."],["h","Read a tab"],["terms",[["Dot","The first thing in every tab. Yellow means there are changes that are not saved. Green means the scene is saved. Faint means the scene is empty. Click the dot of the open tab to save the scene. It does the same as [[Ctrl + S]]."],["Name","The name of the scene. Double-click it to rename the scene. A long name is cut in the middle, so the end of it and the type of the file stay readable."],["Cross","Closes the tab."]]],["p","Every tab has the same width, and nothing in a tab moves when you pick it."],["h","Right-click a tab"],["p","A menu opens with the things you can do with tabs:"],["terms",[["Duplicate scene","Opens a copy of that tab’s scene in a new tab. It is dimmed when the scene is empty."],["Close all","Closes every tab. [[Ctrl + Shift + F4]]."],["Close others","Closes every tab except this one."],["Open…","Opens a file, as [[Ctrl + O]] does."],["Open recent","A list of the files you opened lately."]]],["p","Close all and Close others ask just once for all the scenes that have unsaved changes. Duplicate scene, Close all and Close others are in the search ([[Ctrl + K]]) too."],["h","Put tabs in order"],["steps",["Press a tab and pull it sideways.","The tab rides with the pointer and the others slide over to make room.","Let go. It drops into the nearest place."]],["p","Press [[Esc]] while you are still holding the tab to put it back where it was."],["h","Sidebars across tabs"],["p","When you switch tabs, the widths of the sidebars and the folded left sidebar stay as they were in the tab you came from. To let each tab keep its own layout, switch off {{Same sidebars in every tab}} under {{Settings}}, {{General}}."],["see",["save-scene","first-file","undo-redo"]]]},{"slug":"settings","group":"around","title":"Settings","summary":"One window with its sections and a search. What each section holds, in a sentence or two, from the start-up options and the search to the look of the interface.","blocks":[["p","Open it with [[Ctrl + ,]] or {{Menu}}, then {{Settings…}}. Changes apply as you make them. A click anywhere on a row with a switch turns the switch. There is nothing to confirm: press {{Done}} or [[Esc]] to close the window."],["p","The list at the left has the sections. The field at the top searches every section at once. Press [[Ctrl + F]] to jump to it. While a search is typed, [[Esc]] clears it first. At the bottom of the list are links to {{Help & docs}} and {{Keyboard shortcuts}}, and the version number."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-settings-general.webp","Settings, General."],["h","General"],["p","How the app starts and behaves."],["terms",[["Splash screen","The short splash with the version while the app opens. Off: the app just fades in."],["Start screen","Show the start screen when the app opens."],["Offer the last file","A Resume button on the start screen for the file you had open last."],["Fit the view after loading","Move the camera so a newly opened model fills the view."],["Same sidebars in every tab","Keep the sidebar widths when you switch tabs."],["Confirm destructive actions","Ask before deletes and other steps that are hard to take back."],["Warn before leaving with unsaved changes","Closing or reloading the window asks first when a scene has changes that are not saved. On by default."],["Undo history","How many steps [[Ctrl + Z]] can go back: {{Short}} (50), {{Normal}} (200) or {{Long}} (500). A longer history uses more memory. See [Undo and redo](#undo-redo)."],["Delete groups when they become empty","Remove a group automatically when an action empties it. Off by default."],["Keep a recovery copy","While a scene has changes that are not saved, a copy of it is kept inside this browser every minute, every 2 minutes (the default) or every 5 minutes, and only when something changed. If the app crashes, it offers the scene back. {{Off}} stops it. See [Get your scene back after a crash](#crash-recovery)."],["New scenes: Unit and Up axis","What a scene you start from now on shows sizes in (mm, cm, m, in, ft, or none), and which way is up (Z-up for CAD, Y-up for Maya, glTF and Unity). It applies to every new tab and every reload. The scene you have open keeps its own; change that one under {{Scene}}."]]],["h","Search"],["p","What the search ([[Ctrl + K]] or [[Shift + C]]) looks through, and how closely a name has to fit. The search itself is explained in [Search and commands](#search-and-commands)."],["terms",[["Sidebar controls","Every button, switch and field of the sidebars, besides the commands. Choosing one opens its card and takes you to it."],["Parts of the scene","Parts by name. [[Enter]] selects and frames one. When several carry what you typed in their name, one row selects them all."],["Library parts","Standard parts by name. [[Enter]] adds one to the scene."],["Sums and lengths","Type `12*25.4` or `2 in` and the answer is the first row. [[Enter]] copies it."],["Settings","The rows of this window, by name. Choosing one opens the window and takes you to its row."],["Switches in the results","A switch that is found is drawn in its row. Press it, or [[Enter]], to turn it on or off without leaving the search. [[Shift + Enter]] goes to it instead. Off: choosing the row goes to the switch."],["Other words for the same thing","`remove` also finds Delete, `bolts` finds Fasteners, `picture` finds Screenshot."],["Forgive a slip of the finger","One letter wrong, missing, one too many, or two the wrong way round still finds the command."],["Initials","`rh` finds Remove holes, `ss` finds Save scene."],["Coloured icons","Each kind of result has a colour of its own on its icon. Off: the icons are all grey."],["What you used lately comes first","Among commands that fit about as well, the ones you ran last stand higher."],["How many results","The most commands listed for what you typed: {{Few}}, {{Normal}} or {{Many}}."],["A mark in front narrows the search","A reminder of the four marks: `>` for commands, `@` for parts, `+` for library parts and `#` for settings."],["Recent commands","The commands you ran last lead the list while nothing is typed. {{Forget them}} empties that list."]]],["h","Appearance"],["p","The look of the interface. It does not change the model or the 3D view. {{Reset}} at the bottom puts everything in this section back to the defaults."],["terms",[["Accent","The colour of buttons, selections and highlights. Choose one of nine colours, or the last swatch to pick any colour. That swatch opens the app’s own colour picker. While you drag in it, the Settings window steps aside so you see the colour on the real interface, and [[Esc]] goes back to the accent you had."],["Surfaces","The greys the interface is built from: {{Graphite}}, {{Midnight}}, {{Warm}} or {{Black}}."],["Font","The typeface of the interface: {{Inter}}, {{System}}, {{Mono}} or {{Serif}}."],["Text size","Everything written in the interface, from {{Small}} to {{Larger}}. Buttons and fields grow with it."],["Corners","How round the corners of buttons and cards are: {{Sharp}}, {{Default}} or {{Round}}."],["Density","The height of buttons, fields and dropdowns: {{Compact}}, {{Default}} or {{Roomy}}."],["Interface animations","Fades, slides and the movement of the splash screen. Off: everything just appears."]]],["h","Viewport"],["p","What the 3D view draws around the model."],["terms",[["Highlight small parts","Tints the parts that are under the size threshold of Delete small parts."],["Arrow to a selection out of view","A faint arrow at the edge of the view points to the selection when none of it is on screen. Click the arrow to frame the selection. On by default."],["CAD view look","The look the CAD view (key [[6]]) shades with: {{Ceramic}}, {{Light}} or {{Mono}}. The same choice is in the {{Shading}} card on the right."],["Outlines","A fine dark line on the sharp edges of every part, in every view. On by default. The same switch is in the {{Shading}} card."],["Ground grid","The floor grid. [[G]] switches it too."],["Sun direction gizmo","A handle in the viewport for turning the light."],["FPS counter and Renderer stats","Small read-outs of how fast the view is drawing."],["Show origin points","Shows a dot at the origin of each group in the viewport. Off by default."],["Fog","Fades the grid and distant parts into the background, with start, end and intensity."],["Background","Dark, Studio gradient, Neutral grey, White, or HDRI environment. An HDRI is a picture of surroundings that lights the model. There are four built-in ones (Studio, Outdoor, Sunset, Overcast), or load your own `.hdr` or `.exr` file."]]],["h","Camera"],["p","Projection, field of view, clip planes, and how the view moves."],["terms",[["Projection","Perspective, as a camera sees, or Orthographic, with no perspective."],["Field of view","How wide the lens is, in perspective."],["Clip planes","How near and how far the camera sees. {{Auto}} fits the scene. {{Tight}} is for tiny parts, {{Wide}} for large assemblies and {{Huge}} for the size of a factory."],["Orbit around","What the view turns around when you drag. {{Selection}}: the selected parts, or the centre of the view when nothing is selected. {{Scene}}: always the centre of the view. {{Cursor}}: the point under the pointer when the drag starts. That is the surface the pointer is over, or, over empty space, a point at the depth the view was turning around."],["Zoom toward the cursor","The wheel zooms toward the pointer instead of the centre of the view."],["Orbit speed, Pan speed and Zoom speed","How far the view turns for a drag, slides for a pan drag (middle or right button) and moves for the wheel. Pan and zoom are {{Slow}}, {{Normal}} or {{Fast}}. Orbit has two more steps, {{Very slow}} and {{Very fast}}."],["Invert orbit direction","The view turns the other way for a drag: drag right and the model turns left."],["Stop at the horizon","The camera cannot go below the grid, so you cannot end up looking at a model from underneath by accident. Off: it turns freely all the way round."]]],["p","The mouse moves are explained in [Move the view](#move-the-view)."],["h","Performance"],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-settings-performance.webp","Settings, Performance: the renderer, quality, and what a moving view gives up."],["terms",[["Renderer","WebGPU, or WebGL2 where the browser or the graphics driver has no WebGPU. Changing it reloads the page."],["Quality","Auto adapts to the size of the scene. High always draws at full sharpness. Low draws coarser, for scenes with millions of triangles."],["Auto-instance duplicates","Draw repeated shapes as one mesh with many positions. It applies the next time a model is loaded."],["Share materials by colour","Let parts of the same colour share one material. It applies the next time a model is loaded."],["Skip tiny parts","While the view moves, leave out parts that would be only a few pixels across."],["Lower the resolution if it stutters","While a heavy scene moves, draw it a little coarser, and sharp again when it stops."]]],["h","Claude"],["p","The connection that lets Claude work with the open scene: the switch, what it may do, whether it asks first, and what it has asked for. It is explained in [Work with Claude](#work-with-claude). It is not in the online version."],["h","Updates"],["p","Look for a newer version, choose how the app tells you, and go back to the one before. It is explained in [Stop, restart and update](#stop-restart-update). It is not in the online version."],["h","Scene"],["p","These belong to the open scene and are saved with it. [[Ctrl + ;]] opens this section directly, and so does {{Scene settings…}} in the menu."],["terms",[["Display units","The unit used for sizes and distances on screen: millimetres, centimetres, metres, inches, feet, or none."],["Up axis (viewport)","Which direction is up: Z, as CAD programs have it, or Y, as glTF and some 3D programs have it. It changes how the scene is shown. The exported file follows the choice in the Export window."],["Scene scale (×)","A multiplier for the whole scene on screen. 0.001 turns millimetres into metres."],["Cell size","The size of a grid cell. {{Auto}} fits the model."],["Snap gizmo to grid","The move tool jumps from one grid cell to the next."]]],["p","The light has no settings here. The sun is turned with the {{Sun direction gizmo}} in the viewport."],["h","Storage"],["p","What the app remembers on this computer, each with a button to clear it:"],["terms",[["Recent files","The list on the start screen."],["File access","Permissions that let the app reopen a recent file without asking where it is."],["Import options","Shows the options window again for the formats where you ticked “Don’t ask again”."],["Panel sizes","The widths of the sidebars and the heights of the drawers."],["Recovery copies","The copies kept of scenes with changes that are not saved. Delete them and a crash can no longer be recovered from until the next change."],["All settings","Puts every preference back to its default, the look of the interface included."]]],["h","About"],["p","The version, where to find help, and what to send when something goes wrong."],["terms",[["Details","The version of the app, the renderer, how many parts and triangles the scene has, the size of your screen and your browser."],["Help & docs, Keyboard shortcuts","Open the help window and the list of shortcuts. See [The help window](#help-window)."],["Copy details","Copies the details above, with your graphics card and the look you chose, as text. Paste it into a new issue on GitHub so the problem is easier to find."],["Report a problem","Opens the report page in your browser, with your version and system already filled in."],["Website, Source code","The downloads and docs, and the code on GitHub. The app is free and open source, under the MIT licence."]]],["see",["move-the-view","view-modes","privacy-offline","help-window"]]]},{"slug":"help-window","group":"around","title":"The help window","summary":"These articles also live inside the app. Press F1 to open them, with a search, and read them with no internet connection.","blocks":[["p","You do not have to leave the app to find an answer. The help window holds the same articles as this website. Its text is stored inside the app, so it opens even when you are offline."],["h","Open it"],["list",["Press [[F1]].","Or choose {{Menu}}, then {{Help & docs}}.","Or click the app’s mark at the far left of the top bar, then {{Help & docs}}.","Or open {{Settings}} and click {{Help & docs}} in the list at the left.","On the start screen there is a {{Help}} link at the top."]],["p","The window opens on the article you read last. The first time, it opens on the first one. The command {{Help & docs}} is in the search ([[Ctrl + K]]) too."],["h","Find your way"],["terms",[["The list at the left","Every article, in groups. Click one to read it."],["The article","At the right. At its end, {{Previous}} and {{Next}} take you to the neighbours. A link inside an article opens the other article in the same window. A {{Copy}} button next to a command copies it."],["The search field","At the top. Type a word or two. The list changes into the articles that contain them, best match first, with a few words around the hit. [[Enter]] opens the first one. The words are marked in the article."],["Website","A button at the top right. It opens the same pages on the website, which is where the pictures live."]]],["keys",[["F1","Open the help window"],["/","Jump to the search field, while you are not typing"],["Enter","In the search field: open the first result"],["Esc","Clear the search. Press it again to close the window"]]],["h","Good to know"],["list",["The pictures are loaded from the website. Without an internet connection the text is all there, and the pictures are left out.","The text is a copy made when your version of the app was built. The website can be newer.","The help window does not change anything in your scene. Close it and you are back where you were."]],["see",["the-window","search-and-commands","shortcuts","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"read-the-numbers","group":"lighter","title":"How to read the numbers","summary":"What a triangle count tells you, where the app shows it, how to find the heaviest parts, and how to see what your work has saved.","blocks":[["p","A 3D model on a screen is made of triangles. Every triangle has to be stored in the file and drawn many times a second. So the triangle count is the simplest measure of how heavy a model is: fewer triangles mean a smaller file and a smoother view."],["p","There is no single right number. It depends on where the model will be shown. Ask whoever will use the file what they need, then work towards that."],["h","The whole scene"],["p","With nothing selected, {{Properties}} in the right sidebar describes the scene:"],["terms",[["The large number","Triangles in the scene (“tris”)."],["% of original","How many triangles are left compared with the file as it was opened. It starts at 100%."],["per part","The average number of triangles in a part."],["Parts","How many parts there are."],["Vertices","How many corner points the triangles have."],["Size","How large the whole model is along its three axes, in the display unit."],["Mesh data","How much memory the triangles take. A shape that repeats is counted once."]]],["p","The status bar under the viewport shows the number of vertices, the size of the mesh data, how many parts are selected and flagged, the frame rate and the console."],["h","One part"],["p","Select a part and {{Properties}} describes it instead: its name, its material, its triangle count, its share of the scene, and where it ranks by weight, for example “#28 of 67 by weight”. Below that are its vertices, size and volume."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-inspect.webp","One part selected: its triangles, its share of the scene and its rank by weight."],["p","Every part remembers the triangle count it arrived with. After you have reduced a part, the card leads with what was saved, in the form “21,688 triangles −34% · was 32,728 · saved 11,040”."],["h","What an action saved"],["p","Right after an action that removes triangles, the saving shows beside the scene’s triangle count for a few seconds, for example “−427,543 (−38%)”."],["h","Find the heavy parts"],["steps",["Open the card {{Heavy parts}} in the right sidebar.","It lists the parts with the most triangles, heaviest first, each with a bar that shows how it compares with the heaviest.","Click a row to tick it: the part is selected in the viewport. Right-click a row to move the view to the part. The card {{Heavy parts}} lists the parts with more than 10,000 triangles.","Decide what to do with it: delete it, simplify it, or leave it."]],["p","A few parts often hold a large share of the triangles. Start with those."],["h","Heatmap"],["p","Press [[Alt + 4]] for the heatmap view. Each part is coloured by how densely it is covered in triangles for its size. Dense parts stand out in warm colours. A small part in a hot colour carries far more detail than it needs. Press [[1]] to go back to the normal view."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-heatmap.webp","Heatmap: parts coloured by how dense their triangles are."],["h","The optimisation report"],["p","The report puts the scene as it was opened beside the scene as it is now. It is the number to show whoever asked for a lighter file."],["steps",["Open the card {{Reduce triangles}} and press {{Optimisation report}}. The search ([[Ctrl + K]]) finds it too.","Read the table: {{Original}}, {{Now}} and {{Change}} for triangles, vertices, parts, draw calls, materials and mesh data in memory.","The row {{File}} fills in after you have exported.","Press {{Copy}} to copy the table as text."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-report.webp","The optimisation report: the scene as it was opened beside the scene now."],["see",["workflow","delete-small-parts","fit-to-budget","view-modes"]]]},{"slug":"heavy-parts","group":"lighter","title":"Heavy parts","summary":"A list of the parts that hold the most triangles, heaviest first, with a count beside its title. Tick the ones you do not need and delete them in one step.","blocks":[["p","A few parts often hold a large share of a model’s triangles. The card {{Heavy parts}} lists them, so you can see where the weight is without searching the tree."],["h","What the card lists"],["p","Only parts with more than 10,000 triangles are listed. The heaviest is at the top, and the list holds at most 500 parts. Each row shows the part’s place in the list, its name, its triangle count (for example 12.4k) and a bar that compares it with the heaviest part. Hover over a row to see the full name, the exact count and the rank."],["p","The card is in the right sidebar, under Inspect. Beside its title is a small number: how many parts are over the limit. You can read it while the card is folded. It goes down as you delete or reduce parts, and it reads 0, dimmed, when no part is that big."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card {{Heavy parts}} in the right sidebar, under Inspect.","Click a row to tick it. The part is selected in the viewport. Click the row again to take the tick off. Rows you ticked before stay ticked.","To tick a run of rows, click the first one, then hold [[Shift]] and click the last.","To see where a part is, right-click its row. The view moves to the part. The ticks stay as they are.","Press the button under the list. It says what it will remove, for example “Delete 3 parts · 171.3k triangles”. It cannot be pressed until a row is ticked.","If you removed too much, press [[Ctrl + Z]]. The whole delete is one undo step."]],["note","A tick is the same thing as selecting the part. A part that you selected in the viewport or in the tree is ticked in the list too, if it is listed. The button deletes only the ticked rows, never a selected part that is not on the list."],["h","What to do with a heavy part"],["list",["Delete it, if nobody will miss it. Deleting costs no quality.","Reduce it with [Decimate](#decimate), if it matters and is simply too dense.","Replace it with a simple stand-in using [Smart fit](#smart-fit), if only its size and place matter.","Leave it, if it is the part everybody will look at."]],["h","Good to know"],["list",["The list follows the scene. After a delete, an undo or a decimate it updates a moment later.","The limit of 10,000 triangles is fixed. A scene made of many lighter parts can be heavy without one part in the list. To bring a whole scene down to a number, use [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget) or [Smart optimise](#smart-optimise).","For a picture of where the density is, press [[Alt + 4]] for the heatmap. See [How to read the numbers](#read-the-numbers)."]],["see",["read-the-numbers","decimate","smart-fit","fit-to-budget"]]]},{"slug":"smart-optimise","group":"lighter","title":"Smart optimise","summary":"One card, one button: every clean-up the app has, run one after another on the whole scene. Choose how far to go, read the plan, press Optimise.","blocks":[["p","The other tools in this group each do one job. Smart optimise runs them for you, one after another, on the whole scene. Use it when you want a lighter model and do not want to choose the order yourself."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-smart-optimise.webp","Smart optimise at the top of Reduce, with Reduce triangles on and a target of 323,000. The line under the controls says what the run will do."],["p","It is lossy: it removes parts and triangles. The whole run is one undo step, so one [[Ctrl + Z]] takes everything back."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open a model.","Open the card {{Smart optimise}} in the right sidebar, at the top of Reduce.","Choose how far to go: {{Auto}}, {{Light}}, {{Balanced}} or {{Strong}}. Auto is the default.","Look at {{Triangle target}}. Type another number if you need one, for example `300k` or `1.5m`.","Read the line under the controls. It spells out what the run will do, in order. With Auto it also says which level was chosen, for example “Auto picked Balanced for 1,103,373 triangles in 43 parts”.","Press {{Optimise}}.","When it is done, a message gives the triangles before and after and how many parts were removed.","Look at the model. If it lost too much, press [[Ctrl + Z]] once and try a lighter level."]],["p","You can also run it from the search: press [[Ctrl + K]] and type `Smart optimise`. It runs with whatever the card is set to."],["h","The levels"],["p","A level is a starting point. It sets the switches under {{Steps}} and the triangle target."],["terms",[["Auto","Chooses from how heavy the scene is: under 200,000 triangles it uses Light, under 2 million it uses Balanced, and above that Strong. The line under the controls says what it chose."],["Light","Only tidies. It removes empty parts, parts whose triangles have no area, and empty groups. No triangles are reduced."],["Balanced","Also removes parts hidden inside the model, turns bolts, nuts and washers into plain cylinders, removes parts smaller than 1% of the model, and reduces the scene to half its triangles."],["Strong","Like Balanced, but it removes parts smaller than 2%, also closes holes in flat faces, and reduces the scene to a quarter of its triangles."]]],["h","The steps"],["p","Open {{Steps}} in the card to see each switch. If you change one, no level is lit any more: the settings are yours. Each step is the same tool you could run by hand."],["terms",[["Remove empty and broken parts","Parts with no triangles, parts whose triangles have no area, and groups with nothing left in them. See [Quick fixes and tidying up](#cleanup)."],["Remove parts hidden inside","Parts that cannot be seen from outside, from any direction. See [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts)."],["Bolts, nuts, washers","Found by their shape, up to a thread of M30, as in [Fasteners](#fasteners). {{Keep}} leaves them alone. {{Simplify}} turns each into a plain cylinder. {{Delete}} removes them."],["Remove parts under","Parts whose box, measured from corner to corner, is shorter than this share of the whole model. 0 leaves small parts alone. See [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts)."],["Remove holes","Closes holes in flat faces, with the settings and the size limit of the Remove holes panel. See [Remove holes](#fill-holes)."],["Reduce triangles","A switch of its own. On: the densest meshes are reduced until the whole scene has the number of triangles in {{Triangle target}}, as in [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget). Off: the run only removes and tidies, and every mesh that stays keeps all its triangles. If the target is not lower than the scene has now, nothing is reduced."],["Triangle target","How many triangles the whole scene may have after the run. You can write `150000`, `150k` or `1.5m`. Choosing a level sets it from the scene you have now. A number you type is kept."]]],["p","The steps always run in this order: tidy up, hidden parts, fasteners, small parts, holes, then the reduction. Removing comes before reducing on purpose, so no effort goes into thinning a part that is deleted a moment later."],["h","While it runs"],["p","A progress card names the step that is running. {{Cancel}} stops the run before the next step and keeps what has already been done. Each step writes a line to the Console (the button at the bottom of the window), and one summary is shown at the end."],["h","Undo"],["p","The whole run is one undo step. [[Ctrl + Z]] takes all of it back, and redo does all of it again."],["note","Start with Light or Balanced and look at the result. Undo is one key press, so you can try Strong afterwards at no cost. Do not use the removing steps when someone will open a cover, look through a window in the casing, or need the fasteners. Switch those steps off, or use the single tools one at a time."],["see",["workflow","fasteners","select-hidden-parts","delete-small-parts","fill-holes","fit-to-budget"]]]},{"slug":"delete-small-parts","group":"lighter","title":"Delete small parts","summary":"Remove the screws, washers and clips that are too small to matter, with a slider and a preview of what will go.","blocks":[["p","A CAD assembly is mostly small things: screws, washers, clips, nuts. From a normal viewing distance nobody sees them, but there are hundreds of them and each one costs triangles. This card removes every part below a size you choose."],["h","When to use it"],["p","Early, when the model is for looking at as a whole and the small hardware does not matter. Do not use it when the fasteners are the point of the picture."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card {{Delete small parts}} in the right sidebar, under Clean up.","Drag the slider {{Smaller than}}. The value is a share of the whole model’s size. It starts at 2%.","Read the line under it. It says how many parts would go and how long that is, for example “710 parts under 70.5 mm corner to corner”.","Press {{Isolate}}. Only the parts that would be deleted are shown, so you can check them.","Drag the slider again if needed. While the small parts are isolated, the view follows the slider.","Press {{Delete}}. The button says how many small parts it will remove. When no part is under the threshold, the button is off.","Press {{Isolate}} again to see the rest of the model again. The button stays lit while the small parts are all that shows."]],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Smaller than","The threshold, in percent of the model’s size. A part below it counts as small."],["Measured by: Diagonal","The distance from corner to corner of the part’s bounding box. The usual choice."],["Measured by: Longest side","The part’s longest side. It keeps long thin parts such as rods and pipes, and is stricter with flat ones."],["Measured by: Volume","The volume of the part’s bounding box. It catches thin sheets and wires however long they are."]]],["h","Hidden parts are left alone"],["p","Only parts that are on show are looked at: the ones with an open eye in the parts tree. A part you hid is not counted, not isolated and not deleted. If there are hidden parts, the line under the slider says how many it skipped, for example “3 hidden parts are left alone”. Hiding or showing a part updates the count at once. While the small parts are isolated, the card goes on choosing from the parts that were on show before you pressed {{Isolate}}."],["h","See the small parts without deleting them"],["p","The {{Highlight}} button on the card tints every part under the threshold, in the view and in the parts tree, so you can see what would go. The same switch is {{Highlight small parts}} under {{Settings}}, {{Viewport}}."],["h","Undo"],["p","The delete is one undo step. Press [[Ctrl + Z]] and the parts are back."],["note","A common mistake is a threshold that is too high. At a few percent, handles, knobs, feet and hinges count as small too. Always press {{Isolate}} and look before you delete."],["p","To remove only the hardware, and not every small part, use [Fasteners](#fasteners). [Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) has this step too, under the name {{Remove parts under}}."],["see",["select-hidden-parts","fasteners","smart-optimise","read-the-numbers","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"select-hidden-parts","group":"lighter","title":"Select hidden parts","summary":"Find every part that cannot be seen from outside the model, such as screws inside a housing, and delete them in one step.","blocks":[["p","Much of an assembly is inside it: the screws in a housing, the board under a cover, a bearing in its seat. If the model is only ever seen from outside, those parts are dead weight. This command looks at the model from every side and selects the parts that never show."],["p","On the gearbox used in these pictures it finds nothing, because every part can be seen from outside, and the card says so. On an assembly with a housing it finds what is inside it. On a bigger assembly there are usually many more."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-select-hidden-result.webp","The card on the gearbox: every part can be seen from outside, so nothing is selected."],["h","When to use it"],["p","When the model will be shown closed, from outside. Not when someone will open a cover, look through a window in the casing, or show an exploded view."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Click the {{Select hidden parts}} button on the bottom toolbar. You can also find it in the `…` list or with the search. Its panel opens at the bottom left of the viewport.","Leave {{Whole scene}} chosen.","Press {{Select hidden parts}}, or [[Enter]].","The hidden parts are selected. {{Properties}} shows how many triangles they hold.","Press {{Isolate}} in the panel to look at them on their own. Check that nothing you need is among them.","Press {{Delete}} in the panel to remove them."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-select-hidden.webp","Select hidden parts: the command panel beside the viewport."],["note","The command only selects. Nothing is deleted until you press {{Delete}}."],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Whole scene / Selection only","Check every visible part, or only the parts you selected. With Selection only, the rest of the scene still counts as something that hides them."],["Accuracy","Under {{Advanced}}. How many directions the scene is looked at from: Fast, Normal or Thorough. More directions find parts that show only through a narrow gap, and take longer."],["See-through parts hide what is behind","Off: a see-through part, such as glass or a clear cover, hides nothing, so what is behind it counts as visible. On: it hides what is behind it like any other part."],["Add to the selection","Keeps what is selected now and adds the hidden parts to it."],["Largest part, of model size","Keeps only hidden parts up to this share of the model’s size. Empty means any size."],["Name contains","Keeps only hidden parts whose name contains this text, such as `screw` or `bearing`. Empty means any name."]]],["h","Undo"],["p","Selecting changes nothing. The delete is one undo step: [[Ctrl + Z]]."],["h","Tips"],["list",["Show all parts first ([[Alt + H]]). The check looks at visible parts.","If a part that you can see through a small opening was selected, run it again with {{Accuracy}} on Thorough.","To remove only the hidden fasteners and keep hidden structure, type part of their name in {{Name contains}}, or set a small value in {{Largest part, of model size}}."]],["p","[Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) runs this command as one of its steps, {{Remove parts hidden inside}}, with its usual settings."],["see",["delete-small-parts","fasteners","smart-optimise","hide-isolate","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"fasteners","group":"lighter","title":"Fasteners","summary":"Find every bolt, screw, nut and washer by its shape, whatever the parts are called, then isolate or delete them in one step.","blocks":[["p","A machine can hold hundreds of fasteners: the small metal parts that hold other parts together. In a CAD export their names are often useless, for example `=>0_1_1_9_000009`, so searching by name does not work. Fasteners looks at the shape of every part instead, and selects the ones shaped like a bolt, a screw, a nut or a washer."],["p","It cuts each part across its own axis and reads the outline: a round shank with a wider head, six flats around a hole, a flat ring. It compares that with the proportions of standard fasteners. Bushings, bearings, O-rings, shafts, pipe fittings and cable glands have other shapes, and are left alone."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-fasteners.webp","Fasteners found 48 by their shape on the gearbox: 18 bolts and screws, 20 nuts and 10 washers, selected and ready to isolate or delete."],["h","When to use it"],["p","Before you delete or simplify the hardware, when the names do not help. Not when the fasteners are the point of the picture."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Click the {{Fasteners}} button (a bolt) on the bottom toolbar. You can also find it in the `…` list, or with the search ([[Ctrl + K]], “Select fasteners”). Its panel opens at the bottom left of the viewport.","Set {{Thread size up to}} to the largest thread to look for. It starts at 30. A thread size is written M and the width in millimetres: M6 is a bolt 6 mm wide. A bolt, nut or washer for a bigger thread is left alone.","Leave the three switches on: {{Bolts and screws}}, {{Nuts}} and {{Washers}}. Switch off a kind you do not want.","Press {{Select fasteners}}, or [[Enter]]. It looks at every visible part. On a large assembly it shows its progress and takes a few seconds.","The fasteners are selected. A line says how many, how many triangles they hold and what share of the scene that is, for example “48 fasteners selected: 22,760 triangles, 3.5% of the scene”.","Under it is a short list with one row per kind and the sizes found, for example “12 bolts and screws · M3.5 ×6 · M4 ×4 · M5 ×2”. Click a row to select only that kind. Click it again to go back to all of them.","Press {{Isolate}} to look at them on their own. Check that nothing you need is among them.","Press {{Delete}} to remove them, or leave them selected and use another tool on them, such as [Smart fit](#smart-fit)."]],["note","The command only selects. Nothing is deleted until you press {{Delete}}."],["h","What the options mean"],["p","Open {{Advanced}} in the panel."],["terms",[["Look in: Whole scene / Selection","Check every visible part, or only the parts you selected first."],["How sure: Certain","Only parts with a clear fastener shape: a hex head, a key socket, a hex nut, a washer of standard proportions."],["How sure: Likely too","The default. Also plain two-cylinder screws and headed pins, which have the proportions of a fastener and nothing else to show for it."],["Pins, studs and threaded rods","Plain round bars up to the size limit. Off by default, because a shaft or a roller is a round bar too."],["Add to the selection","Keeps what is selected now and adds the fasteners to it."],["Used at least … times","Takes a fastener only if the scene holds at least this many of the same shape and size. Fasteners come in sets. 1 takes single ones too."],["Smallest thread","Leaves the fasteners for threads below this alone. 0 means no lower limit."],["Reset to defaults","Puts every option back."]]],["h","Sizes and units"],["p","The app works in millimetres. A file saved in metres, centimetres or inches would show its fasteners at impossible sizes. The app checks which unit gives the sizes of real fasteners and reads the file that way. When it had to, the line under the button says so, for example “Sizes read as inches”."],["h","Simplify instead of delete"],["p","To keep a stand-in where each fastener was, leave them selected and run [Smart fit](#smart-fit) with {{Cylinder}}. [Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) does the same with its {{Simplify}} choice."],["h","Undo"],["p","Selecting changes nothing. The delete is one undo step: [[Ctrl + Z]]."],["h","Tips"],["list",["Show all parts first ([[Alt + H]]). Only visible parts are looked at.","If a part that is not a fastener was selected, press {{Certain}} under {{How sure}} and run it again.","If a fastener was missed, raise {{Thread size up to}}, or switch on {{Pins, studs and threaded rods}}."]],["see",["select-hidden-parts","delete-small-parts","smart-fit","smart-optimise"]]]},{"slug":"stacked-copies","group":"lighter","title":"Stacked copies","summary":"Find parts that sit exactly on top of an identical copy of themselves, drawn twice, and remove the extra ones.","blocks":[["p","Some files hold the same part twice, in the same place. From a distance nothing looks wrong. But the two surfaces lie on each other, so the screen flickers where they meet, because the graphics card cannot tell which one is in front. And the triangles are stored and drawn twice. Stacked copies finds these parts and keeps one of each."],["p","Ten identical bolts in ten different places are not stacked copies. Only parts in the same place count."],["h","Open the card"],["p","Press [[Ctrl + K]] to open the search, type `stacked`, and choose {{Find stacked copies…}}. The card opens over the viewport, at the bottom left, like [Split](#split) and [Remove holes](#fill-holes). [[Esc]] puts it away."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card {{Stacked copies}} as above.","Set {{Same place within}}. The slider goes from 0 to 5 percent of the part’s size, and starts at 0.5. At 0 the parts must lie almost exactly on each other. A higher value also accepts copies that are a little off.","Switch on {{Also near copies}} if the copies may differ a little. Off, two parts count as copies only when they have the same number of triangles. On, the number may differ by up to a quarter, as with another export of the same part, or a copy that was rebuilt with a different mesh.","Press {{Find stacked copies}}.","The extra copies are selected, and a line says how many, for example “6 extra copies of 6 parts selected; the first of each is kept”. The part that was loaded first stays. If there are none, it says “No stacked copies found”.","Press {{Isolate}} to look at the extra copies on their own. Check them.","Press {{Delete}} to remove them."]],["note","The command only selects. Nothing is deleted until you press {{Delete}}."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["Hidden parts are not looked at. Show all parts first ([[Alt + H]]).","The card remembers {{Same place within}} and {{Also near copies}} for next time.","Faces that lie exactly on each other still flicker in the viewport. This card is the way to find the parts that cause it."]],["h","Undo"],["p","Selecting changes nothing. The delete is one undo step: [[Ctrl + Z]] brings the copies back."],["see",["select-hidden-parts","cleanup","delete-small-parts","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"fill-holes","group":"lighter","title":"Remove holes","summary":"Close bolt holes, slots, pockets and lettering in flat faces. Each closed hole removes the triangles of its walls.","blocks":[["p","A plate with forty bolt holes carries forty small cylinders of triangles that nobody notices from a distance. Remove holes closes holes in flat faces and leaves the rest of each part exactly as it was. The part keeps its outline and loses the triangles inside the holes."],["p","With the limit at 12 mm it closes bolt holes, slots and pockets up to that size, and leaves bosses and the bore of a washer or a nut alone."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-fill-holes.webp","Remove holes: closes bolt holes, slots and pockets in flat faces and leaves the rest alone."],["h","What it closes, and what it leaves"],["list",["Closed: holes that go right through, holes with a floor, counterbores, slots, pockets and engraved lettering in flat faces.","Countersinks and flared holes: the hole counts by its bore. The wide opening of a countersink may be up to five times the limit and is closed with the hole.","An opening that leads into a hollow space: with {{Close openings into hollow spaces}} on, as it is to begin with, it is closed flush with the face from outside. What lies behind it stays as it was, out of sight. With the switch off, it is left alone.","Loose walls: a broken file can leave a cut-loose tube or cup floating where a hole was. With {{Remove vertices left over}} on, it is taken out and the opening it sat in is closed. Only a piece that is small next to its part goes, never more than a third of the part.","Left alone: a boss or a pin that stands up from a face.","Left alone: an opening that is what the part is, such as the bore of a washer, a nut or a bushing.","Left alone: an opening that leads out through a curved surface.","Left alone: holes in curved surfaces. The tool works on flat faces only.","Left alone: instanced parts, which the file stores as repeats of one shared shape. The result line says how many it passed over."]],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the parts to work on. With nothing selected, it works on every visible part.","Press [[P]], or click {{Remove holes}} on the bottom toolbar. The panel opens at the bottom left of the viewport.","Set {{Hole width up to}} to the largest hole you want closed, in millimetres, measured across the hole’s widest point. It starts at 12.","Press {{Remove holes}}, or [[Enter]].","The holes close. The app reports how many, and the saving shows beside the triangle count."]],["h","What the advanced options mean"],["p","Open {{Advanced}} in the panel."],["terms",[["Through holes","Holes that go right through: an opening on both sides."],["Blind holes and pockets","Holes and pockets with a floor: an opening on one side only."],["Open holes","Openings with nothing behind them: a missing patch in a flat face."],["Close openings into hollow spaces","An opening that leads into a hollow space cannot be taken out whole. On: it is closed flush with the face from outside, and the wall of the hole under it is removed. What lies behind stays as it was, out of sight. Off: such an opening is left alone. It is on to begin with."],["Raised lettering and logos","Lettering and marks that stand proud of a flat face are flattened into it. Bosses, pins and ribs are taller than the limit and stay."],["Raised, up to","A raised detail is flattened only if it stands no higher than this above its face. Embossed type is usually 0.1 to 0.5 mm high."],["Keep openings that shape the part","On: an opening that takes up much of its face and of the part is kept, such as the bore of a washer or a window in a frame. Off: only the size limit decides."],["Smallest hole","Holes narrower than this are left alone."],["Remove vertices left over","On: the points that ringed each hole are taken out of the flat face, and the face is triangulated again with the fewest triangles. Press {{Remove holes}} a second time to clean the same way what an earlier run, or an earlier tool, left behind, even when no hole is left to close. It also works on parts that are already in n-gons. Loose walls that a broken file left floating in an opening, a cut-loose tube or cup that belongs to no solid, go with it."],["Depth limit","How far the inside of a hole may reach, as a multiple of the size limit. Lower it to leave deep bores alone."],["Flatness","How far apart the directions of two triangles may be and still count as one flat face. Raise it for meshes whose flat faces are slightly bent."],["Surface may move","After a hole is closed, a vertex of the flat face round it may be a little off the plane of that face, as on a gently curved panel. It may still be removed if the surface moves no more than this, and never more than 1% of the part’s size. It starts at 0.5 mm. With 0, only vertices exactly in the plane go."],["Reset to defaults","Puts every option back."]]],["h","Undo"],["p","A whole run is one undo step. Press [[Ctrl + Z]] and every hole is back."],["h","Tips"],["list",["Start with a small limit, look at the result, and raise it. A limit that is too high closes openings you wanted, such as a cable cut-out.","The filled result is what gets saved and exported.","A hole that was formed by pushing through the sheet, with a collar on the other side, is closed, and its collar stays as a small stub.","Points of a closed hole still show in the Lines view? Press {{Remove holes}} again: with {{Remove vertices left over}} on, it removes them.","A hole whose wall is wound the wrong way round, or whose edge is doubled, is found and closed too, instead of being closed twice.","Two holes that touch at a corner, or a hole that touches the edge of its face, are closed as well.","While it runs, Remove holes shows in the Tasks list of the status bar.","Small loose pieces left next to a part are a job for [Remove floating pieces](#remove-floating-pieces). To turn the closed faces into clean polygons, or back into triangles, see [Remesh](#remesh)."]],["p","[Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) can run this command as one of its steps, with the settings and the size limit of this panel."],["see",["remove-floating-pieces","remesh","smart-fit","decimate","smart-optimise","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"remove-floating-pieces","group":"lighter","title":"Remove floating pieces","summary":"Delete the small loose pieces of a part that touch nothing: a plug, a shaving, a dot of a thread. The biggest piece of each part always stays.","blocks":[["p","A part is usually one piece: all its triangles hang together. A CAD export, or a hole that was closed, can leave small loose pieces next to it that belong to the same part. The plug a hole was cut with, a shaving, a dot of a thread. These floating pieces touch nothing. Nobody sees them, and they still cost triangles. Remove floating pieces deletes them."],["h","When to use it"],["p","After you open a CAD file, or after [Remove holes](#fill-holes), when a part still has specks beside it or inside a hole that was closed. It is a clean-up, so run it before you reduce triangles."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the parts to work on. With nothing selected, it works on every visible part.","Open the card {{Quick fixes}} in the right sidebar and press {{Remove floating pieces…}}. Or press [[Ctrl + K]] and type `floating`. The panel opens at the bottom left of the viewport.","Set {{Piece size up to}} to the longest loose piece you want removed, in millimetres. It starts at 5.","Press {{Remove}}, or [[Enter]].","A message says how many floating pieces were removed, from how many parts, and how many triangles that took away."]],["h","The two fields"],["terms",[["Piece size up to","A loose piece is removed if its longest side is no more than this. 5 mm to begin with."],["Keep if over","A loose piece with more triangles than this is kept, however small it is. It starts at 0, which switches it off: the number of triangles does not matter."]]],["h","What always stays"],["list",["The biggest piece of each part, whatever its size. A part is never emptied.","Every loose piece that is longer than the limit.","Every loose piece that has more triangles than {{Keep if over}} allows, when that field is above 0."]],["h","Undo"],["p","A whole run is one undo step. Press [[Ctrl + Z]] and every piece is back."],["note","This tool looks inside each part. A small part with a row of its own in the tree, such as a tiny screw, is not a floating piece. For those, see [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts)."],["see",["fill-holes","remesh","delete-small-parts","cleanup","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"remesh","group":"lighter","title":"Remesh: Untriangulate and Triangulate","summary":"Join the triangles of each flat face into one polygon and take away the vertices in its middle, or go back to even triangles. The shape stays as it is.","blocks":[["p","Screens draw triangles, so every flat face of a part is cut into triangles, often more than it needs. A polygon, also called an n-gon, is a flat face with any number of corners: several triangles in one plane, joined into one face."],["p","Remesh has two actions in one panel, and a quick way to make a part lighter, [Quick remesh](#remesh), further down. {{Untriangulate}} joins flat triangles into polygons and takes away the vertices in the middle of each flat face. {{Triangulate}} is the way back: the flat faces stop being polygons and show their triangles again."],["h","When to use it"],["list",["Untriangulate: on a part whose flat faces carry vertices they do not need, for example around holes you closed with [Remove holes](#fill-holes). The part loses those vertices and the triangles that hung on them.","Triangulate: to go back to plain triangles, or to even out long thin triangles on flat faces."]],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the parts to work on. With nothing selected, the panel works on every part.","Click {{Remesh}} on the bottom toolbar. Its icon is a grid. The panel opens at the bottom left of the viewport. You can also find it with the search ([[Ctrl + K]], “Remesh”).","Choose {{Untriangulate}} or {{Triangulate}} at the top of the panel. The settings of the one you chose show under it.","Press the button under the settings, or [[Enter]]. It carries the name of the action you chose.","A message says what was done, for example how many triangles a part had before and after."]],["h","From the keyboard"],["keys",[["U","Untriangulate the selection"],["Shift + U","Triangulate the selection"]]],["p","The two keys work on the selection only, so a key never changes the whole scene by accident. With nothing selected, they only say “Select a part first”."],["h","The settings of Untriangulate"],["terms",[["Flat faces within","How far neighbouring triangles may turn from each other and still count as one flat face, in degrees. It starts at 1."],["Even triangles","Turns the edges inside each rebuilt face until its triangles are as even as its vertices allow, instead of long thin fans. No vertex is added or taken away."],["Flatten shading on flat faces","A vertex whose triangles all lie in one plane is shaded flat. The rim of a hole you closed needs this. It still carries the tilted shading it had against the wall of the hole, and a face like that is not joined."],["Shading tolerance","A flat face is left alone if the shading at its vertices differs from the face by more than this, because joining it would change smooth shading. It starts at 5°."],["Surface may move","A vertex that is a little off the plane of its face, as on a gently curved panel, may still go if the surface moves no more than this, and never more than 1% of the part’s size. It starts at 0: only vertices in the plane go."],["Points on straight lines","Also takes away the points that only sit on a straight line of a face’s outline, such as a long edge cut into pieces. They go from every face that touches them at once, so nothing cracks and the shape does not change."],["Dissolve nearly flat within","For faces that are only nearly flat, such as a panel with a little noise or a gentle bend. A point that is left goes if the triangles round it turn from each other by less than this angle and the surface moves by less than {{Surface may move}}. 0 turns it off."]]],["p","Untriangulate repeats until nothing more can be joined. Joining faces makes bigger faces, and a vertex that was on the edge between two of them is then in the middle of one."],["h","The settings of Triangulate"],["terms",[["Even out the triangles","Turns the edges inside each flat face until the triangles are as even as its vertices allow, so there are fewer long thin ones. The number of triangles and the vertices do not change."],["Show their triangle lines","Draws the triangle lines of these parts in the wireframe, whatever the {{Lines}} setting is."],["Flat faces within","How far neighbouring triangles may turn from each other and still count as one flat face when they are made more even."]]],["h","What does not change"],["p","The shape of the part. Untriangulate takes away only vertices that lie in the middle of a flat face, so the face keeps its outline. Only when you raise {{Surface may move}} above 0 can the surface shift, and by no more than the distance you typed. Triangulate adds no vertex and takes none away."],["h","How it shows in the view"],["p","In the wireframe view ([[Alt + 2]]), a part that has been through Untriangulate draws its polygons, and a part that has been through Triangulate draws its triangles. That holds whatever {{Lines}} is set to under {{Settings}}, {{Viewport}}."],["h","Quick remesh"],["p","Quick remesh makes the selected parts lighter by choosing a level, with a live preview, instead of typing numbers. It is a separate tool from Untriangulate and Triangulate: it removes triangles, so it is lossy, like [Decimate](#decimate)."],["steps",["Select the parts to make lighter.","Press [[B]], or find {{Quick remesh}} with the search. A column of levels opens under the pointer. Each level is how many of the triangles stay, written as a number without a % sign.","Move up and down the column. The selected parts take the level the pointer is on straight away, so you judge the result on the real model. Moving back up gives the detail back.","Let go of the mouse button on a level, press [[Enter]], or let go of [[B]] if you held it, to keep that level."]],["p","Keeping a level is one undo step. [[Esc]] or a press beside the column puts the parts back as they were. It needs a selection: with nothing selected it says “Select a part first”. It does not run while [Edit mesh](#edit-mesh) is on."],["p","A small or thin part can lose its shape if too much is taken away. If a level would leave a part with less than about 60 % of its surface, or would shrink it by more than about 10 % of its diagonal, that result is refused and the part takes the next gentler level instead. So a small bolt does not shrink to a scrap while the big parts beside it go down to the level you chose."],["h","Undo"],["p","A run is one undo step. Press [[Ctrl + Z]] and the mesh is as it was."],["see",["fill-holes","remove-floating-pieces","decimate","view-modes","workflow","edit-mesh"]]]},{"slug":"split","group":"lighter","title":"Split","summary":"Take apart a mesh that arrived as one piece but is really several separate solids, or cut it into its faces, so each can be selected on its own.","blocks":[["p","Some files arrive with several solid pieces fused into one part: a bracket, its pin and two bolts as a single row in the tree. You cannot hide, delete or simplify one of them without the others. Split finds the pieces that do not touch and makes each one a part of its own. It can also cut a mesh into its faces, at the creases."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-split.webp","Split: recovers the separate solids of a mesh that was fused on export. Here the whole gearbox, merged into one mesh, comes back as 206 parts."],["h","When to use it"],["p","When clicking one thing selects several things that are clearly separate objects. An STL file, for example, holds a whole model as a single mesh."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the part that should come apart.","Press [[X]], or click {{Split}} on the bottom toolbar. The panel opens.","Read the preview line in the panel. It says what the split would produce.","Press {{Split selected}}, or [[Enter]].","The part is replaced by its pieces in the tree. A message says how many parts were made."]],["p","To check every part of the model at once, press {{Scan whole model}} instead."],["h","What the options mean"],["p","The four buttons at the top set how readily pieces are told apart:"],["terms",[["Strict","The most pieces. Nothing is joined. Use it if you suspect the file has doubled points along its edges."],["Tight","The default. Points that are exact duplicates are joined, which keeps one solid whole along its sharp edges without joining neighbouring solids."],["Normal","Also joins points that are a tiny distance apart, to allow for small rounding differences."],["Loose","Joins points within 0.1% of the model’s size, so solids that touch are joined on purpose. Use it when you want fewer, larger parts."]]],["p","Right under the four buttons is {{Weld tolerance}}. It is the same distance, set by hand, for when none of the four fits. It sits on the panel itself, not under Advanced. The line below it says what the distance comes to at the size of your model."],["p","{{Algorithm}} chooses how connection is decided:"],["terms",[["Vertex connectivity","The default. Triangles that share a corner point belong together."],["Edge connectivity","Stricter. Triangles belong together only when they share a whole edge, so solids that meet at a single point stay separate."],["Spatial proximity (AABB)","Groups triangles by where they are in space instead of how they are connected. Useful when the connections in the file are fused but the solids are still apart."],["Hybrid (vertex + spatial)","Vertex connectivity first, then a second pass by position on any piece that still holds more than half of the mesh."],["Faces (split at creases)","Every face becomes a part. Triangles that share an edge and lie within the {{Face angle}} of each other stay together, and every crease starts another part. A crease is an edge where the surface turns sharply. A flat face, or a smooth curved one, comes out as one part."]]],["p","Two fields sit under the list:"],["terms",[["Face angle","Shown only with {{Faces (split at creases)}}. Two neighbouring triangles are one face if the angle between them is no more than this. It starts at 20°, which keeps a smooth curve as one face. 5° follows every facet of a curve, and 60° keeps only the sharp creases."],["Smallest part","For every method. Pieces with fewer triangles than this do not become parts of their own: together they make one more part. It starts at 0, where every piece is a part. Raise it to go from thousands of parts to a few."]]],["h","Good to know"],["p","The pieces of a split part stay in every group the part was a member of."],["h","Undo"],["p","One split is one undo step: [[Ctrl + Z]] puts the part back together."],["note","If the message says no separable parts were found, the mesh is one connected solid at this setting. Try {{Strict}}, or the {{Spatial proximity (AABB)}} algorithm."],["see",["parts-tree","select-parts","remesh","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"smart-fit","group":"lighter","title":"Smart fit","summary":"Replace a detailed part with a simple stand-in of the same size and position: a box, a cylinder, a few boxes, or blocks that keep its outline.","blocks":[["p","Some parts only need to be there. A bolt can carry a great many triangles, and from a distance a plain cylinder looks the same. Smart fit replaces the selected parts with a simple shape of the same size, in the same place."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-smart-fit.webp","Smart fit: box, turned box, cylinder, a few boxes, or blocks at the detail you choose."],["h","When to use it"],["p","For nuts, bolts, pins and housings: parts that fill a space but whose detail does not matter. Not for parts whose shape people will look at. This is the most drastic of the reducing tools: the part’s own mesh is thrown away."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the parts to replace.","Press [[Ctrl + B]], or click {{Smart fit}} on the bottom toolbar. The panel opens.","Choose a shape, or leave it on {{Auto}}.","Press {{Smart fit}}, or [[Enter]].","Each selected part becomes its stand-in. Its size and position are kept."]],["h","The shapes"],["terms",[["Auto","Picks a box, a turned box or a cylinder for each part, whichever fits it best."],["Box","A box lined up with the scene’s axes."],["Turned box","A box turned to follow the part. Better for parts that sit at an angle."],["Cylinder","A cylinder along the part’s long axis."],["A few boxes","A handful of boxes, each sized and turned to one piece of the part. A levelling foot becomes a slab and a bar, a frame becomes four bars. Each box is 12 triangles."],["Blocks","The part rebuilt from a coarse grid of boxes. It looks boxy, but its steps, arms and openings stay where they are."]]],["p","One box says where a part is and nothing about what it is: a bracket becomes a brick. {{A few boxes}} and {{Blocks}} keep the outline. Both fall back to a plain box when they would not be clearly lighter than the part."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-blocks.webp","Smart fit with Blocks on two heavy parts of the gearbox: 72,666 triangles down to 212, the outline kept."],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["At most … boxes","For {{A few boxes}}: the most boxes a part may become. Simple parts use fewer. It starts at 6."],["Blocks detail","For {{Blocks}}: how many cells the part’s longest side is cut into. More cells follow the outline more closely and cost more triangles. An opening narrower than a cell closes. It starts at 8."],["Roundness for a cylinder","Under {{Advanced}}. How round a part must be, looking down its axis, to become a cylinder. 1 is a perfect circle, a hexagon is 0.87, a square 0.71."],["Cylinder to box volume","A cylinder is chosen only if its volume is at most this share of the best box’s volume."],["Shortest cylinder, length to width","How many times longer than it is wide a part must be to become a cylinder. 0 lets flat round parts such as gears and washers be cylinders too."],["Find the part's own axes","Works out which way each part points from its mesh. Off: only the scene’s axes and the axes stored with the part are tried."]]],["h","Every part at once"],["p","The button {{Smart fit all parts}} in the card {{Reduce triangles}} replaces every part in the scene with a box, a turned box or a cylinder. It removes most of the detail of the model. Use it only when you need the roughest possible stand-in for a whole assembly."],["h","Undo"],["p","One run is one undo step: [[Ctrl + Z]] brings the original meshes back."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["Only the selected parts change. Another part with the same shape that stands elsewhere is left as it is. Only a copy that lies on the selected part itself, drawn twice in the same place, is fitted along with it.","If the view is exploded, Smart fit works on the parts at rest and then puts the explosion back as it was."]],["note","A quick way to simplify all fasteners: press {{Select fasteners}} in [Fasteners](#fasteners), then run Smart fit with {{Cylinder}}. For the copies of one fastener only, select one, press {{Select similar}} in the card {{Selection & actions}}, then run Smart fit with {{Cylinder}}."],["see",["decimate","fasteners","select-parts","smart-optimise","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"decimate","group":"lighter","title":"Decimate","summary":"Remove a share of the triangles from the selected parts while keeping their shape. Choose a percentage or a target, and preview first.","blocks":[["p","Decimate thins out the triangles of a part while keeping its overall shape. It is the tool for a part that matters and is simply too dense."],["p","It is lossy: the removed detail is gone from the mesh. It can be undone."],["h","When to use it"],["p","On the heavy parts you found in [How to read the numbers](#read-the-numbers), after you have deleted what is not needed. For a whole scene at once, [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget) is easier."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select the parts to reduce.","Open the card {{Reduce triangles}} in the right sidebar, under Reduce.","In the list beside {{Decimate}}, choose how much to remove: −25%, −50%, −75% or −90%.","Press {{Preview…}} under it. A table shows each part with its triangle count now and after.","Press {{Decimate}}. The button shows how many parts are selected.","Look at the parts. If they have lost too much, press [[Ctrl + Z]] and try a smaller percentage."]],["h","A triangle target instead of a percentage"],["steps",["Choose {{Target…}} in the list.","Type the number of triangles that everything selected may have together.","A line under the field says what will happen, in the form “12,400 → about 6,200 triangles”.","Press {{Decimate}}. Each selected part gives up the same share."]],["h","What it keeps"],["list",["Normals, so the shading stays smooth.","UVs, the coordinates that place a texture on a surface.","Vertex colours.","Material ranges, for a part that uses more than one material.","Sharp edges, when {{Keep sharp edges}} is on (it is by default). The vertices on a rim, a step or a corner, where the faces turn by more than 40 degrees, stay where they are while the triangles around them go. It only applies when the part has few such edges and the goal can still be reached. A part that is mostly hard edges, such as a gear (more than 8% of its vertices), is reduced the old way. The switch is in the {{Reduce triangles}} card and is remembered; Fit to budget uses it too."]],["h","Good to know"],["list",["It runs in the background, several parts at a time. The window stays usable, and a long run can be stopped.","The reduced mesh is what gets saved and exported.","Decimate has no keyboard shortcut on purpose, because it is lossy. Find it in the card, in the `…` list or with the search."]],["note","A common mistake is to decimate everything by 90% at once. Curved parts become visibly faceted. Reduce in steps, and reduce the densest parts more than the rest."],["see",["fit-to-budget","heavy-parts","read-the-numbers","smart-fit","smart-optimise"]]]},{"slug":"fit-to-budget","group":"lighter","title":"Fit to budget","summary":"Type the number of triangles the whole scene may have. The densest meshes are reduced first until the total fits.","blocks":[["p","Often you are given one number: “the model must be under 500,000 triangles”. Fit to budget takes that number and decides for you which parts to reduce and by how much. The densest meshes give up the most. Large, sparse ones are left alone."],["h","When to use it"],["p","Near the end, after you have deleted the parts that are not needed. Deleting is free. Reducing costs detail, so reduce only what is left."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card {{Reduce triangles}} in the right sidebar.","Type the budget in the field beside {{Fit to budget}}. You can write `150000`, `150k` or `1.5m`.","Press {{Preview…}}. A table lists the meshes that will be reduced, with their triangle count now and after, and an estimate of the new total.","Press {{Fit to budget}}.","A progress card appears. {{Cancel}} stops it, and then nothing is changed.","A message gives the result, for example how many triangles the scene went from and to, and how many meshes were reduced."]],["h","What to expect"],["list",["The preview is an estimate. A mesh can stop a little short of its share.","Every mesh keeps at least 16 triangles. If the budget is lower than the scene can reach, the app goes as far as it can and says that the budget was not reached.","If the scene is already under the budget, the app says so and changes nothing.","{{Keep sharp edges}}, under Decimate, applies here too: rims, steps and corners keep their vertices while the budget is met."]],["h","Undo"],["p","The whole step is one undo: [[Ctrl + Z]] restores every mesh."],["note","If the result looks too rough, undo, delete more of what is not needed (see [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts) and [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts)), and run it again. The same budget then leaves more triangles for the parts that remain."],["p","[Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) can do this as its last step, together with the other clean-ups. The whole run is then one undo step. Its card is just above the Reduce triangles card in the sidebar."],["see",["decimate","smart-optimise","heavy-parts","read-the-numbers","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"cleanup","group":"lighter","title":"Quick fixes and tidying up","summary":"Small one-click repairs: remove empty parts and empty groups, fix shading, recentre the model and make moves permanent.","blocks":[["p","Two cards in the right sidebar hold one-click commands: {{Quick fixes}}, under Optimise, and {{Edit selection}}, under Select. Each command is one undo step."],["h","Quick fixes"],["terms",[["Remove empty parts","Deletes parts that contain no triangles. Files often carry a few of these."],["Deduplicate geometry","Looks for parts whose mesh data is identical to another part’s. See the note below before you use it."],["Fix degenerate parts","Removes parts whose triangles have no area, which can confuse the programs that draw them."],["Remove floating pieces…","Opens a panel that deletes the small loose pieces of a part that touch nothing. See [Remove floating pieces](#remove-floating-pieces)."],["Delete empty groups","Removes groups that have no parts left in them."],["Recompute smooth normals","Works out the shading directions of every surface again. Use it when parts look oddly faceted or dark after large changes."]]],["p","Each command reports what it did, or says that it found nothing."],["note","Be careful with {{Deduplicate geometry}} on an assembly. It removes a part only when another part with identical mesh data sits in the same place (two copies stacked on each other), so a set of identical bolts placed in different positions stays. Check the parts count afterwards, and press [[Ctrl + Z]] if it took parts you wanted. (Before v0.20.0 it removed every repeat, placed or not; that was a bug.)"],["p","[Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) starts every run with the first three commands, empty parts, degenerate parts and empty groups, under the name {{Remove empty and broken parts}}. It does not run {{Deduplicate geometry}} or any of the other commands on this page."],["h","Edit selection"],["terms",[["Recentre on origin","Moves the model so the centre of its bounding box sits at 0,0,0."],["Bake gizmo transforms","Makes the moves, turns and scalings you did with the gizmo permanent in the mesh itself."],["Centre pivot","Moves the pivot of each selected part to the centre of its bounding box. The part does not move on screen."]]],["h","Flags"],["p","The card {{Filters & flags}}, under Find, marks suspicious parts without changing them:"],["terms",[["Flag low-triangle parts","Marks parts with fewer triangles than the slider says."],["Flag thin slivers","Marks parts that are extremely long for their width, such as gaskets, wires and decals."]]],["p","The status bar shows how many parts are flagged. Click that number to isolate them, look at them, and decide what to do."],["see",["remove-floating-pieces","stacked-copies","smart-optimise","parts-tree","align-to-floor","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"workflow","group":"lighter","title":"A sensible order to work in","summary":"A suggested routine for a first clean-up: look, delete what is not needed, simplify what remains, check, save and export.","blocks":[["p","This is a suggestion, not a rule. It follows from what the tools do: deleting a part costs nothing in quality, so delete first. Reducing a mesh costs detail, so reduce only what you decided to keep. And there is no point in simplifying a part you are about to delete."],["p","If you want the shortest way, [Smart optimise](#smart-optimise) follows this same order for you: one button, one undo. The steps below are for when you want to look at the result of each tool before the next one."],["steps",["Open the file and look at it. Read the totals in {{Properties}}. See [How to read the numbers](#read-the-numbers).","If the model lies on its side or sits far from the grid, stand it up with [Align to floor](#align-to-floor).","Find out where the weight is: open [Heavy parts](#heavy-parts) and press [[Alt + 4]] for the heatmap. Delete the heavy parts that nobody needs.","Remove what nobody will see with [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts). Isolate, check, delete.","Remove copies that lie on top of each other with [Stacked copies](#stacked-copies). Find, isolate, check, delete.","Find the bolts, nuts and washers with [Fasteners](#fasteners). Delete them if the model does not need them, or replace them with stand-ins using [Smart fit](#smart-fit).","Remove other small parts with [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts), if the model does not need them.","Close holes with [Remove holes](#fill-holes). If small loose pieces are left beside a part, take them out with [Remove floating pieces](#remove-floating-pieces).","Replace other filler parts with stand-ins, using [Smart fit](#smart-fit).","Reduce the heavy parts that remain with [Decimate](#decimate), or bring the whole scene to a number with [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget).","Tidy up: {{Remove empty parts}} and {{Delete empty groups}} in the card {{Quick fixes}}.","Look at the whole model again in the normal view ([[Alt + 1]]) and in clay ([[5]]). Open the {{Optimisation report}} to see what you saved.","Save the scene, so you can come back to it. See [Save a scene](#save-scene).","Export the file for the program it is meant for. See [Export](#export)."]],["h","Habits that help"],["list",["Check before you delete. {{Isolate}} shows exactly what a tool has selected.","Work in steps and look after each one. [[Ctrl + Z]] takes back any of them.","Save a scene before a drastic step. Your original file is never touched, and a saved scene is a second safety net.","Stop when the number is reached. A model that is lighter than it needs to be has lost detail for nothing."]],["see",["smart-optimise","remove-floating-pieces","remesh","read-the-numbers","save-scene","export"]]]},{"slug":"view-modes","group":"look","title":"View modes","summary":"Six ways to draw the same model: solid, wireframe, X-ray, heatmap, clay and CAD. One key each, plus the Shading card for the clay and CAD looks.","blocks":[["p","A view mode changes how the model is drawn, not the model itself. Switch with [[Alt]] and a number for the first four, and with the number alone for Clay and CAD. The first four also have buttons on the tool strip at the left edge of the viewport. Clay and CAD are chosen on the {{Shading}} card in the right sidebar (see below)."],["note","Since v0.24.0 the keys [[1]] to [[4]] without [[Alt]] pick Points, Edges, Polygons and Model in [Edit mesh](#edit-mesh). They used to switch the view. On a Mac the keys are read by their place on the keyboard, so [[Option + 1]] to [[Option + 4]] work although Option + 1 types another character there."],["keys",[["Alt + 1","Solid: the normal view, with colours and lighting"],["Alt + 2","Wireframe: only the edges of the triangles"],["Alt + 3","X-ray: every part as a see-through shell"],["Alt + 4","Heatmap: parts coloured by triangle density"],["5","Clay: every part in one plain material. Press it again to return to solid."],["6","CAD: flat, matte shading with a dark line on every sharp edge. Press it again to return to solid."]]],["h","Solid"],["p","The everyday view. Parts have their own colours and are lit by a soft studio and a light that follows the camera, so dark parts keep an edge and a sheen."],["h","Wireframe"],["p","Shows the triangles themselves. Use it to see how dense a mesh is, and to check what Decimate has done. Where the lines are so close that they merge into a solid patch, the mesh is very dense."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-wireframe.webp","Wireframe: every edge of every part."],["h","X-ray"],["p","Makes every part translucent, so you can see what is inside without hiding anything."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-xray.webp","X-ray: the whole assembly as translucent shells."],["h","Heatmap"],["p","Colours each part by how many triangles it has for its size. It answers the question “where is the weight?” at a glance. See [How to read the numbers](#read-the-numbers)."],["h","Clay"],["p","Takes the colours away. Every part gets the same plain material, so all that is left is form: edges, curvature, and what stands in front of what. It is a good view for judging whether a simplified model still reads as the same object."],["p","Clay has four looks: {{Clay}} (matte grey), {{Porcelain}} (pale and glossy), {{Steel}} (metal) and {{Red wax}}. You choose the look on the Shading card."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-clay.webp","Clay: every part in one plain material, so only the form is left."],["h","CAD"],["p","The look of a CAD program. There are no lights, no reflections and no shadows. A surface gets its shade only from the way it faces the camera: lighter where it looks left, darker where it looks right, and a warm sand tone where it looks up. (This kind of shading is called a matcap: a picture of one lit ball, and every surface takes the colour of the spot on the ball that faces the same way.)"],["p","A grey or white part takes the shading as it is. A coloured part keeps its colour, with the same shading on top. A fine dark line follows the sharp edges of every part, and the sides of round parts get a thin dark outline too, so the form stays easy to read."],["p","CAD has three looks: {{Ceramic}} (the default, with warm tops), {{Light}} (brighter) and {{Mono}} (plain grey, no warm tone)."],["h","The Shading card"],["p","In the right sidebar, under {{Properties}}, is the {{Shading}} card. If it is folded, click its header to open it. It is one row of small 3D nuts, each drawn in its own look, so you see what a look does before you choose it. The row scrolls sideways when the sidebar is narrow."],["terms",[["Default","The plain Solid view, with the model’s own materials."],["Clay, Porcelain, Steel, Red wax","The four clay looks."],["Ceramic, Light, Mono","The three CAD looks. They carry a small CAD badge."],["Outlines","A switch under the row. It turns the dark lines on sharp edges on or off."]]],["steps",["Click a look. The view switches to clay or CAD, whichever the look belongs to, and the look you clicked is lit.","Click another look to change it. The model changes at once.","Click the lit look again to go back to Solid. Clicking {{Default}} does the same."]],["p","Keys 5 and 6 do the same as clicking: they switch to clay or CAD with the last look you used, and press again to go back to solid."],["h","Outlines"],["p","The {{Outlines}} switch draws a fine dark line along the sharp edges of every part. It works in Solid, Clay and CAD views, and is on to begin with. It is not drawn in Wireframe, X-ray or Heatmap. The same switch is under {{Settings}}, {{Viewport}}, together with the choice of CAD look."],["p","On a very large scene, the smallest parts may be drawn without outlines to keep the view quick. The app tells you when it does this."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["A view mode never reaches a file. Saving and exporting switch to solid while they run, then switch back.","To see inside a model, use X-ray, [Hide, isolate, show all](#hide-isolate) or [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts).","The background and the lighting are under {{Settings}}, {{Viewport}} and {{Settings}}, {{Scene}}. CAD does not use the lights at all.","A [screenshot](#screenshots) uses the view that is on, unless you choose Solid, Wireframe or X-ray in the screenshot window."]],["see",["read-the-numbers","hide-isolate","screenshots","edit-mesh"]]]},{"slug":"colours-materials","group":"look","title":"Colours and materials","summary":"Open the materials dock to see every material in the scene, recolour all the parts that share one, assign a material with a button or by dragging it onto a part, and let the dock float as a window.","blocks":[["p","A material is what a surface looks like: its colour, and how rough or metallic it is. Parts share materials. A CAD file with fifty grey brackets usually has one grey material that all fifty use. Change that material and all fifty change."],["h","Open the materials dock"],["p","Press [[Shift + M]], or click the palette button on the bottom toolbar. The dock slides up from the bottom of the window and shows every material as a small ball, with the number of parts that use it."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-materials.webp","The materials dock: every material in the scene, with how many parts use it."],["h","Find a material"],["list",["Type in {{Filter…}} to filter by name, by colour (as a hex code such as `#ff6600`) or by type.","Choose the order: {{Most used}}, {{Most triangles}}, {{Name}} or {{Colour}}.","Switch between a grid and a list with the two small buttons.","Select a part in the viewport and look at {{Properties}}: it shows the part’s material. The small badge in the corner of that card is the number of parts that use the material."]],["h","Let the dock float as a window"],["p","The button {{Pop out as a floating window}} in the head of the dock turns it into a window over the view. Drag the window by its head to move it. Drag any edge or corner to resize it. The same button, now called {{Dock to the bottom}}, puts it back."],["list",["A floating dock does not push the toolbar up and does not close the other dock. Materials and [the library](#library) can both float, so the two can be open side by side.","Where you left the window, and whether it floats, is remembered in this browser.","With several windows open, the one you pressed or typed in last stands in front."]],["h","See where a material is used"],["steps",["Click a material in the dock.","The inspector at the right of the dock shows its colour, roughness, metalness and opacity, and how many parts and triangles use it.","Press {{Select parts}} to select every part that uses it, or {{Isolate}} to see only those parts."]],["h","Recolour"],["steps",["Double-click a material in the dock, or select it and press {{Edit}} in the inspector.","Change the colour in the editor that opens. You can also change how rough or metallic the surface is.","Every part that shares the material changes at once."]],["terms",[["Roughness","How matt the surface is. Low is glossy, high is dull."],["Metalness","How much the surface behaves like metal."],["Opacity","How solid the surface is. Lower values are see-through."]]],["p","The material editor is a small window of its own. It stays where you put it, also when you open another material in it, and it opens there again the next time. When it holds more than fits on the screen, its body scrolls. Drag its top or bottom edge to make it shorter."],["h","Rename a material"],["p","Select a part that uses the material and double-click anywhere on the material card in {{Properties}}. The name turns into a field you can type in. An open material editor takes the new name at once."],["h","Give parts a different material"],["steps",["Select the parts in the viewport or in the tree.","Click the material you want in the dock.","Press {{Assign}} in the inspector."]],["p","You can do the same with the mouse. Drag a material out of the dock, a tile or a row, and drop it in the view. It goes onto everything that is selected. With nothing selected, it goes onto the part you drop it on. That part is tinted while the material is over it. It is one undo step."],["h","Make, copy, merge, delete"],["p","The buttons at the right of the dock’s header:"],["terms",[["New material","The `+` button. Makes a new material. If parts are selected, they get it."],["New material from a preset","Hold the `+` down, or right-click it, and a list of ready-made looks opens. You can press, slide to a preset and let go in one move. The arrow beside the `+` opens the same list with one click. A preset makes a new material, assigned to the selected parts if there are any, in one undo step."],["Duplicate","Copies the picked material."],["Merge","Turns several picked materials into one. Hold [[Ctrl]] and click to pick several."],["Delete","Deletes the picked materials."]]],["p","To merge two materials with the mouse, hold [[Shift]] and drag one onto the other. The dragged material stays and takes the other one over. A tip that starts with “Merge into” shows before you let go. Without [[Shift]], a drop on another material does nothing."],["p","A right-click on a material gives the same commands, and one to copy its colour code. The material card in {{Properties}} has the same menu."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["A new material is named after its colour, and the name follows the colour when you change it.","Every change in the dock is an undo step.","Drag the top edge of the dock to make it taller. Double-click the edge to reset it.","While it is docked at the bottom, the dock shares its place with the library and the console, and they take turns. A floating dock stays open."]],["see",["view-modes","select-parts","export"]]]},{"slug":"move-rotate-scale","group":"look","title":"Move, rotate, scale and measure","summary":"Use the gizmo to move, turn and resize parts, drag a part over other surfaces with Dynamic place, type exact values in the transform card, and measure the distance between two points.","blocks":[["p","Select a part and a gizmo appears on it: a set of coloured handles. Red is the X axis, green is Y and blue is Z. Drag a handle to change the part along that axis."],["keys",[["E","Move"],["R","Rotate"],["T","Scale"],["Q","Hide the gizmo"],["D","Dynamic place: drag the selected part over other surfaces"],["Shift","Hold while dragging to snap to round steps"]]],["h","Move a part"],["steps",["Select the part. To move several parts together, select them all, or group them first with [[Ctrl + G]].","Press [[E]].","Drag one of the arrows. A small read-out shows how far the part has moved.","Hold [[Shift]] while you drag to move in round steps sized to the model."]],["p","Rotate ([[R]]) and scale ([[T]]) work the same way. With [[Shift]] held, rotation snaps to 15° and scale to steps of 0.1."],["h","Choose the snap step"],["p","While you hold [[Shift]] and drag a handle, turn the mouse wheel to change the step. Wheel up gives a coarser step and wheel down a finer one. A move steps through 1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50 and so on. A turn steps through 1°, 5°, 10°, 15°, 30°, 45° and 90°. A scale steps from 0.01 to 1. The part takes the new step at once, and the step is kept for your next drags."],["p","While snap is on, a small green pill at the top of the viewport, beside the camera pill, shows the step, for example {{Snap 10 mm}} or {{Snap 15°}}."],["h","Gizmo axes: World or Local"],["p","Hold the Move, Rotate or Scale button on the tool strip, or right-click it, for a short list. {{World}} lines the gizmo up with the scene’s X, Y and Z. {{Local}} lines it up with the part’s own axes, as the part is turned. In [Edit mesh](#edit-mesh) the list also has {{Normal}}, where the gizmo’s Z points along the average normal of the picked pieces, so a face can be pulled straight out of itself."],["h","Type exact values"],["steps",["Click the transform panel button on the viewport’s tool strip, the fifth button from the top. The {{Transform}} card rises into place at the foot of the left sidebar, under the parts tree.","It has three columns, {{Position}}, {{Rotation}} and {{Size}}, with a row for X, Y and Z.","Click a number and type a value, or drag it sideways.","Press {{Reset}} to put position, rotation and size back."]],["p","The switch beside {{Reset}} turns the third column from {{Size}} into a scale factor. It works for instanced copies too. A scale typed for a group in the parts tree scales the parts inside it, with undo, and {{Reset}} takes it back. [[Tab]] goes down each column."],["h","Several parts at once"],["p","The nine fields work on a whole selection too. With several parts selected, a field shows the number when every part has the same one, and {{mixed}} when they differ."],["p","A number you type goes to each part on its own, about its own origin. Every part gets that position, that rotation or that size, and the other eight numbers of each part stay as they were. The whole change is one undo step."],["list",["Locked parts, shared (instanced) copies and parts with no thickness along an axis are skipped. A message lists what stayed.","With more than 400 parts selected, the fields just say {{mixed}}, without comparing the parts. You can still type a number into them.","The {{Scale}} switch is respected: with it on, the third column is a scale factor for every part."]],["p","The gizmo helps too. When the selected parts are all turned the same way, the gizmo keeps that turn instead of snapping to the scene’s axes as soon as a second part is picked. To line parts up on an axis or space them evenly, see [Align parts](#align-parts)."],["p","Right-click a column heading to copy all three values or paste them onto another part. The units follow {{Display units}} under {{Settings}}, {{Scene}}."],["h","Dynamic place"],["p","Dynamic place moves a part by dragging it over the model, instead of by its handles. The part rests on whatever surface is under the pointer and turns to face it, as when you drag a part out of the [library](#library) onto a model. It is handy for putting a bolt on a plate or a bracket on a wall."],["steps",["Select the part, or several parts.","Press [[D]], or click the fourth button on the tool strip (an arrow pointing down at a dot). The {{Dynamic place}} card opens over the viewport. The gizmo is hidden, and a thin blue box appears round the selection to show which box is being set down.","Press on the selected part and drag. The part follows the pointer and rests on the surface under it. The underside of the box touches the surface and never sinks into it.","Let go to drop it there.","Press [[Esc]] or {{Done}} to close the card and get the gizmo back."]],["p","A press that is not on the selected part selects as usual, and a press with [[Shift]], [[Ctrl]] or [[Alt]] held is left alone. If the view is exploded, put the parts back together first."],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Align to surface","On to begin with. The part turns so that its top points away from the surface it rests on. Off: the part keeps the way it faces and only slides."],["Land on the grid","On to begin with. Where nothing is under the pointer, the part lands on the grid. Off: it stays where it last rested."],["Top axis","Which of the part’s own axes, X, Y or Z, counts as its top. It is the part’s axes as it stands when you first pick it up. Z is the default when Z is up."],["Direction","{{+}} or {{−}}: whether the top axis points away from the surface or into it. Choose {{−}} to put a part on upside down."],["Gap","How far the part floats above the surface, in millimetres. A negative number sinks it in."],["Turn","Turns the part about the surface it rests on. Type an angle, or press 0°, 90°, 180° or 270°."],["Reset gap and turn","Sets the gap and the turn back to 0."]]],["p","While you hold a part, the mouse wheel turns it in steps of 15°, and the {{Turn}} box follows. Press [[Esc]] during a drag and the part goes back to where it was before the drag."],["p","The whole drag is one undo step. Choosing a gizmo with [[E]], [[R]] or [[T]] also ends Dynamic place and closes the card."],["h","Copy and duplicate"],["keys",[["Ctrl + D","Duplicate the selection"],["Ctrl + C","Copy the selection"],["Ctrl + V","Paste"]]],["h","Lock a part"],["p","Right-click a part in the tree and choose {{Lock}}. A locked part cannot be moved, and delete skips it. Choose {{Unlock}} in the same menu to release it."],["h","Measure"],["steps",["Press [[M]], or click the ruler on the bottom toolbar.","Click a point on the model. A dot follows the pointer to show where you will pick.","Click a second point. The distance appears between them.","Measure again, or press [[M]] or [[Esc]] to leave measure mode."]],["list",["Dragging still turns the view while you measure.","While you measure, a yellow {{Measure}} pill sits at the top of the viewport. Click it to leave measure mode.","Measurements stay on screen after you leave measure mode, and are listed in the card {{Measurements}} in the right sidebar. Click a row there to pick that measurement: its line gets thicker and its label turns yellow.","{{Clear all}} in that card removes them."]],["h","Undo"],["p","Moves, turns and scalings are undo steps, whether you dragged the gizmo or typed a value."],["note","Moving a part with the gizmo changes where it sits, not its mesh. To write the change into the mesh itself, use {{Bake gizmo transforms}} in the card {{Edit selection}}. Most people never need to."],["see",["align-parts","align-to-floor","select-parts","cleanup","edit-mesh"]]]},{"slug":"edit-mesh","group":"look","title":"Edit mesh: points, edges and polygons","summary":"Pick and change the pieces a part is made of: its points, edges and polygons. Move, delete, dissolve, planarize, straighten, weld, fill and extract them, from a bar at the right of the view.","blocks":[["p","Until now a click in the 3D view picked a whole part. Edit mesh lets you into the part. You pick its points, its edges or its polygons, and you can move them, take them out, join them or close a hole. If you have used Cinema 4D, it works the same way."],["p","A mesh is made of three kinds of piece. A {{point}} is a corner (the glossary calls it a vertex). An {{edge}} is the line between two points. A {{polygon}} is a flat face. Two triangles that together make a four-sided shape count as one polygon, as a cell of a grid or a strip of a tube wall does. A triangle with no such partner is a polygon of its own. See [Words you will meet](#glossary)."],["h","Go in and out"],["p","The mode button is at the top of the tool strip on the left edge of the view. It offers four modes: {{Points}}, {{Edges}}, {{Polygons}} and {{Model}}. {{Model}} is the app as it always was: a click picks a part."],["keys",[["1","Points"],["2","Edges"],["3","Polygons"],["4","Model: leave Edit mesh"]]],["list",["Click the button once to go into the last piece mode you used, or back out again.","Hold the button to see the list of all four.","While Edit mesh is on, three more buttons appear under the mode button: {{Points}}, {{Edges}} and {{Polygons}}. The lit one is the kind of piece you are picking, and a click on another switches to it. The mode button itself shows the Model icon then, and a click on it goes back to Model.","The view modes (solid, wireframe, X-ray, heatmap) that these keys used to switch are now on [[Alt + 1]] to [[Alt + 4]]. See [View modes](#view-modes)."]],["note","Select the part or parts first. In a piece mode only the selected parts show their mesh lines, and only they can be picked."],["note","A shape from the [library](#library) that still has {{Shape parameters}} is not edited in place, because its parameters would build it again. When you go into a piece mode with one selected, the app says {{Bake geo first}}. Press {{Bake geo}} under the shape’s parameters in {{Properties}} to turn it into an ordinary mesh, then pick its pieces."],["h","What you see"],["list",["The selected parts show their mesh lines in blue, as polygons rather than triangles.","The pieces you have picked are orange. The one under the pointer is light.","In Points mode every point of the part is shown."]],["h","Pick pieces"],["p","The Edit mesh tools are a bar at the right edge of the view, under the view cube. It replaces the card that earlier versions had. At the top of the bar are three tools that decide how the left mouse button picks. With all three, [[Shift]] adds to what is picked and [[Ctrl]] takes away from it."],["terms",[["Click","One piece at a time. A click beside the model clears the pick. A double-click picks a loop. With Click, a point is taken only when the pointer is within 16 pixels of it, and an edge within 12 pixels."],["Box","Drag a rectangle. What lies in it whole, and can be seen, is picked. The pick is live: it follows the box while you drag, and pieces the box lets go of are let go. The gizmo is put away during the drag. The history gets one entry when you let go. If you change tool, or the pointer is lost, in the middle of a drag, the box is cancelled and what you had picked comes back."],["Brush","Paint over the model. What the brush passes over is picked. Hold the middle button and drag to change the size of the brush, or type it in the bar."]]],["p","With Box and Brush, [[Alt]] and drag turns the view. {{Loops}} in the bar does what a double-click does for every click, and lights the loop under the pointer before you click."],["p","A loop of polygons runs across four-sided polygons like a ring round a tube. Where the polygons are not four-sided, the loop is the whole face. A loop of edges is the outline of a face when the edge is sharp or open, and the row of edges when it is smooth."],["keys",[["Ctrl + A","Pick everything of the selected parts"],["Ctrl + Z","Take back the last change of what is picked"]]],["h","Move, turn and scale"],["p","The gizmo sits in the middle of what is picked. [[E]] moves, [[R]] turns and [[T]] scales, and [[Shift]] snaps, as for a whole part (see [Move, rotate, scale](#move-rotate-scale)). It moves the points the pieces stand on, so everything that uses those points follows."],["p","The bar also sets the gizmo’s axes: {{World}}, {{Local}} or {{Normal}}. With {{Normal}} the gizmo’s Z points along the average normal of the picked pieces, so a polygon can be pulled straight out of itself."],["p","Letting go of the gizmo is one undo step, and the pieces stay picked. Clicking a handle without dragging adds nothing to the history. In the wireframe view the part keeps one set of lines while its pieces are worked on, and they follow the move."],["h","Delete and reverse"],["list",["[[Del]] takes the picked pieces out. A point or an edge takes every polygon that uses it. A part is never emptied this way. The polygons of what stays are kept.","{{Reverse normals}} turns the picked polygons round, so their front becomes their back. Use it for faces that came in the wrong way, which look dark or see-through from one side."]],["h","The tools that change the mesh"],["p","Each one is a button in the bar, below the gizmo’s axes, and each is one undo step. A button works on one kind of piece and is dimmed when something else is picked."],["terms",[["Dissolve","With [[Ctrl + Backspace]]. Picked points are taken out and the surface closes over them. For picked edges and polygons the polygons become one, and the points left inside them, or on a straight line of their outline, go. The shape does not change there."],["Planarize","For polygons. Moves the points of each picked polygon onto its best-fit plane. A polygon that would have to bend a long way, such as a curved face, is left as it is."],["Straighten","For edges. Moves the points along a picked line of edges onto the straight line between its two ends."],["Weld","For points and edges. Opens a small card of its own, with {{Join within}} and a {{Weld}} button. Picked points become one, at their middle. With a distance in {{Join within}}, the points that are that close to each other become one each. With 0, all the picked points become one. Picked edges collapse to a point. Triangles that are left with two corners in one place go."],["Fill","For edges. Closes a hole whose whole rim is picked. A double-click on an open edge picks the whole loop. The cap has points of its own, so it shades on its own."],["Extract","For polygons. Takes the picked polygons out into a part of their own, in the same place and with the same material."],["Reverse normals","For polygons. Described above."]]],["h","Key tips"],["p","While Edit mesh is on, the faint list of tips at the bottom right of the view shows what the mouse and keys do right now. It follows the tool you hold and what is picked. With nothing picked it tells you how to pick, and that [[Ctrl + A]] picks all and [[Esc]] goes back to Model. With something picked it adds [[Del]] to delete, [[Ctrl + Backspace]] to dissolve and [[Esc]] to let go."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["The quads and n-gons of an OBJ or FBX file are remembered after the file is read, so the polygons of the file are known afterwards.","[Quick remesh](#remesh) does not run while Edit mesh is on. Go back to Model first.","Leaving Edit mesh in the middle of a drag finishes the move as one undo step.","Everything here is one undo step, and after an undo the pieces are picked again. See [Undo and redo](#undo-redo)."]],["see",["move-rotate-scale","view-modes","remesh","fill-holes","library","undo-redo","shortcuts"]]]},{"slug":"align-to-floor","group":"look","title":"Align to floor","summary":"Stand a model upright on the grid, centred over the origin, when it arrived on its side or far away from the centre of the scene. A small drawing shows the result first.","blocks":[["p","Files arrive anywhere and any way up: a part modelled 40 metres from its origin, an assembly lying on its side. Align to floor turns the whole model in steps and puts it on the grid, over the point 0,0,0."],["p","It is a floating card over the viewport. A small 3D drawing at the top shows what will happen. The model itself does not move until you press the {{Align to floor}} button."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-align.webp","Align to floor: turn the model in steps and choose which side of it sits at zero."],["h","When to use it"],["p","Right after opening a file, before anything else, if the model is not standing the way you expect. A model that sits on the floor at the origin is easier to orbit, and easier to place in the next program."],["h","Open the card"],["list",["Press [[Ctrl + K]] and choose {{Align model to the floor…}}.","Or click the three dots at the right end of the bottom toolbar (All commands) and choose {{Align to the floor…}}.","Press [[Esc]] or {{Done}} to put the card away."]],["h","The drawing"],["p","The drawing shows the floor with its grid, the origin where the three axes meet, and the model as the box it fills. The model’s own top and front are two arrows on it. They turn with the model, so you can see which way it will end up. A yellow ring marks the point of the model that will go to zero, and a dashed line joins it to the origin while the two are apart."],["p","While the drawing differs from the real model, it says {{Preview: not applied yet}}. With no model open, it draws an example box, so you can try the buttons before you open a file."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card.","If the model lies on its side, use the turn buttons. Each of the rows X, Y and Z has two arrows that turn the model about that axis, one step back or one step forward. A step is 90° to begin with.","Press them until the arrows in the drawing show the model the right way up. Only the drawing turns.","Choose which side of the model goes to zero with the buttons {{Low}}, {{Mid}}, {{High}} and {{Keep}}. Again only the drawing changes.","Press {{Align to floor}}. Now the model turns and moves so that it stands on the grid with its middle over 0,0,0. You watch it move."]],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Turn, step","How far one press turns the model. 90 gives quarter turns. A small number straightens a model that is slightly tilted."],["Sits at zero with its: X and Y","Which side of the model goes to 0 on that axis: {{Low}}, {{Mid}} (the middle), {{High}}, or {{Keep}} to leave it where it is. The rows are named after the scene’s floor axes: X and Y when Z is up."],["Sits at zero with its: Up","{{Floor}} stands the model on the grid. {{Top}} hangs it from the grid. {{Mid}} centres it. {{Keep}} leaves its height alone."],["Align to floor","Does it: turns and moves the model as the drawing shows."],["Back to how it was loaded","Puts the model back where and how it was loaded, and puts the card back to its start: no turn waiting, and Mid, Mid and Floor."]]],["p","Mid, Mid and Floor is the usual choice: it stands the model on the grid, centred over the origin."],["p","To repeat the last choices without opening the card, press [[Ctrl + K]] and choose {{Align model to the floor (with the last choices)}}."],["h","Undo"],["p","Pressing {{Align to floor}} is one undo step. {{Back to how it was loaded}} in the card does what its name says."],["note","Which direction is “up” is a setting of the scene: {{Up axis (viewport)}} under {{Settings}}, {{Scene}}. CAD files normally use Z as up. If the whole scene looks turned by a quarter, check that setting before you turn the model."],["see",["align-parts","move-rotate-scale","export","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"align-parts","group":"look","title":"Align parts","summary":"Line the selected parts up along an axis, or space them evenly, with one press. Each press is one undo step.","blocks":[["p","Align parts moves the selected parts so they line up on one side or on the middle along X, Y or Z, or so they stand evenly apart. It is a floating card over the viewport. It moves whole parts, the way a layout program moves boxes, and does not change their meshes."],["p","To stand the whole model on the floor, use [Align to floor](#align-to-floor) instead."],["h","Open the card"],["list",["Press [[Ctrl + K]] and choose {{Align and spread parts…}}.","Or choose {{Align parts…}} in the {{Clean}} slice of the [Quick wand](#quick-wand) when nothing is selected. It has no key of its own.","Select the parts first. The card says how many are selected. Press [[Esc]] or {{Done}} to put it away."]],["h","Align to"],["p","Three choices at the top say what the parts line up on."],["terms",[["Selection","The box round the whole selection. The parts line up on its low side, its middle or its high side."],["Last picked","The part you picked last. It stays where it is, and the others line up on it."],["Origin","Zero. The parts line up on 0 on that axis. This is the only choice that works with a single part."]]],["h","Align and spread"],["p","There is a row for each of X, Y and Z, with two groups of buttons."],["terms",[["Align","Three buttons: the low sides, the middles and the high sides of the parts go to one place along that axis."],["Spread, middles","The middles of the parts end up the same distance apart. The two outermost parts stay where they are, and the ones between move."],["Spread, gaps","The gaps between the parts become equal. Without a number in {{Gap}}, the parts are spread evenly between the two outermost ones, which stay where they are."]]],["p","The {{Gap}} field sets a fixed gap for the second Spread button, in the scene’s units. The parts are laid out one after the other with that gap between them, counted from the lowest part. Leave it empty for an even spread."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Select two or more parts.","Choose what to align to.","Press a button in the row of the axis you want. The parts move at once.","A line under the buttons says what moved, for example how many parts moved, or that they were already in line."]],["h","What to know"],["list",["Parts are measured by their box in the scene, as they stand now.","Align needs at least two parts, or one part and the Origin. Spread needs three parts, or two when a gap is typed in. Otherwise the line under the buttons says so.","Locked parts stay where they are. The line under the buttons says how many were locked.","If the view is exploded, the parts are put back together first, because the parts at rest are what is lined up.","Each press is one undo step, so [[Ctrl + Z]] takes back one press."]],["note","Which axis is up is a setting of the scene: {{Up axis (viewport)}} under {{Settings}}, {{Scene}}. The pictures on the buttons stand upright for the axis that is up."],["see",["align-to-floor","move-rotate-scale","select-parts","search-and-commands","undo-redo"]]]},{"slug":"exploded-view","group":"look","title":"Exploded view","summary":"Push the parts of an assembly apart with one slider, choose the directions, and put them back with one click.","blocks":[["p","An exploded view moves every part away from the centre of the model, so parts that sit inside or behind others come into the open. It is a way of looking, like in an assembly manual."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-exploded.webp","Exploded view, with a slider for each axis."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Open the card {{Exploded view}} in the right sidebar, under Inspect. If it is folded, click its header.","Drag the {{Amount}} slider. The parts move apart. The value is a percentage, and the slider goes up to 300%. You can click the number and type one.","Under {{Along}}, the buttons X, Y and Z say which directions the parts move in. All three are on to begin with. Switch one off and the parts stop moving along that axis. Switch it on again and they follow.","Under {{Quick}}, press 25%, 50%, 100% or 200% to jump to that amount.","Press {{Reset}} to bring every part back."]],["h","Fine tune each axis"],["p","Click {{Fine tune each axis}} to open three more sliders, {{X axis}}, {{Y axis}} and {{Z axis}}. Use them when you want a different amount in each direction, for example to pull a stack apart upwards a long way and sideways only a little. The {{Amount}} slider then follows the largest of the three."],["h","While the view is exploded"],["list",["A pill that says {{Exploded}}, with the amount, appears at the top of the viewport beside the camera pill. Click it to put the parts back and set the sliders to zero.","Moving a slider lets go of the selection. You can still click parts and see their outlines, but they have no gizmo to move them until the parts are back together.","Smart fit and Remove holes work on the model at rest, then put the explosion back as it was."]],["note","A suggestion: put the parts back before you edit, save or export. The exploded view is for looking, and a model is easier to work on when its parts are where they belong."],["see",["hide-isolate","view-modes","screenshots"]]]},{"slug":"library","group":"look","title":"The parts library and shapes","summary":"Add standard parts to a scene from a drawer under the viewport: 112 bolts, nuts, pipes, profiles, machine parts, electronics and brackets. Look at a part and set its size before it goes in.","blocks":[["p","MeshOptimiser can also add parts. The library holds 112 ready-made parts on eight shelves. Each one is parametric, which means it is built from numbers such as a diameter and a length, and you can change those numbers before you place it and after."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-library.webp","The library drawer: a pillow block and a handwheel placed, and a stepper motor on its way in, shown where it will stand."],["h","Open the library"],["p","Press [[Shift + L]], or click the button with the small shapes on the bottom toolbar, just left of the palette button. The library is a drawer: it slides up from the bottom of the viewport and leaves the parts tree where it is. Press [[Shift + L]] again, or click the cross in its header, to close it."],["list",["Drag the top edge of the drawer to make it taller. Double-click the edge to reset it.","One drawer is open at a time. The library, the materials dock and the console give way to each other.","To keep the library open next to the materials, press {{Pop out as a floating window}} in its header. The library becomes a window over the view: drag it by its head, and resize it from any edge or corner. The same button, now {{Dock to the bottom}}, puts it back. Where you left it is remembered in this browser."]],["p","The drawer has two halves. On the left are the parts, in shelves, each with a small picture. On the right is the part you have picked: a view of it, its facts, its parameters, and the button that adds it."],["h","What is in it"],["terms",[["Shapes","Cube, sphere, cylinder, cone, torus, capsule, wedge, rounded box, star and more."],["Fasteners","Hex bolt, flange bolt, Allen screw, countersunk and button head screws, wood screw, thumb screw, carriage bolt, U-bolt, eye bolt, set screw, threaded rod, rivet, clevis pin, washers, retaining ring, standoff."],["Nuts","Hex, lock, jam, flange, cap, castle, wing, thumb, square, coupling, eye and T-slot nuts."],["Pipes and flanges","Tube, elbow, tee, cross, reducer, pipe cap, flange."],["Profiles and plates","I-beam, box tube, angle, C-channel, strut channel, T and Z profiles, T-slot extrusion, flat bar, round and hex bar, perforated and slotted plate, gusset, hinge."],["Machine parts","Gear, rack, sprocket, pulley, timing pulley, bearing, thrust bearing, flanged bearing, roller bearing, bushing, pillow block, shaft collar, stepped shaft, shaft coupler, linear rail, rail carriage, spring, handwheel, star knob, pull handle, levelling foot, wheel, enclosure, stepper motor."],["Electronics","Axial fan, heatsink, pin header, circuit board, micro servo."],["Brackets and connectors","Angle bracket, T-slot bracket, three-way corner, joining plate, shelf bracket, joist hanger, post base, column base plate, pipe clamp."]]],["p","The timing pulley is made for GT2 belts, with 10 to 80 teeth."],["h","Find a part"],["list",["{{Search…}} in the header filters all the shelves as you type, by name or by shelf.","The shelves are tabs in a row under the header. Click a tab to see one shelf at a time. {{All}} shows everything.","The parts you used last wait on a shelf called {{Recently used}} at the top, and under the tab {{Recent}}. Its {{Clear}} button forgets them.","Pick a part and press the star in the right half to keep it on the {{Favourites}} shelf. Press the star again to take it off.","Two buttons in the header switch between large and small tiles."]],["h","Look at a part first"],["steps",["Click a tile. A single click only picks the part. It does not add it.","The right half shows the part. Drag it to turn it about any axis, so you can roll it over and see it from underneath. Use the mouse wheel to zoom. Double-click to go back to the first view.","Under the name are its number of triangles and its size.","Below that are the same rows as the {{Shape parameters}} of a placed part (see below). Change a number there and the view follows."]],["p","What you set in the right half is what the part has when it goes in. The numbers you change stay until you close the app’s page."],["h","Add a part"],["steps",["For a fastener, first choose its thread size and length. They sit at the right of the row of tabs, beside the word {{Fasteners}}, and show only on the shelves they are for: open the tab {{Fasteners}} or {{Nuts}}. M6 and 24 mm to begin with. Every screw, nut and washer you add comes out in that size, even one you changed in the right half.","Add the part in one of three ways: double-click its tile, or press {{Add to scene}} in the right half, or drag the tile into the viewport.","A double-click or {{Add to scene}} puts the part on the selected part, or on the floor at the origin when nothing is selected.","When you drag, a see-through copy follows the pointer and shows where the part will stand: square to the face under the pointer, or upright on the floor. It has the parameters you set. Let go to drop it.","The part is there, with a row of its own in the parts tree."]],["h","Change it afterwards"],["steps",["Select the part.","Look at {{Properties}} in the right sidebar. It now has a section called {{Shape parameters}}.","Drag a number sideways to change it, or click it and type. Hold [[Shift]] to go ten times faster and [[Alt]] to go ten times finer.","The part rebuilds as you go."]],["h","Turn a shape into an ordinary mesh: Bake geo"],["p","A library part is built from its numbers, so the mesh tools cannot change it in place: a change to its parameters would build it again. When you want to work on its mesh, select the part and press {{Bake geo}}, the button under {{Shape parameters}} in {{Properties}}. The shape looks exactly as it did, but it is now an ordinary mesh part. The parameters go, and [Edit mesh](#edit-mesh), [Decimate](#decimate), [Split](#split) and [Remove holes](#fill-holes) work on it as they do on an imported part."],["p","Baking is one undo step, and [[Ctrl + Z]] gives the parameters back. The button is off while the part is locked. A part that has been baked cannot be resized with its parameters any more, so bake it last."],["h","A bolt with its own nut"],["p","A hex bolt, an Allen screw, a threaded rod or an eye bolt can carry a nut."],["steps",["Select the bolt.","In {{Shape parameters}}, switch on {{Nut on the bolt}}.","Choose the kind in {{Nut}}: a hex nut, a wing nut, a castle nut and so on.","Set {{Nut from the head}}: how far from the head the nut sits."]],["p","The nut is part of the bolt’s own shape. It follows the bolt’s size and length, stays one row in the tree, and cannot sit past the end of the thread."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-bolt-nut.webp","A hex bolt that carries its own nut: size, length and the nut’s distance from the head are all parameters."],["h","Other ways to add a shape"],["list",["The search: press [[Ctrl + K]], type `add flange` and press [[Enter]].","The add button on the bottom toolbar, next to the search: a click adds the shape you used last, and holding the button down opens a choice of shapes.","The start screen: pick Cube, Sphere, Cylinder or Torus to start a new scene with that shape."]],["h","Undo"],["p","Adding a part is an undo step, and so is each change to its parameters and each use of {{Bake geo}}."],["note","Library parts count like any other part: they have triangles, appear in the totals, and are saved and exported with the scene."],["see",["search-and-commands","move-rotate-scale","parts-tree","edit-mesh","align-parts"]]]},{"slug":"draw-lines","group":"look","title":"Draw lines and shapes","summary":"Draw curves, rectangles, circles and polygons on the floor or on a face, edit their points, and use them as paths.","blocks":[["p","MeshOptimiser can draw lines. A line is a curve you place yourself, point by point, or a ready-made shape such as a rectangle or a circle. It is a part of the scene like any other: it has a row in the parts tree, can be hidden, renamed, grouped and moved with the gizmo, and every change can be undone. Lines have no triangles; they are there to look at, to measure against, and to sweep a profile along (see [Sweep](#sweep))."],["h","Start drawing"],["steps",["Press [[L]], or click {{Draw}} on the bottom toolbar. The card opens bottom left in the viewport.","Choose a tool at the top of the card, and the kind of curve: {{Bezier}}, {{Cubic}}, {{B-spline}} or {{Linear}}.","Click in the viewport. The keys that do something right now are listed at the bottom right.","Press [[Esc]] to put the tool away."]],["h","The tools"],["terms",[["Pen","Click for a point; drag to pull out handles and make a curve (Bezier). [[Enter]] finishes the line, [[C]] closes it, and a click on the first point closes it too. [[Backspace]] takes back the last point."],["Rectangle","Click one corner, move, click again to place it. [[Shift]] makes a square and [[Alt]] draws from the centre."],["Circle","Click the centre, move, click again. The ellipse switch gives it two radii."],["Arc","Three clicks: the start, the end, and a point the arc passes through."],["Polygon","Click the centre, move, click again. The arrow keys change the number of sides; the card has Star and Around the circle."],["Freehand","Draw with the mouse held down; the stroke is smoothed into a curve when you let go."],["Edit","Change a line that is already there (see below)."]]],["p","While a rectangle, circle or polygon waits for its second click you can type its size, for example `20 x 10`, and press [[Enter]]."],["h","Where it draws"],["terms",[["Ground, Front, Side","The three flat planes of the scene."],["View","A plane that faces the camera."],["Surface","The face of a part: the first click picks the face, and the line is drawn on it."],["Offset","Moves the plane along its normal, in millimetres."]]],["h","Snapping"],["p","With {{Snap to grid}} on, a field of dots fades in around the pointer, and a click a few pixels from a dot lands exactly on it. {{Grid step}} is Auto (it follows the zoom) or a number you type. {{Snap to points}} snaps to the points of other lines. Holding [[Shift]] snaps the angle in steps of 15°. The dots hide themselves when they would crowd, for example when you look at the plane from far away or edge-on."],["h","Edit a line"],["steps",["Choose {{Edit}} in the card and click a line (or pick it in the tree).","Drag a point or a handle. The curve follows while you drag.","Click on the curve to add a point. Double-click a point to make it smooth or sharp. [[Delete]] removes the selected point and [[C]] opens or closes the line.","Hold [[Ctrl]] while you drag a handle to move it on its own; the other handle of that point stays where it was, and stays free afterwards.","The buttons in the card close or open the line, reverse it, simplify it, make a point smooth or sharp, round a corner (or all of them), or add a mirrored copy."]],["p","Bezier handles snap to the grid like points do."],["h","Round or cut the corners"],["p","A rectangle or polygon that is still a shape shows a small ring on its first corner in Edit. Drag it along the diagonal to round or cut every corner at once; it snaps to the grid and the card says how far the corner reaches. Click {{Round}} or {{Bevel}} next to the ring to change the kind: a true arc, or a straight cut. The same two settings are in the card and in Properties."],["h","Properties of a line"],["p","Select a line and look at {{Properties}} in the right sidebar."],["terms",[["Shape","For a rectangle, circle, ellipse, polygon or star that has not had its points moved: width, height, radius, sides, star and inner radius, corner type and size. A rectangle grows about its centre."],["Spline","Type (Bezier, Cubic, B-spline, Linear), Close Spline, and {{Interpolation}}, which says how the curve is cut into the short straight pieces that are drawn: None keeps only the points, Natural and Uniform put a number of points on each piece, Adaptive adds points until no corner turns by more than the Angle, and Subdivided does that and also keeps every piece shorter than the Maximum length. The settings that do not apply are greyed."]]],["p","Once you move a point of a shape by hand it becomes an ordinary spline, and the Shape card goes away."],["note","Lines are written into GLB, glTF and saved scenes. The other export formats have no lines and leave them out."],["see",["sweep","organise-the-tree","move-rotate-scale"]]]},{"slug":"sweep","group":"look","title":"Sweep a profile along a line","summary":"Turn a path and a profile into a solid part: a pipe, a rail, a ring.","blocks":[["p","Sweep carries a shape (the profile) along a line (the path) and makes a solid part of it. A circle along a curve makes a pipe, a rectangle along a straight line makes a bar, and a closed path makes a ring."],["steps",["Draw the path with the Draw tool (see [Draw lines and shapes](#draw-lines)). If the profile should be a drawn shape, draw that too.","Open the Sweep card from the search: press [[Ctrl + K]], type `sweep` and choose {{Sweep…}}. It has no button on the bottom toolbar.","Pick the {{Path}} in the list.","Choose the {{Profile}}: a {{Circle}} (give its size), a {{Rectangle}} (width and height) or another {{Line}} from the list.","Press {{Sweep}}."]],["p","The result is a normal part called Sweep, with a row in the tree, and one undo step."],["h","Advanced"],["terms",[["Scale","How big the profile is at the start and at the end, as a percentage. 100 and 50 make a pipe that narrows."],["Twist","How far the profile turns along the path, in degrees."],["Close the ends","Puts a lid on each end so the part is solid."],["Hard-edge angle","Corners sharper than this are kept sharp; gentler ones are shaded smooth."]]],["note","The sweep follows the line as it is drawn, as short straight pieces. A line with a coarse Interpolation (see Properties of a line) gives a coarse pipe."],["see",["draw-lines","organise-the-tree","cleanup"]]]},{"slug":"screenshots","group":"look","title":"Screenshots","summary":"Save a picture of the viewport as a PNG, at the size of the view or at any resolution up to poster size.","blocks":[["steps",["Set up the view: orbit to the angle you want and choose a view mode.","Click the camera button at the right end of the top bar. The screen flashes like a shutter and the {{Save screenshot}} window opens.","Choose a resolution.","Check the file name, or type your own.","Press {{Save…}}. Where the browser allows it, you choose the folder. Otherwise the picture goes to your downloads folder."]],["h","What the options mean"],["terms",[["Resolution","Six presets: the viewport at 1×, 2× (sharp) or 4× (poster), and 1080p, 1440p and 4K."],["Custom (px)","Any width and height, from 16 to 16,384 pixels a side. The aspect ratio and the number of megapixels are shown beside it."],["View mode","{{Current}} uses the view mode that is on. {{Solid}}, {{Wireframe}} and {{X-ray}} use that mode for the picture only."],["Hide scene grid","Leaves the floor lines and the origin axes out of this picture."],["Filename","Starts as the model’s name and the time."],["Stamp info on image","Prints a small panel in a corner of the picture. Tick what it should show: model name, statistics, selection counts, timestamp, camera, units, and the app’s name. Then pick the corner."]]],["p","The picture is a PNG file. Right-clicking an empty spot of the viewport also offers {{Save screenshot…}}."],["note","A 4× picture of a large viewport is a very large file. The limit is 16,384 pixels a side."],["see",["view-modes","move-the-view","exploded-view"]]]},{"slug":"save-scene","group":"save","title":"Save a scene","summary":"Save your work as a scene file so you can carry on later. What a scene file is, how to name it with the file-name buttons, and how it differs from an export.","blocks":[["p","Saving a scene keeps your work so you can open it again and carry on. Exporting writes a file for another program. Do both: save a scene for yourself, export for whoever gets the result."],["h","What a scene file is"],["p","A scene file is a `.glb` file. It holds the model as it is now, after everything you deleted, reduced and recoloured. It also remembers things only MeshOptimiser uses: the camera, the view settings, which parts were hidden or flagged, and your measurements. Other programs can open the file as an ordinary GLB and ignore the rest."],["h","Save"],["steps",["Press [[Ctrl + S]]. You can also click the dot on the scene’s tab, or choose {{Menu}}, then {{Save scene…}}.","The {{Save scene}} window opens. At the top it says what is about to be written: how many parts, how many triangles and about how big the file will be.","Check the {{Filename}}, or type your own. The ending `.glb` is added for you. The buttons under the name can build it for you (see below).","Look at the options under the name, and press {{Save…}}.","Where the browser allows it, a file window opens. Choose a folder and confirm. Otherwise the file goes to your downloads folder.","The dot on the tab turns green."]],["p","Saving again under the same name writes to the same file without asking where. The window then says “Saves over” and the name of the file, and the button reads {{Save}}."],["h","Build the file name"],["p","Under the name are small buttons. Each one adds a piece to the name. Press it again and the piece comes off. The numbers are those of what is about to be written, and they follow the options below, so the name stays true."],["terms",[["+ date","Today’s date, like `2026-10-08`."],["+ time","The time now, like `1432h`."],["+ version","A version number like `v2`. Press it again to raise the number. Hold [[Shift]] while you press to take it off."],["+ triangles","How many triangles the file holds, like `17k-tris`."],["+ parts","How many parts, like `67-parts`."],["+ size","About how big the file is, like `370KB`."],["+ saving","How much lighter than the file you opened, like `-42pct`. It shows only when the new file is lighter."],["+ optimised","Adds the word `optimised`, to mark the file as the optimised one."],["+ selection","Adds the word `selection`, to mark the file as a part of the scene."],["Source name","Starts again from the name of the file you opened."]]],["p","A button that is in use shows a minus sign instead of the plus. You can also type in the name by hand."],["h","The options"],["terms",[["Whole scene","On: every part. Off: only the selected parts. It is dimmed when nothing is selected, and says how many parts are selected."],["Include hidden parts","Hidden parts are written too, and come back hidden when you open the scene again. It says how many are hidden."],["Remember the view and settings","Stores the camera, the display settings, flags and measurements in the file. Other programs ignore them."],["Save as a copy","Writes the file without making it this scene’s file. The scene keeps its unsaved mark, and the next save goes where it went before."]]],["p","A file that holds only some of the scene (only the selection, or no hidden parts) is never the scene’s own file. The app turns on {{Save as a copy}} for you."],["h","Know whether you have saved"],["p","The dot on the tab is yellow while there are changes that are not saved, and green when the scene is saved. Other tabs show a yellow dot too when they have unsaved changes. Closing a tab or quitting with unsaved changes asks first."],["h","Open a scene again"],["p","Open the `.glb` like any other file: drop it on the window, press [[Ctrl + O]], or pick it from the recent files on the start screen. The camera and the view settings come back with it, and hidden parts are still hidden."],["h","Scene or export?"],["terms",[["Save scene","Everything, including hidden parts, at the scene’s own scale and orientation. For continuing your work."],["Export","What you choose, in the format, scale and orientation the next program needs. For handing over."]]],["note","Your original file is never overwritten by a save, unless you choose its name and folder yourself. Give the scene a name of its own."],["see",["export","scene-tabs","undo-redo","crash-recovery"]]]},{"slug":"crash-recovery","group":"save","title":"Get your scene back after a crash","summary":"While a scene has changes that are not saved, a copy is kept inside your browser. If the app crashes, it offers the scene back the next time it starts.","blocks":[["p","A crash, a browser that is killed or a power cut used to take the scene you were working on with it, because a scene is only a file once you save it. Now the app keeps a recovery copy while there is something to lose, and offers it back when the app comes up again."],["h","What is kept, and when"],["list",["A copy of the scene, the same file {{Save scene}} would write, with the camera and the view. It is stored inside the browser, on this computer, and goes nowhere else.","Only while the scene has changes that are not saved, and only when something changed since the last copy. Every minute, every 2 minutes (the default) or every 5 minutes, as you choose in {{Settings}}, {{General}}, {{Recovery}}.","Not while you are dragging, and not while a file is loading. It also takes one when the browser tab goes to the background, because that is where trouble is likeliest.","When the app hits a string of errors, or the graphics card is lost, it takes one straight away.","A big scene takes a moment to copy, so it is copied less often. Each scene tab has a copy of its own; up to six are kept, for 14 days."]],["p","Saving the scene clears the copy, and so does undoing back to the saved state. Closing a scene tab on purpose, or closing or reloading the window and choosing to leave without saving, does not bring the scene back: the copy is for a crash. If the browser has no room left for copies, the app says so and pauses them."],["h","When the app comes back"],["steps",["The app notices that the last run did not end properly and says {{MeshOptimiser closed unexpectedly last time}}, with the name of the scene and how long ago the copy was made.","Press {{Recover}} and the scene opens as a scene with changes that are not saved, with the unit and up axis it had. Press {{Reopen last file}} for the file you had open, {{Safe mode}} if the crashes keep coming, {{Delete the copy}} to let it go, or {{Not now}} to leave it for later.","The same scene is on the start screen, first in the list of recent files: a wide yellow card tagged {{Continue}} (more than one are tagged {{Recovered}}), marked “not saved”. Click it to bring the scene back, or the × to delete the copy.","Save the recovered scene to keep it. Until you do, it stays a scene with changes that are not saved."]],["h","Turn it off"],["p","In {{Settings}}, {{General}}, {{Recovery}}, choose {{Off}}. {{Recovery copies}} under {{Storage}} deletes the copies that are kept. The online version keeps none."],["note","A recovery copy is not a backup. It lives in the browser that made it: clearing the site’s data deletes it, and another browser or another computer cannot see it. Save the scene to a file as you go."],["see",["save-scene","settings","troubleshooting","undo-redo"]]]},{"slug":"export","group":"save","title":"Export","summary":"Write the model to GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ, STL, PLY or a parts list, name the file, and set the scale, up axis and origin the next program needs.","blocks":[["h","Steps"],["steps",["Click {{Export}} at the right of the top bar, or press [[Ctrl + E]]. A list of formats drops down. The button is dimmed while no model is open.","Choose a format. The Export window opens with that format selected. You can still change it in the list on the left.","Check the file name. Set the options. For a first try, the defaults are fine.","Press the button at the bottom right, for example {{Export GLB}}.","The file arrives as a download in your browser, normally in your downloads folder. A message confirms it."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-export.webp","Export to GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ, STL, PLY, or a parts list."],["h","The window"],["p","The list of formats is at the left, in groups: 3D formats, 3D printing, point cloud and data. The rest of the window is for the format you chose. From the top:"],["terms",[["Format and Compare","The name of the format and a line about it. {{Compare}} opens a sheet that shows what each format keeps."],["What will be written","Three figures that follow your options: how many parts, how many triangles and about how big the file will be. For a parts list: rows, columns and size."],["File name","The name the file gets, with the ending for the format. The buttons under it build the name (see below)."],["Size and position","Unit scale, up axis and origin."],["Parts","Which parts go in the file, and how they are arranged."],["Format options","Only the ones that fit the format."],["Bottom line","Says what the file will be called, for example “Saves as rd-fine.glb”. For OBJ it adds that a `.mtl` file with the colours comes with it. If no part matches your options, it says so and the export button is dimmed."]]],["p","The choices you make for scale, axis, origin and the parts switches are kept for the next export. The file name and {{Selected parts only}} are not."],["h","The formats"],["terms",[["GLB","Modern binary glTF, in one file. The app calls it best for the web and Cinema 4D."],["glTF","The same as GLB, written as text (JSON glTF 2.0)."],["FBX","Binary FBX 7.4. The app lists Blender, Maya, 3ds Max, Houdini and Unreal."],["USDZ","For Apple Quick Look and Reality Composer."],["OBJ","Wavefront OBJ. Universal, geometry only. The colours go in a `.mtl` file beside it."],["STL","Binary STL. The standard for 3D printing."],["PLY","Geometry with vertex colours."],["CSV","A parts list as a spreadsheet: name, colour, triangles, vertices, volume, size, and whether the part is flagged, visible or locked. No geometry is written."]]],["p","[Which format should I choose](#choose-format) helps you pick."],["h","Name the file"],["p","Type a name, or let the buttons under it build one. They are the same as in [Save a scene](#save-scene). Each button adds one piece to the name, and a second press takes it off. The counts and the size are those of what is about to be written, and they change when you change an option."],["terms",[["+ date, + time","Today’s date, or the time now."],["+ version","A version number. Press it again to raise it. Hold [[Shift]] to take it off."],["+ triangles, + parts, + size","The triangle count, the number of parts and the approximate file size."],["+ saving","How much lighter than the file you opened. It shows only when the export is lighter."],["+ optimised, + selection","The words `optimised` and `selection`."],["Source name","Starts again from the name of the file you opened."]]],["p","The ending is added for you, so leave it out. Characters a file name cannot have are replaced with `_`. When the name is empty, the scene’s own name is used."],["h","Size and position"],["terms",[["Unit scale","Multiplies every size in the file. {{1.0 (no change)}} keeps the model as it is. CAD models are usually in millimetres. If the next program works in metres, choose {{× 0.001 (mm → m)}}. The list also has cm to m, m to mm, m to cm, inches to mm, mm to inches, and {{Custom…}}."],["Custom scale","The multiplier, when {{Unit scale}} is on {{Custom…}}."],["Up axis","{{Keep as in the scene}} writes the model the way it stands. {{Turn Z-up into Y-up}} turns the model so Y is up, which is what glTF, OBJ and Maya expect. A model that already stands Y up, such as one that came from Cinema 4D or from a glTF, must be kept as it is. Turning it lays it on its side."],["Origin","{{Keep model origin}} leaves the model where it is. {{Recentre to bbox centre}} puts the middle of the model at 0,0,0. {{Centre XY, drop to floor (Z=0)}} centres it and stands it on the floor."]]],["p","The up axis and the scale you set in {{Settings}}, {{Scene}} only change how the scene is shown on screen. They do not go into the file. The two choices in this window decide what the file says."],["h","Parts"],["terms",[["Visible parts only","Leaves hidden parts out of the file. On by default."],["Selected parts only","Writes only the parts that are selected, shown or not. It overrides the switch above."],["Merge into one mesh","Writes the whole model as one object. Useful for 3D printing. The group tree is lost."],["Flatten groups","Writes every part at the top level, without the group folders. Handy for programs that stumble on groups inside groups. It is shown for the formats that have a tree (GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ), and it is dimmed when you merge into one mesh."]]],["h","Format options"],["terms",[["Draco compression","GLB only. Compresses the mesh data, which typically makes the file 5 to 20 times smaller. Exporting and loading are slower."],["Meshopt compression","GLB only. Compresses the vertex data with Meshopt and rounds positions, normals and UVs to a fixed number of bits. It is quick to decode and streams well, and the file is about a quarter of the plain size. Draco is smaller still (about a fifth, measured on a CAD assembly), and the gap narrows once the server compresses the file too. The viewer must read it: three.js, Babylon.js and model-viewer do, some others do not, and Draco is the safer choice for those. The file is saved as `name.meshopt.glb`."],["Faster to draw","GLB only. Puts the triangles and the vertices in the order the graphics card likes, so the model draws faster. The shape and every attribute stay exactly as they are, and any viewer opens the file. Meshopt compression does this too. If you tick Draco as well, Draco is used."],["ASCII FBX","FBX only. Writes the FBX as text instead of binary. Try it if the next program cannot read the binary file."],["ASCII STL","STL only. Writes the STL as readable text. It is much larger. Use it only for a program that cannot read binary STL."]]],["p","For a parts list, the options about scale, axis, origin and merging are left out, because no geometry is written."],["h","Good to know"],["list",["The export contains the model as it is now: deleted parts are gone and reduced meshes are reduced.","It runs in solid view and switches back afterwards, so a view mode never ends up in the file.","After an export, the {{Optimisation report}} shows the size of the exported file beside the size of the file you opened.","If the file you opened was compressed (Draco, Meshopt or quantised), the window says so, because an export without compression is two to twenty times larger.","Draco compression needs an internet connection the first time, because the app fetches its compression code on demand. Meshopt compression and Faster to draw do too: the first time, the app fetches glTF Transform. If that fails, it saves a plain GLB and says so.","A very large model can be too big for a text format such as OBJ. The app then suggests GLB or STL, which are binary and much smaller.","An FBX says which unit it uses and which way is up, so the next program can read the size and the direction correctly. It also shares one material between all the parts of the same colour, which makes the file quicker to open.","OBJ keeps parts that share a name apart, by giving them a number: `name`, `name.001` and so on."]],["note","A common mistake is the wrong scale. If the model arrives a thousand times too big or too small in the next program, export again with a different {{Unit scale}}."],["note","Another is a model that arrives lying on its back. Export again with the other {{Up axis}}."],["see",["choose-format","save-scene","file-formats"]]]},{"slug":"choose-format","group":"save","title":"Which format should I choose","summary":"A short guide by purpose: the web, Cinema 4D, Blender, game engines, AR on an iPhone, 3D printing, or a parts list.","blocks":[["p","If you are not sure, export GLB. It is one file, it is compact, and it keeps the parts tree, the materials and the colours. The app’s own format sheet calls it the best all-round choice."],["terms",[["A web page or a web viewer","GLB. Switch on {{Draco compression}} only if the viewer you use can read it."],["Cinema 4D","GLB. The app recommends it for Cinema 4D 2026."],["Blender, Maya, 3ds Max, Houdini","FBX. These are the programs the app lists for it."],["Unreal","FBX. The app lists Unreal under FBX."],["Unity","The app does not name Unity for any format. FBX is the usual choice for game engines."],["AR on an iPhone or iPad","USDZ, the format of Apple Quick Look."],["3D printing","STL. Switch on {{Merge into one mesh}} if the printer software wants one object."],["A program that reads nothing else","OBJ. It is understood almost everywhere, but it carries no hierarchy: the tree is flattened into the names of the parts."],["Research or scanning tools","PLY, which holds geometry and a colour for every vertex."],["A list of parts for a spreadsheet","CSV."],["Carrying on later in MeshOptimiser","Not an export at all: [Save a scene](#save-scene)."]]],["h","What each format keeps"],["terms",[["GLB and glTF","The parts tree, full materials, vertex colours."],["FBX","The parts tree and materials. No vertex colours."],["USDZ","The parts tree and materials. No vertex colours."],["OBJ","Materials through a separate `.mtl` file. A flat list of parts, no tree."],["STL","Geometry only, as a single mesh. No colours."],["PLY","Geometry as a single mesh, with vertex colours."],["CSV","No geometry."]]],["h","Two settings that go with the format"],["list",["Scale. A CAD model is usually in millimetres. Many 3D programs and the glTF format work in metres. If the model arrives huge, export with {{× 0.001 (mm → m)}}.","Up axis. CAD uses Z as up. glTF, OBJ and Maya use Y. If the model arrives lying down, export with {{Turn Z-up into Y-up}}."]],["note","These are safe starting points. The program you are exporting to has the last word, so do one test export before you spend time on the model."],["see",["export","file-formats","save-scene"]]]},{"slug":"convert-step-to-glb","group":"jobs","title":"How to convert a STEP file to GLB","summary":"Convert a STEP CAD file to a small GLB for the web, on your own computer and for free. The model is never uploaded. Drop it in, check it, export.","blocks":[["p","A STEP file is how engineers share a design. A web page cannot show one. GLB is the format it can show: a compact 3D file that browsers, AR viewers and most 3D programs open. This page takes you from one to the other with MeshOptimiser, on your own computer. Nothing is uploaded, and there is nothing to pay."],["h","What you need"],["list",["MeshOptimiser, started once. See [What you need](#requirements) and [Start it the first time](#first-start).","Your model as a STEP file (`.step` or `.stp`). If it is still in your CAD program, see [Native CAD files: export a STEP file first](#cad-files-step-first)."]],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Start the app with `start.bat` (Windows) or `start.command` (macOS).","Drag the STEP file onto the window.","In the {{Import settings}} window, leave everything as it is and press {{Import}}. {{Standard}} under {{Surface quality}} is the recommended choice. See [Open your first file](#first-file).","Wait while the progress card works. A big assembly can take several minutes. Repeated parts are found and stored once, and the result is compressed. Open the same file again later, with the same settings, and it appears at once: the converted model is kept.","Look at the model. {{Properties}} on the right shows how heavy it is. Press [[Alt + 1]] for the normal view.","Press [[Ctrl + E]] and choose {{GLB}}. In the Export window, check {{Unit scale}} and {{Up axis}} (below).","Press {{Export GLB}}. The file arrives in your downloads folder."]],["h","Two settings that save a second try"],["terms",[["Unit scale","CAD models are usually in millimetres, and glTF and most web viewers expect metres. If the model shows up a thousand times too big in the viewer, export again with {{× 0.001 (mm → m)}}."],["Up axis","CAD uses Z as up. glTF uses Y. If the model lies on its back in the viewer, export with {{Y-up (glTF, OBJ, Maya)}}."]]],["p","Leave {{Draco compression}} off unless the viewer you use can read it. See [Which format should I choose](#choose-format)."],["h","Smoother curves, or fewer triangles"],["p","How round a hole looks, and how heavy the GLB is, is decided when the CAD surfaces are turned into triangles. That step is called tessellation. In the {{Import settings}} window, the four choices under {{Surface quality}} set it for you. Open {{Tessellation}} under them to see the numbers, or to type your own."],["terms",[["Sag","How far a triangle may stand off the true surface, in millimetres. Smaller is smoother, with more triangles."],["Angle","The most a curve may turn from one triangle to the next, in degrees. Smaller means rounder holes and fillets."],["Min edge","The smallest triangle edge allowed. It keeps tiny fillets and threads from filling up with slivers. 0 is no limit."],["Coarse, Standard, Fine, Ultra","Each sets a sag and an angle: 1 mm and 30°, 0.5 mm and 30°, 0.2 mm and 20°, 0.05 mm and 12°. The finer choices tighten the angle too, which is what rounds small holes."],["Custom","Selected as soon as you type a sag or an angle of your own."],["By part size","Small, medium and large parts each get a sag and an angle, so the housing comes in fine and the screws coarse. You set where small ends and large begins, as a share of the model’s size."],["Smooth shading","Writes the normals of the real CAD surfaces into the GLB. A fillet runs into the faces beside it without a visible seam, and sharp edges stay sharp. On by default."],["Swap Y and Z","In the same window, under Clean-up. For a file that comes in lying on its side."]]],["p","If the model is too heavy, try a coarser choice here before you reduce triangles afterwards. Fewer triangles at the source usually look better than a fine mesh that was thinned out."],["h","How small will it be?"],["p","It depends on the model. The app’s README gives one example: a 320 MB STEP file became an 11 MB compressed GLB. Assemblies with many identical parts, such as bolts, shrink the most, because each shape is stored once. That is an example, not a promise for every file."],["p","If the GLB is still too heavy, remove what nobody will see before you export. [A sensible order to work in](#workflow) shows how, and [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget) takes a triangle count for the whole scene."],["h","Without opening the viewer"],["p","To convert a file and do nothing else, drag it onto `step2glb.bat` (Windows) or `step2glb.command` (macOS). The GLB is written next to it. Or use the command line:"],["code","python step2glb.py input.step --meshopt"],["p","The command line is described in [The command-line converter](#command-line). Meshopt compression needs the free tool gltfpack on your PATH. If a viewer cannot open a Meshopt-compressed GLB, convert again with `--no-meshopt`, or export from the app instead."],["note","A common mistake is to send the file from the `inbox` folder. That folder is where the app keeps its working copy. The file you export is the one to send, because it has your clean-up in it."],["h","Next"],["p","To see what the conversion did, read [How to read the numbers](#read-the-numbers). For the file formats in detail, see [Export](#export)."],["see",["first-file","export","workflow"]]]},{"slug":"remove-bolts-from-step-assembly","group":"jobs","title":"How to remove bolts and screws from a STEP assembly","summary":"Take the bolts, screws, nuts and washers out of a CAD assembly before it goes on the web. Select one, select the rest, check, delete, and undo if needed.","blocks":[["p","Fasteners are a large part of a CAD assembly. A model can hold hundreds of bolts, nuts and washers, and each carries thousands of triangles that nobody sees from a normal viewing distance. Taking them out makes the model much lighter. It also leaves your hardware out of the file you share."],["p","On the rear derailleur used in these pictures, twelve bolts and screws held 129,460 triangles, about 12% of the whole model."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-fasteners.webp","Bolts and screws found in the rear derailleur: twelve parts, 129,460 triangles. They were found with the Fasteners tool."],["p","The quickest way is the {{Fasteners}} card on the bottom toolbar: it finds bolts, screws, nuts and washers by their shape, whatever they are called, and selects them. The steps below do the same by hand, one kind at a time."],["h","The steps by hand"],["steps",["Open the STEP file. See [Open your first file](#first-file).","Click one bolt in the viewport or in the parts tree.","In the card {{Selection & actions}} in the right sidebar, press {{Select similar}}. Every part with the same shape as the bolt is selected, so all copies of that bolt are selected at once.","Press [[S]] to isolate the selection. Look at it. Only the bolts should be left on screen.","Press [[S]] again to come back, then press [[Del]] to delete them.","Do the same for the next kind of bolt, then for the nuts and the washers. Bolts of different lengths are different shapes, so each size is one round.","Press [[Alt + H]] to show everything, and look at the model once more."]],["p","Every delete is one undo step. [[Ctrl + Z]] brings the bolts back. The {{Optimisation report}} shows how many triangles you saved."],["h","Other ways to find the hardware"],["terms",[["By name","In {{Selection & actions}}, press {{By name}} and type `bolt|screw|nut|washer`. It selects every part with one of those words in its name, in capitals or not. This only works when the CAD names are honest, and they often are not: `Part_0031` tells you nothing. A short word such as nut can also match other names, so isolate before you delete."],["Delete small parts","Removes every part below a size you set, with a preview. Hardware is small, but so are other things. Press {{Isolate}} and look before you delete. See [Delete small parts](#delete-small-parts)."],["Select hidden parts","Finds the screws inside a housing, because nobody can see them. To keep hidden structure and remove only hardware, type part of the name in {{Name contains}}. See [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts)."]]],["p","The next version of the app adds a Fasteners tool that finds bolts, screws, nuts and washers by their shape, whatever they are called. It is in the project’s repository and not in the released version, so this page does not rely on it."],["h","When you want the bolts to stay"],["p","If the bolts must be visible but light, do not delete them. Select them the same way, then use [Smart fit](#smart-fit) with {{Cylinder}}. A bolt becomes a plain cylinder of the same size in the same place."],["h","After they are gone"],["list",["Bolt holes stay in the plates they held. [Remove holes](#fill-holes) closes holes in flat faces, so the surfaces look whole again.","Take out the bolts that hold a cover only if you do not mind a cover that seems to float. From outside it usually looks fine."]],["note","Hide ([[H]]) is not delete. A hidden part is still in the scene, and whether it is written to a file depends on the export options. To be sure a part is not in the file, delete it."],["h","Next"],["p","To remove hardware and everything else that is hidden before you send a model to someone, read [How to make a CAD model safe to send to a customer](#make-model-safe-to-send)."],["see",["select-parts","delete-small-parts","select-hidden-parts","smart-fit"]]]},{"slug":"make-model-safe-to-send","group":"jobs","title":"How to make a CAD model safe to send to a customer","summary":"Show your product without handing over your design. Remove hidden parts and fasteners, simplify what is left, clean the names and export a small GLB.","blocks":[["p","A customer, a dealer or a web visitor needs to see your product. They do not need the screws inside it, the board under the cover or the exact shape of every internal part. This page takes a STEP assembly and turns it into a small GLB that shows the outside and little else."],["note","No file is a guarantee. Anyone can measure what they can see. This page makes the file show less, which is a good habit. It does not stop a determined person from copying what is left, and it is no replacement for an agreement with the customer."],["h","What a GLB already leaves out"],["p","A GLB holds triangles, colours and the names of parts. It has no feature tree, no sketches, no dimensions and no history of how the model was built. A STEP file has the exact surfaces. A GLB has an approximation made of small flat triangles."],["h","Steps"],["steps",["Export a STEP file from your CAD program and open it. See [Native CAD files: export a STEP file first](#cad-files-step-first).","Decide what the customer should see. Usually that is the closed product, seen from outside.","Remove what is inside. Use {{Select hidden parts}}, press {{Isolate}} to check what it found, and press {{Delete}}. See [Select hidden parts](#select-hidden-parts).","Remove the hardware: bolts, screws, nuts and washers. See [How to remove bolts and screws from a STEP assembly](#remove-bolts-from-step-assembly).","Simplify the parts whose shape is the secret. [Smart fit](#smart-fit) can swap a part for a box, a cylinder, a few boxes or blocks that keep its outline. {{Remove holes}} closes bolt holes and pockets.","Bring the weight down to a number with [Fit to budget](#fit-to-budget), if the file must be small.","Clean the names (below).","Export a GLB. See [Export](#export) and the settings below.","Check the exported file (below)."]],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-select-hidden-result.webp","Select hidden parts: one part inside the housing can’t be seen from any side. It is selected and ready to delete."],["h","Clean the names"],["p","Part names travel into the GLB. Names like `Gearbox_internal_v7_ACME` say more than a picture does. Select the parts and press [[F2]] to rename many at once, or use {{Flatten}} to remove levels of grouping that show how the product is built. See [The parts tree](#parts-tree)."],["p","Material names travel too. Look at them in the materials dock ([[Shift + M]]), and see [Colours and materials](#colours-materials)."],["p","Leave the extra information off when you convert a STEP file. In the import settings, keep {{Layer attributes}} and {{Validation properties (mass, area)}} switched off, so layers and per-part volume and area are not read in the first place."],["h","Export settings"],["list",["Format: GLB.","Keep {{Visible parts only}} on, which is the default.","Better still, delete what you do not want to share instead of only hiding it. A deleted part is gone from the file for certain.","Choose {{Unit scale}} and {{Up axis}} for the viewer your customer will use. See [How to convert a STEP file to GLB](#convert-step-to-glb)."]],["h","Check the file before you send it"],["steps",["Drop the exported GLB onto the app window. A file never replaces a scene that has something in it, so it opens in a new tab.","Open the parts tree and read every name.","Press [[Alt + 3]] for X-ray and look inside. Nothing should be there that you took out.","Look at {{Properties}} and the {{Optimisation report}}. The parts and triangle counts should match what you expect."]],["h","What to send, and what not to send"],["list",["Send the file you exported, and only that.","Do not send the STEP file. It has the exact surfaces.","Do not send a saved scene. It keeps your view, your selection and your recolouring, and it is meant for you.","Do not send files from the `inbox` folder. Next to each converted GLB the app keeps a small `.params.json` record that includes the path of the original file on your disk."]],["h","What this does not do"],["list",["It removes whole parts, not the inside of a part. A housing keeps its inner walls, because the tool selects parts, not faces.","It does not hide what is visible. The outside of your product is in the file, and anyone can measure it.","It does not encrypt or lock the file."]],["see",["select-hidden-parts","smart-fit","export","privacy-offline"]]]},{"slug":"shortcuts","group":"reference","title":"Keyboard shortcuts","summary":"Every keyboard shortcut and mouse action in MeshOptimiser v0.24.0, grouped by what it does.","blocks":[["p","On macOS, use [[⌘]] wherever this page says [[Ctrl]]."],["p","Inside the app, press [[?]] for the same list with a search field and a picture of the keyboard. Shortcuts with a single key do nothing while you are typing in a field, or while a window is open in front. This page lists the keys the app comes with. If you have changed some (see “Change a key” below), the app shows yours."],["img","Images/apps/meshoptimiser-shortcuts.webp","The keyboard shortcuts overlay, with search."],["h","File"],["keys",[["Alt + N","New scene, in a new tab. A browser keeps Ctrl + N for itself, to open a new browser window. In the installed app, Ctrl + N works too."],["Ctrl + O","Open a file"],["Ctrl + Shift + O","Import a file into the open scene"],["Ctrl + S","Save the scene"],["Ctrl + E","Export"],["Ctrl + Shift + F4","Close all scenes. It asks once about the ones with unsaved changes."],["Ctrl + ;","Scene settings"]]],["h","Edit"],["keys",[["Ctrl + Z","Undo"],["Ctrl + Y","Redo"],["Ctrl + Shift + Z","Redo"],["Ctrl + C","Copy the selected parts"],["Ctrl + V","Paste"],["Ctrl + D","Duplicate the selection"],["Del","Delete the selection. Backspace does the same."],["Ctrl + G","Group"],["Ctrl + Shift + G","Ungroup"],["Ctrl + M","Merge the selected parts into one"],["F2","Batch rename"]]],["h","Tools"],["keys",[["X","Split"],["P","Remove holes"],["Ctrl + B","Smart fit"],["U","Untriangulate the selection"],["Shift + U","Triangulate the selection"],["B","Quick remesh: pick how many triangles stay, under the pointer"],["M","Measure"],["L","Draw lines and shapes"],["Enter","Run the open command card"],["Esc","Put the open command card away. A second press clears the selection."]]],["p","The key of a tool also closes its card when the card is already open. [[U]] and [[Shift + U]] work on the selection only. See [Remesh](#remesh), which also describes Quick remesh."],["p","Smart optimise, Decimate, Fit to budget, Remove floating pieces, Sweep, Select hidden parts, Fasteners, Stacked copies and Align to floor have no key of their own. Find them on the toolbar, in the `…` list or with the search."],["h","Selection"],["keys",[["Ctrl + A","Select all"],["Ctrl + I","Invert the selection"],["Esc","Select nothing"],["Shift + S","Find the selected part in the tree"],["Ctrl + Click","In the tree: add a row to the selection or take it out. A group goes in or out with its parts."],["Shift + Click","In the tree: pick every row from the last one you clicked to this one, groups and parts alike."],["↑ ↓","After a click in the tree: go to the row above or below. With [[Shift]], add a row to the selection."],["→ ←","After a click in the tree: open a group, or close it. On a part, [[←]] goes to the group that holds it."],["Home End","After a click in the tree: go to the first or the last row."]]],["h","Visibility"],["keys",[["H","Hide the selection"],["Shift + H","Hide the rest"],["Alt + H","Show all"],["S","Isolate the selection, and back"]]],["h","View"],["keys",[["F","Frame the selection, or the whole model"],["Alt + 1","Solid"],["Alt + 2","Wireframe"],["Alt + 3","X-ray"],["Alt + 4","Heatmap"],["5","Clay, and back to solid"],["6","CAD view, and back to solid"],["Ctrl + 1","Camera view (perspective)"],["Ctrl + 2","Top view"],["Ctrl + 3","Front view"],["Ctrl + 4","Side view"],["G","Show or hide the floor grid"]]],["h","Edit mesh"],["p","These work in the piece modes of [Edit mesh](#edit-mesh). [[1]] to [[4]] are the keys that used to switch the view; the view modes are now on [[Alt]] and the same numbers."],["keys",[["1","Points mode"],["2","Edges mode"],["3","Polygons mode"],["4","Model mode: a click picks a whole part, as before"],["Ctrl + A","Pick everything of the selected parts"],["Del","Take out the picked pieces"],["Ctrl + Backspace","Dissolve the picked pieces"],["Shift + Click","With any pick tool: add to what is picked"],["Ctrl + Click","With any pick tool: take away from what is picked"],["Double-click","With the Click tool: pick a loop"]]],["h","Move, rotate, scale"],["keys",[["E","Move"],["R","Rotate"],["T","Scale"],["D","Dynamic place: drag the selected part over other surfaces. Press it again to leave."],["Q","Hide the gizmo"],["Shift","Hold while dragging the gizmo to snap"],["Shift + Scroll","While snapping and dragging: a coarser or a finer step"]]],["h","Panels and windows"],["keys",[["Ctrl + K","Search commands, parts, the library and settings"],["Shift + C","The same search"],["Shift + Enter","In the search, on a switch: go to the switch instead of turning it"],["W","Quick wand: hold it over the viewport, flick toward a command, let go. A quick tap leaves the ring open."],["Shift + L","Library drawer"],["Shift + M","Materials drawer"],["`","Console"],["Ctrl + ,","Settings"],["F1","Help window"],["?","Keyboard shortcuts"],["Ctrl + F","In Settings: jump to the search field"],["Ctrl + Enter","In Batch rename: apply"],["Esc","Close the open window or menu. It closes the Export, Import and Save scene windows too."]]],["h","In the help window"],["keys",[["/","Jump to the search field"],["Enter","In the search field: open the first result"],["Esc","Clear the search. Press it again to close the window."]]],["h","Mouse, in the viewport"],["keys",[["Click","Select a part"],["Shift + Click","Add to the selection"],["Ctrl + Click","Add to or remove from the selection"],["Ctrl + Drag","Box select"],["Ctrl + Shift + Drag","Box select, adding to the selection"],["Drag","Orbit"],["Middle drag","Pan"],["Right drag","Pan"],["Scroll","Zoom, toward the cursor"],["Alt + Right drag","Zoom smoothly"],["Right-click","Menu for the part or the view"]]],["h","Mouse, elsewhere"],["keys",[["Double-click","On a name in the tree or on a tab: rename"],["Click","In the tree: select a row"],["Shift + Click","In the tree: select a range of rows. In Heavy parts: tick a run of rows."],["Ctrl + Click","In the tree: add a row to the selection or take it off"],["Drag","In the tree: move rows into a group or into another place"],["Right-click","On a tab: duplicate, close all, close others, open"],["Middle click","On a tab: close it"],["Esc","While dragging a tab: put it back"],["Shift","While dragging a number: ten times faster"],["Alt","While dragging a number: ten times finer"],["Click","On a number: type a value"],["Right-click","On a number field: Cut, Copy, Paste, Select all, Reset and quick edits of the number"],["Double-click","On the material card in Properties: rename the material"],["Drag","A material from the Materials dock into the view: assign it"],["Shift + Drag","A material onto another material: merge the two"]]],["h","On the start screen"],["keys",[["← → ↑ ↓","Walk the cards of the recent files. Up and down move by a row."],["Enter","In the search field of the recent files: open the first match"]]],["h","Change a key"],["p","Every key can be changed except [[Esc]], the Quick wand’s [[W]] and the mouse gestures. Open the list with [[?]]."],["steps",["Click the shortcut you want to change, and press the new keys.","If another command already has that combination, the app says which one and offers {{Replace}}. A few combinations are kept by the browser for itself (such as [[Ctrl + W]] and [[F5]]) and are refused.","Use {{All commands}} to list the commands that have no key yet, so you can give them one."]],["p","The picture of the keyboard shows the keys that do something, lit and named after their command. Hold [[Ctrl]], [[Shift]] or [[Alt]], or click them in the picture, to see the keys of that combination, with the mouse gestures for the same keys under it. Press any combination to see what it does, or click a key to list everything on it."],["h","Profiles"],["p","Your changes are kept in named profiles, in this browser. You can make a new one, duplicate, rename or delete one, and put every key back. {{Default}} is the app’s own keys and cannot be changed, so your first change starts a profile of your own."],["p","The command search, the tooltips and the right-click menu show your keys. The keys written in the app menu and in the toolbar’s lists are still the default ones, and a command left without a key shows none."],["see",["search-and-commands","move-the-view","select-parts","help-window","edit-mesh"]]]},{"slug":"command-line","group":"reference","title":"The command-line converter","summary":"Convert STEP files to GLB without opening the viewer. How to run step2glb.py, and what every option does.","blocks":[["p","The converter is a Python script, `step2glb.py`. The app runs it for you when you drop a STEP file on the window. You can also run it yourself in a terminal, for example to convert many files, or to use options the import window does not show."],["p","You never need this page to use the app. It is here for when you want more control."],["h","Before you start"],["steps",["Start the app once with its launcher, so the Python environment exists. See [Start it the first time](#first-start).","Open a terminal in the app folder. On Windows: open the folder in File Explorer, click the address bar, type `cmd` and press [[Enter]]. On macOS: open Terminal, type `cd` and a space, drag the app folder onto the window, and press [[Enter]].","Switch on the app’s Python environment. This makes the command `python` use the add-ons the launcher installed. On Windows, type the first command below. On macOS, type the second."]],["code",".venv\\Scripts\\activate"],["code","source .venv/bin/activate"],["h","Convert a file"],["code","python step2glb.py input.step"],["p","Replace `input.step` with the path of your file. The result is written next to it, with the same name and the ending `.glb`. The terminal prints what the converter is doing."],["h","Examples"],["terms",[["`python step2glb.py input.step --quality 0.2 --min-size 0.5`","A finer tessellation, and parts smaller than 0.5% of the model left out."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --relative`","Quality as a fraction of the model’s size, so it does not depend on the file’s units."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --meshopt`","Compress the result with gltfpack."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --simplify 0.5`","Also halve the triangle count. Lossy."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --no-colors --no-instance`","The plain reader: no colours, no instancing."],["`python step2glb.py input.step --out small.glb --target-tris 200000`","Adjust the quality automatically until the result has at most 200,000 triangles."],["`python step2glb.py a.step b.step c.step`","Convert several files, one after the other."]]],["h","Input and output"],["terms",[["`input`","One or more STEP files."],["`--out`, `-o`","The file to write. Only with a single input file. Without it, the GLB is written next to the input."],["`--force`","Convert again even if an output that is newer than the input already exists. Without it, the converter skips a file that was already converted with the same settings."]]],["h","Mesh quality"],["terms",[["`--quality`, `-q`","How closely the triangles follow the true surface (the linear deflection). Smaller is finer and heavier. The default is 0.5, in the units of the file, normally millimetres."],["`--relative`","Reads `--quality` as a fraction of the model’s bounding-box diagonal instead of an absolute distance."],["`--angular`","The angular deflection, in radians: a second limit on how coarsely curved surfaces are cut into triangles. The default is 0.5, about 28.6°."],["`--tiers`","Tessellate by part size. Give the two limits between small, medium and large parts, in percent of the size of the whole model, then a quality in millimetres and an angle in degrees for small, for medium and for large parts. For example `--tiers 5,30:2,60:0.2,30:0.05,10`."],["`--min-edge`","The smallest triangle edge the mesher may make, in the units of the file. It stops tiny fillets and threads from filling up with slivers. The default, 0, is no limit."],["`--no-normals`","Do not write the normals of the CAD surfaces into the GLB. The viewer then works out the shading from the triangles. Without this option the normals are written, which is what makes fillets blend smoothly into the faces beside them."],["`--swap-yz`","Turn the model a quarter turn about the X axis, for a Z-up CAD file that should stand in a Y-up scene."],["`--min-size`","Leave out parts whose size is below this percentage of the model’s diagonal. The default, 0, keeps everything."]]],["h","Budgets"],["terms",[["`--target-tris`","Adjust `--quality` automatically to land at or under this triangle count. The conversion is run again up to five times."],["`--target-size-mb`","The same, for the size of the output file in megabytes. With both set, the tighter one wins."]]],["h","Repeated parts"],["terms",[["`--no-instance`","Do not collapse identical shapes into instances. According to the README, it only has an effect together with `--no-colors`."],["`--pca-instances`","An experimental, more aggressive way to find repeated shapes. It can turn symmetric parts the wrong way."]]],["h","Colours, names and the tree"],["terms",[["`--no-colors`","Use the plain reader: geometry only, no colours, names or hierarchy. The fastest."],["`--force-colors`","Always read colours, names and the hierarchy. Without this option, the converter skips them for files over 1 GB."],["`--no-shuo`","Skip per-instance colour overrides. A big saving in time on assemblies with many repeated parts."],["`--no-layers`","Skip CAD layer information."],["`--no-materials`","Skip material information."],["`--no-step-names`","Skip product names. Parts get generic names in the tree."],["`--no-step-props`","Skip validation properties such as mass and area."],["`--props`","Work out the volume and surface area of every solid. Slow on big files."]]],["h","Compression and reduction"],["p","These options use gltfpack, a separate free tool that has to be installed and on your PATH. Without it they are skipped, with a warning."],["terms",[["`--meshopt`","Compress the GLB with Meshopt, for files roughly ten times smaller. This is on by default whenever gltfpack is found."],["`--no-meshopt`","Switch that default off."],["`--quantize`","Store positions, normals and texture coordinates as smaller numbers."],["`--simplify`","Keep this fraction of the triangles, between 0 and 1. 0.5 halves the count. Sharp edges are preserved. Lossy. It switches Meshopt on."]]],["h","Speed"],["terms",[["`--parallel`","Use this many worker processes for building the meshes. The default, 0, does one at a time."]]],["h","Without typing"],["p","The folder also holds `step2glb.bat` (Windows) and `step2glb.command` (macOS). Drag a STEP file onto one of them to convert it with the default settings, without opening the viewer."],["h","Options of the server"],["p","The launcher starts `serve.py`. Started by hand, it takes these options:"],["terms",[["`--tab`","Open the app in an ordinary browser tab instead of a window of its own."],["`--no-browser`","Start the server and open nothing. Type the printed address into a browser yourself."],["`--open`, `-o`","Open this file at start. Accepts STEP, GLB and glTF files. For a STEP file it asks about quality first."],["`--port`","Use this port instead of 4242."]]],["see",["first-file","files-in-folder","troubleshooting"]]]},{"slug":"file-formats","group":"reference","title":"File formats","summary":"Which files the app opens and which it writes, and what each format keeps: the parts tree, materials and vertex colours.","blocks":[["p","This page follows the format sheet inside the app. Open it there with {{Compare}} in the Export window."],["h","Formats it opens"],["terms",[["STEP (.step, .stp)","The CAD exchange format. Converted by the local Python converter. Keeps the parts tree and materials. Open only: the app does not write STEP."],["IGES (.iges, .igs)","An older CAD exchange format, often a surface model. Converted by the local Python converter. Keeps the names, colours and layers the file has, but not an assembly tree. Open only."],["BREP (.brep, .brp)","The OpenCascade shape format. Converted by the local Python converter. Geometry only. Open only."],["GLB","Binary glTF. Keeps the parts tree, full materials and vertex colours. The best all-round format."],["glTF","The text form of GLB. The same capabilities, larger."],["FBX","A common exchange format for Maya, Blender, Unreal and 3ds Max. Keeps the parts tree and materials."],["OBJ","Universal, but with no transforms and no tree. Materials come from a separate `.mtl` file."],["3MF","A 3D-printing format. Keeps the parts tree and materials. Open only."],["STL","Geometry only, as a single mesh. Used for 3D printing."]]],["p","STEP, IGES and BREP files go through the converter. The others load directly in the browser."],["h","Formats it writes"],["terms",[["GLB","Parts tree, full materials, vertex colours. Optional Draco compression."],["glTF","The same as GLB, as text."],["FBX","Parts tree and materials. Binary FBX 7.4 by default, with a text (ASCII) version as a fallback."],["USDZ","Parts tree and materials. For Apple Quick Look and Reality Composer. Write only."],["OBJ","A flat list of parts, with the hierarchy folded into their names. Materials in a `.mtl` file."],["STL","One mesh, geometry only."],["PLY","One mesh, with a colour for every vertex. Write only."],["CSV","A parts list. No geometry. Write only."]]],["h","The three columns of the sheet"],["terms",[["Hierarchy","Whether the format keeps the parts tree: groups, and groups inside groups."],["Materials","Colour and shading for each part. “PBR” means the full set with metalness and roughness. A plain dot means colour only."],["Vertex colours","A colour stored on every corner point of the mesh, with no separate material."]]],["see",["export","choose-format","first-file"]]]},{"slug":"files-in-folder","group":"reference","title":"Files in the folder","summary":"What the files and folders inside the MeshOptimiser folder are for, which ones you use, and which ones to leave alone.","blocks":[["p","You only ever need to double-click one file: the launcher for your system. The rest is the app itself."],["h","The ones you use"],["terms",[["`start.bat`","The launcher for Windows. Double-click it to start the app."],["`start.command`","The launcher for macOS."],["`start_hidden.vbs`","Windows: starts the app like `start.bat`, without showing the launcher window."],["`step2glb.bat`, `step2glb.command`","Convert a STEP file that you drag onto them, without opening the viewer."],["`test-converter.bat`, `test-converter.command`","Run the converter on a STEP file that you drag onto them and show everything it prints. Useful when a conversion fails and you want to see why."],["`README.txt`","A short quick start, in plain text."]]],["h","Folders the app creates"],["terms",[["`.venv`","The app’s own Python environment, created on the first run. It can take up to about 2 GB. It is safe to delete: the launcher builds it again, which takes a few minutes. On macOS the folder is hidden in Finder, because its name starts with a dot."],["`inbox`","Where STEP files are converted. The finished GLB files stay here."]]],["note","The `inbox` folder is not emptied automatically, so it grows with every conversion. With the app closed, you can delete the files in it that you no longer need."],["h","The app itself"],["terms",[["`serve.py`","The small local server. It hands the app’s pages to your browser and starts the converter when you drop a STEP file."],["`step2glb.py`","The converter from STEP to GLB."],["`index.html`, `app-v2.js`","The viewer: the page and its program."],["`holefill.js`","The hole filler behind Remove holes."],["`mesh-worker.js`","A background worker for Remove holes and Decimate, so the window stays usable while they run."],["`cloner.js`","A cloner that makes linear, grid and radial arrays of a part."],["`vendor`","The libraries the viewer needs, bundled so that it starts without the internet."],["`assets`","The app’s icons, pictures and help clips."],["`manifest.webmanifest`","What lets Chrome and Edge install the page as an app."],["`requirements.txt`","The list of Python add-ons the launcher installs: cadquery-ocp, trimesh and numpy."],["`tests`","The self-tests. See [Self-test](#self-test)."],["`docs`, `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`","Screenshots, the project’s description, and the list of changes in every version."]]],["note","Do not open `index.html` by double-clicking it. The page then says it needs a local server. Always start with the launcher."],["see",["first-start","command-line","stop-restart-update"]]]},{"slug":"privacy-offline","group":"reference","title":"Privacy and offline use","summary":"MeshOptimiser runs on your own computer. What stays local, when the internet is used, and what the browser remembers.","blocks":[["h","Your files stay on your computer"],["p","MeshOptimiser is not an online service. The viewer runs in your browser, and the converter runs in Python on your machine. The local server answers only on your own computer, at an address that starts with `localhost`."],["p","When you drop a STEP file, the page hands it to that local server, which converts it in the app’s `inbox` folder. The file does not go anywhere else. There is no account and no sign-in."],["h","When the internet is used"],["terms",[["The first start","The launcher downloads the Python add-ons the converter needs (cadquery-ocp, trimesh, numpy). If Python itself is missing and you agree, it downloads Python too."],["Every later start","Nothing. The libraries the viewer needs are bundled in the `vendor` folder, so the app opens, edits and exports with no connection at all."],["A few rarely used converters","Fetched from the internet the first time they are needed: the transcoder for compressed textures, and the converters behind Draco export and some older FBX files."]]],["h","What the browser remembers"],["p","The app keeps a few things in your browser’s storage, on this computer:"],["list",["your settings;","the list of recent files, with a small picture of each;","permission to reopen a recent file without asking where it is;","the sizes of the sidebars and docks."]],["p","You can clear each of these under {{Settings}}, {{Storage}}. Because they live in the browser’s profile, they survive an update of the app, and they are separate for each browser you use."],["h","What is written to disk"],["list",["Converted GLB files, in the `inbox` folder.","Scenes you save and files you export, where you choose.","The Python environment, in `.venv`."]],["see",["requirements","files-in-folder","settings"]]]},{"slug":"troubleshooting","group":"help","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Fixes for the usual problems: Python not found, a blocked launcher on macOS, missing modules, a blank view and slow conversions.","blocks":[["p","Find the heading that matches what you see. If nothing here helps, use [Report a problem](https://lukagrcar.com/apps/meshoptimiser/report.html). It asks for what I need and packs it into one file. Say which version you have, and add the log: {{Copy log}} on the progress card, or the copy button in the console. In {{Settings}}, {{About}}, the button {{Copy details}} copies the version, your browser and your graphics card in one go."],["h","“python is not on PATH” on Windows"],["p","Windows cannot find Python. Run the Python installer again and tick “Add Python to PATH”. Or close and reopen the terminal or the launcher, so the new PATH is picked up. See [What you need](#requirements)."],["h","“Operation not permitted” on macOS"],["p","Right-click `start.command` and choose “Open”. macOS blocks a double-click on a freshly unzipped script the first time."],["h","“ModuleNotFoundError: No module named ‘OCP’”"],["p","The Python environment is incomplete. The same goes for a missing `numpy` or `trimesh`. Quit the app, delete the folder `.venv` inside the app folder, and start the launcher again. It rebuilds the environment from scratch."],["h","The first run stops while installing"],["list",["Check your internet connection. The first run has to download the add-ons.","Check your Python version. The converter needs 3.10, 3.11 or 3.12. With 3.13 the add-ons cannot be installed. Install a supported version, delete the folder `.venv` if one was created, and start again.","Check that the drive has about 2 GB free."]],["h","“This page needs a local server”"],["p","You opened `index.html` directly. Browsers do not let the app run that way. Start it with `start.bat` or `start.command`, and use the address the launcher prints, normally `http://localhost:4242`."],["h","The app window is empty, or the browser cannot reach the page"],["p","The local server is not running. This happens when you open the installed app or a bookmark without starting the launcher first, or after you closed the launcher window. Start the launcher again."],["h","The address is not localhost:4242"],["p","Something else on your computer was already using port 4242, so the server chose another one. That is fine. The launcher window prints the address it is using."],["h","The app starts twice"],["p","If the app is already running, starting the launcher again does not start a second copy. It opens a window on the one that is running."],["h","The app opened in a normal browser tab"],["p","The launcher found no Chrome, Edge or Brave, so it used your default browser. The app works the same in a tab."],["h","The window looks squeezed, or has scroll bars"],["p","The window cannot be smaller than 960 by 560 pixels. In a smaller window the app scrolls instead of squeezing its panels. Make the window bigger, or drag the edge of a sidebar to make the sidebar narrower. The left one cannot go under 220 pixels and the right one not under 240. See [The window](#the-window)."],["h","The 3D view is empty or looks wrong"],["list",["Press [[F]] to frame the model, and [[Alt + H]] to show hidden parts.","The viewer uses WebGPU when the browser has it and falls back to WebGL2 when it does not. To switch by hand, open {{Settings}}, {{Performance}} and set {{Renderer}} to WebGL2. The page reloads.","If a message says the GPU was lost, reload the page.","Update your browser and your graphics driver."]],["h","A STEP conversion takes very long"],["p","Large assemblies take time, and the reading of names, colours and the tree is the slowest part. The progress card keeps showing the converter’s latest message while it works. To make it faster, change the import settings:"],["list",["Under {{What to read}}, leave {{Instance overrides}}, {{Layers}} and {{Validation properties}} off.","Choose a coarser {{Surface quality}}.","Set {{Drop small parts}} above 0 to skip tiny fasteners.","For a huge file where you only need the shape, choose {{Geometry only}} under {{Colours and structure}}."]],["p","{{Cancel}} on the progress card stops a conversion that is running."],["h","“Conversion failed”"],["p","A STEP file with nothing solid in it gets a plain message that says so. For any other failure, press {{Copy log}} on the progress card and read the last lines. To see everything the converter prints, drag the STEP file onto `test-converter.bat` or `test-converter.command`. Include the log when you report the problem."],["p","When the conversion fails because a Python package is not installed, the log and the notice name the package and the command that installs it: `python -m pip install -r requirements.txt`."],["h","The model has no colours or no parts tree"],["p","A STEP file may have been converted with geometry only. Open it again and choose {{Colours & hierarchy}} under {{Colours and structure}}. A GLB, FBX, OBJ or STL file can only show what the file itself contains."],["h","The view stutters on a heavy model"],["list",["Open {{Settings}}, {{Performance}}. Keep {{Skip tiny parts}} and {{Lower the resolution if it stutters}} on, and try {{Quality}} on Low.","Make the model lighter. See [A sensible order to work in](#workflow)."]],["h","Small parts disappear while I turn the view"],["p","That is {{Skip tiny parts}} at work. They come back the moment the view stops. Switch it off under {{Settings}}, {{Performance}} if you prefer."],["h","A recent file asks me to pick it again"],["p","The browser needs a fresh permission before the app may read that file. Open it once more with {{Open file…}}, or drop it on the window."],["h","The exported model is the wrong size or lies on its side"],["p","Export again with a different {{Unit scale}} or the other {{Up axis}}. A model that came from Cinema 4D or a glTF already stands Y up, so choose {{Keep as in the scene}} for it. See [Export](#export)."],["h","Cinema 4D or Maya cannot open an FBX I exported long ago"],["p","FBX files written by versions before 0.14.0 are refused by the FBX software in Cinema 4D and Maya. Export the file again with this version, or open it in Blender and save it again."],["h","An export fails on a very large model"],["p","Text formats such as OBJ can run out of memory. Use GLB or STL, which are binary."],["h","I closed the window and the app is still running"],["p","Closing the window does not stop the server. Close the launcher window too, or next time use {{Menu}}, then {{Quit app}}."],["see",["requirements","first-start","faq","self-test","crash-recovery"]]]},{"slug":"faq","group":"help","title":"Frequently asked questions","summary":"Short answers: cost and licence, privacy, browsers, file sizes, whether Python is needed, and where to report a bug.","blocks":[["h","Is it free?"],["p","Yes. MeshOptimiser is free and open source."],["h","What is the licence?"],["p","MIT. In short, you may use, copy and change it, also for commercial work, as long as the licence text stays with it. It comes with no warranty."],["h","Can I send the exported file to a customer?"],["p","Yes, once you have taken out what they should not see. See [How to make a CAD model safe to send to a customer](#make-model-safe-to-send)."],["h","Does my file leave my computer?"],["p","No. The viewer and the converter both run on your machine. See [Privacy and offline use](#privacy-offline)."],["h","Do I need an internet connection?"],["p","Only for the first start, which downloads the Python add-ons. After that the app runs offline, and so does the help window ([[F1]]). A few rarely used converters are fetched the first time they are needed."],["h","Which browsers work?"],["p","A recent Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari. The viewer uses WebGPU and falls back to WebGL2. For the app to open in a window of its own, the launcher needs Chrome, Edge or Brave. Installing it as an app is offered by Chrome and Edge."],["h","Which systems does it run on?"],["p","Windows and macOS."],["h","Can I use it without Python?"],["p","No. The app is served by a small Python program on your computer, and STEP files are converted in Python. On Windows, the launcher offers to install Python for you if it is missing."],["h","Which Python version?"],["p","3.10, 3.11 or 3.12. Version 3.13 is not supported yet."],["h","How big a file can it handle?"],["p","There is no fixed limit in these pages, because it depends on your computer’s memory and graphics card. The app is tested on assemblies of thousands of parts and millions of triangles. The README’s example is a 320 MB STEP file. The local server accepts STEP files up to 4 GB."],["h","Does it change my original file?"],["p","No. Nothing reaches a file until you save a scene or export, and both write a new file."],["h","Can it open SolidWorks, Inventor, Creo or CATIA files?"],["p","Not the files those programs save for themselves. Export a STEP file from your CAD program first. See [Native CAD files: export a STEP file first](#cad-files-step-first)."],["h","Can it write STEP files?"],["p","No. It reads STEP and writes mesh formats: GLB, glTF, FBX, USDZ, OBJ, STL and PLY, and a parts list as CSV."],["h","Where is the converted file?"],["p","In the folder `inbox` inside the app folder. To put a file where you want it, save a scene or export."],["h","Can I undo everything?"],["p","Every editing action can be undone with [[Ctrl + Z]]. By default the history goes back 200 steps, and {{Settings}}, {{General}} can make it 50 or 500. {{Revert to source…}} in the {{Menu}} reloads the file as it was. See [Undo and redo](#undo-redo)."],["h","Why do I have to start it with a launcher?"],["p","Because the converter is a Python program, and a web page cannot start one. The launcher starts a small local server that connects the two."],["h","How do I update?"],["p","Download the new zip and unpack it into a new folder. See [Stop, restart and update](#stop-restart-update)."],["h","Where do I find help inside the app?"],["p","Press [[F1]]. The help window holds these same articles, with a search. See [The help window](#help-window)."],["h","Where do I report a bug or ask for a feature?"],["p","On the [Report a problem](https://lukagrcar.com/apps/meshoptimiser/report.html) page. The app’s mark in the top bar has a link called {{Report a problem}} that opens it with your version and system already filled in. Say what you did, what you expected and what happened, and add the version number and the log. {{Settings}}, {{About}}, {{Copy details}} copies the version number, your browser and your graphics card for you."],["see",["troubleshooting","privacy-offline","what-it-is"]]]},{"slug":"self-test","group":"help","title":"Self-test","summary":"For the curious: the app can test itself. One address runs a suite of checks inside the live app and reports the results.","blocks":[["p","You do not need this to use the app. It is useful when you suspect that something is broken on your computer, or before you report a bug."],["h","Run it"],["steps",["Start the app.","Open the address below in the browser. If your launcher printed a different port, use that number.","The app starts driving itself: it works the parts tree, the toolbar, the windows and the right-click menu the way a user would.","Results appear in a panel at the bottom right, and in the console."]],["code","http://localhost:4242/?selftest"],["h","What it checks"],["p","Undo and redo, exports, saving a scene and opening it again, and more. Nothing is downloaded to your computer: the exports are captured in memory."],["h","Run part of it"],["p","Add a word after `selftest=` to run only the tests whose name contains it. This address runs the tests about groups:"],["code","http://localhost:4242/?selftest=groups"],["h","The hole filler has its own test"],["p","It needs Node.js, a separate program for running JavaScript, and nothing else. In a terminal in the app folder:"],["code","node tests/holefill.test.mjs"],["p","It builds plates, pockets, counterbores, engraved letters, a boss, a washer and a hollow box, runs the hole filler on them, and checks that the result is closed, has the right volume, and is untouched where it should be."],["see",["troubleshooting","fill-holes","command-line"]]]},{"slug":"work-with-claude","group":"claude","title":"Work with Claude","summary":"Let Claude look at the open scene, find what is heavy and do the clean-up in the app, asking you first.","blocks":[["p","Claude can work with the scene that is open in MeshOptimiser. You describe the job in plain words. Claude looks at the scene, selects, hides and explodes parts, and, with your permission, reduces triangles, repairs meshes, renames, moves and exports. It does this through the app, in your window, so you watch it happen."],["p","It uses the same tools you would click, so it saves clicking, not judgement: you still decide what loss is acceptable, and you should look at the result. If you already know which buttons to press, pressing them yourself is just as fast. It is most useful for repeated clean-up, a size to reach, or a folder of files. A general 3D program such as Blender can be driven by Claude in a similar way; MeshOptimiser opens STEP and IGES CAD files directly and has its own finders for bolts and for parts nobody can see."],["h","Connect it"],["steps",["Start MeshOptimiser and open a model.","Open Settings with [[Ctrl + ,]] and choose {{Claude}}.","Turn on {{Let Claude connect}}. A {{Claude connected}} pill shows in the top bar while it is on.","Once, in a terminal on the same computer, add the app to Claude:"]],["code","claude mcp add --transport http meshoptimiser http://localhost:4242/mcp"],["p","If the launcher printed a different port, use that number. Another program that supports MCP over HTTP takes the same address. The {{Claude}} section of Settings has a button that copies the command, and its top line says whether the app is off, listening, working or waiting for you. The same word sits beside {{Claude}} in the list on the left."],["h","What to ask"],["list",["“Find all the bolts and hide them.”","“Get this under 300,000 triangles and tell me what you took out.”","“Show it exploded 50% and take a screenshot.”","“Which ten parts carry the most triangles?”","“Rename every part that starts with body to Housing.”","“Stand the model on the floor and make a turntable video.”"]],["h","What stays in your hands"],["list",["Anything that changes the model shows an {{Allow}} or {{Don’t allow}} prompt first. Looking, selecting, hiding, isolating, exploding and moving the camera do not.","Deleting always asks, even when you switch asking off for the rest.","Every change is a step in the undo history. [[Ctrl + Z]] takes it back. See [Undo and redo](#undo-redo).","Nothing happens until you switch {{Let Claude connect}} on, and nothing can reach the app after you switch it off."]],["h","The switches"],["terms",[["Let Claude connect","The app listens for Claude while this is on. Off, it asks for nothing and nobody can reach it."],["Connect when the app opens","If it was on last time, it switches itself on again when you open the app."],["Change the model","Reduce triangles, fill holes, remove hidden faces, repair, clean up, merge, split and duplicate."],["Move, rename and group","Move, turn and scale parts, recentre the model, stand it on the floor, rename parts, make and dissolve groups."],["Delete parts","Delete chosen parts, or every part under a size."],["Open files and save exports","Open a file from this computer, save exports and turntable videos into the app’s exports folder."],["Ask before Claude edits","The {{Allow}} or {{Don’t allow}} prompt before every change to the model."],["Always ask before deleting","Deleting still asks when the switch above is off."],["Show the Claude pill in the top bar","A small pill that says when Claude is listening, working or waiting for you."],["Show a note when Claude acts","A short message names what Claude is doing."]]],["p","A switch that is off refuses the request, and Claude is told why."],["h","The badge in the top bar"],["p","While Claude is connected, a {{Claude connected}} badge sits at the right of the top bar. Click it for a dropdown: whether the app is listening, working or waiting for you, the number of requests, how many were done, declined or failed, the last three requests with how long each took, and three switches: {{Let Claude connect}}, {{Ask before Claude edits}} and {{Always ask before deleting}}. {{Copy command}} copies the connect command, {{Console}} opens the console, and {{More…}} opens Settings, Claude. {{Esc}} or a click elsewhere closes it."],["h","What Claude asked for"],["p","At the bottom of the Claude section, {{This session}} counts the requests and names the ones asked for most, with how long each takes, and {{Recent requests}} lists what Claude asked for and what happened: done, you said no, turned off, or failed. {{Copy report}} puts the numbers and the recent requests on the clipboard, for a bug report. Every request and every change of the connection is also written to the console (the button in the status bar), tagged claude, and to the window that runs the app."],["h","Knowing where it stands"],["p","Before it changes anything, Claude can check the model in one call ({{check_model}}: where the weight is, what is tiny, thin, hidden or drawn twice, the unit, and what to do first) and say what the file would weigh against the app’s own guidelines ({{estimate_export_size}}: about 5 MB and 100,000 triangles for a web page, 15 MB for Shopify, 10 MB and 100,000 triangles for Apple AR Quick Look, 25 MB and 200,000 triangles for a phone app). Afterwards {{optimisation_report}} gives the file as it was opened against now, with the change in percent. If you have saved Smart optimise recipes, Claude can list them ({{list_recipes}}) and run one ({{run_recipe}}) instead of choosing settings itself, and {{save_scene}} keeps a scene file before a drastic step."],["p","It can also select by shape, colour or material ({{select_similar}}, {{select_by_colour}}, {{select_by_material}}), list the materials, and add standard parts from the library ({{add_library_part}})."],["h","Longer jobs"],["p","Some jobs are big, so Claude has a few ways to make them easier to trust. A dry run ({{dry_run}} on a step that removes or reduces something) does the step, reads the numbers and takes it back with undo: Claude can tell you “this would save this many triangles” without anything staying changed, and nothing is asked because nothing is kept."],["p","{{optimise_to_target}} takes the whole scene to a size or a place (web, Shopify, AR or a phone, or a number of megabytes or triangles) in one call: it tidies up, runs Smart optimise, then reduces the triangles in rounds against the app’s own size estimate. It says whether it got there, why it stopped if not, and how many undo steps it took."],["p","{{compare_images}} puts the model as it was opened next to the model now, with the same camera and the triangles, parts and size under each, in one picture saved in the exports folder. It steps back through the undo history to draw the original and forward again, so the scene ends exactly as it was."],["p","{{list_folder}} lists the model files in a folder and {{batch_optimise}} does them one after another: each file opens in a scene tab of its own, is optimised (a saved recipe, a goal or Smart optimise), is exported as name_optimised and its tab is closed. The scene you have open is not touched. Try one file by hand first."],["p","While a long job runs, the badge in the top bar says which step it is on and the dropdown shows a progress bar with a Stop button. Cancelling in Claude stops it too. The job ends after the step it is on and says what was done."],["h","Pictures for a client"],["p","Claude can make clean pictures of the model with the tool {{render_image}}: big (up to 8192 pixels a side), drawn at twice the size and scaled down so the edges are smooth, in one angle or several (perspective, front, side, top), in solid, clay, CAD, wireframe, x-ray or the heat view, on a white, light, grey, dark or black background or on none at all (a transparent PNG). The model is framed to fill the picture and the grid is left out. Each picture is saved as a PNG in the {{exports}} folder next to the app, and Claude looks at a small preview of it. For a before and after, ask for the same pictures before and after you optimise: the camera, the look and the size are the same."],["p","The quick tool {{screenshot}} is a look at the viewport exactly as it is. Claude can also frame the picture on the whole model or on the selected parts: it is then fitted edge to edge on the geometry itself, the move handles are left out, and your own camera is put back afterwards. {{turntable_video}} saves one slow turn round the model."],["h","What Claude already knows"],["p","Claude does not start from nothing. The app gives it the know-how of an experienced user, so it works in the order a person would and asks the questions a person would ask."],["list",["Six playbooks: how to optimise (the rules and the order of work), recipes by goal (web viewer, AR, a game engine, 3D printing, sending to a customer, a review), judgement (what makes a scene heavy, what each step costs in looks, when to ask), the tools by job, what is safe, and what to do when something fails.","About fifty of the app’s own help articles, the same ones you can read here. Claude searches them by words or by a symptom, and reads only the one it needs.","Ready-made jobs it can start from: optimise for the web, lighten it in one go, take the bolts out, make it safe to send, review the scene.","A hint on every tool that says whether it only reads or changes the model, which Claude’s own program uses to decide what to ask."]],["h","Does it save tokens?"],["p","It should, but there is no fixed percentage. Claude works in fewer, better-chosen steps instead of finding the order by trial. One Smart optimise call stands in for a dozen separate ones. Replies are compact: about a fifth fewer characters than an indented reply, measured on a list of 40 parts. The main playbook is about 2,800 tokens, read once; the help articles cost nothing until Claude searches for one, and a search returns short snippets, not whole articles."],["h","What leaves the computer"],["p","The app talks to Claude only through a small server on your own computer. Your model file is not sent anywhere. Claude gets back what its requests return: part names, counts, sizes, and the pictures it asks for, such as a screenshot of the viewport. Those pass through Claude the way anything you type to it does. Exports and turntable videos are saved in the app’s {{exports}} folder."],["h","If it does not connect"],["list",["Claude says the app is not listening: the app is not open in a browser, or {{Let Claude connect}} is off. Open the app and switch it on.","The top bar pill is missing: switch {{Let Claude connect}} on in Settings, or turn on {{Show the Claude pill in the top bar}}.","The pill says another window took over: the app was opened in a second window and switched it on there. Switch it on again in the window you want.","A request says it was turned off in Settings: switch on the matching one of the four switches.","A long job such as Fit to budget on a large model can take minutes. Keep the app open and in front.","The Claude section is missing from Settings: update the app. The connection is part of version 0.22.0 and later.","Stuck or odd after an update: in Settings, Claude, {{Reconnect}} starts the connection from scratch (the small refresh button in the badge’s dropdown does the same). {{Restart the server…}} stops and starts the app’s server; open scenes stay and the connection comes back by itself."]],["note","The online version has no connection to Claude. Run the app on your own computer."],["see",["undo-redo","settings","scene-tabs","fasteners"]]]}]};
